        <p>CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>A lady who is an enthusiastic admirer of Mr. Gladstone gives an account of Mr. Gladstone's ar- rival at Folkestone on Saturday. She writes: "We all went to see him arrive. Large crowds were there, standing even on the tops of trunks, and on each side of the gangway. We managed to get a place before the arrival of the boat. A great many policemen were there to keep order. Tha passage was very riiugh,and the unfortunate passengers must have felt rather uncomfortable to see so many people looking at them. Mr. Gladstone's appear- ance was th e signal for a terrible outburst of cheer- ing. Mrs. Gladstone came on shore, leaning en a gentleman's arm. Both she and the Prime Minister looked older than when I saw them last at Brigh- ton a few years ago. Mr. Gladstone walked bare- headed from the boat to :he station. Indeed he could not have done otherwise as the cheering was continual, an-I not a single hiss was heard, I am thankful to say. English people do give a hearty welcome to those whom they love. One man near me positively shed tears, so much was he affected. For myself, I longed more than I could tell to shake hands with the Old Man. Ile looked bright and happy. We heard the cheering even after the train had left the station. One of Mr. Gladstone's grand- children—a very pretty child—was with him."</p>
        <p>One of the most notable Englishmen of the day passed away on Monday morning at Claydon. Sir Harry Verney, the venerable baronet, who died at his ancestral hpme, had reached a very advanced age, but, even under the pressure of a grave and painful domestic misfortune, he retained is cheer- fulness, his energy, and his unaffected concern for the welfare of all with whore he was connected until the very last. A better type of the English country gentleman never existed. The squirearchy would be a powerful and a deservedly popular class for generations to come if they possessed many Sir Harry Verneys in their ranks.</p>
        <p>Sir Harry Verney was born towards the end of the first year of this century. His father was General Sir Harry Calvert, who died in 1826, and the new baronet next year discarded the name of Calvert for that of the ancient family of Verney, the change being duly effected by means of Royal license. Seventy-five years ago tl,e gentleman who died only on Monday joined the Army from the Royal Military College at Sandhurit. His regiment was the Seventh Royal Fusiliers, from which he re- tired with the rank of major in 1827. Afterwards he served on the British Legation to Baden and Wurtemburg, and was elected to Parliament for Buckingham in 1832 the year of the great Reform Bill. As everyone knows,he was a steady and con- sisterlt Liberal during hispolitical career.</p>
        <p>M. Jules Simon, the well-known French politician has performed an act of self-abnegation, which he did not imitate from an illustrious example in this country. Some old lady, of whose existence M. Simon was profoundly ignorant, admired his poli- tical conduct so much that she left him a legacy of four thousand pounds. There are people who would pocket the entire sum. When one of Qaeen `tic- toria's subjects remembered her in a will, Her Majesty availed of her good luck with remarkable aptitude. But the French statesman did not act tnus. lie took the money and forthwith distri- buted it among various charities, retaining not a franc for himself. These Itepublicans do act hand- somely sometimes,</p>
        <p>The Grand Duke and Princess •Alex of Iresse arrived in London on Monday and remaiq at Buckingham Palace. Definite arrangements have not yet been made as to the date of the Queen's visit to Italy, nut she will probably leave about the mid- dle of March and remain away fora month. Several -Royal dinner pavies will be given at Osborne dur- ing the next few weeks. Contrary to the predictions of all the prophets, the Princess of Wales suddenly decided to return to Sandringham from Marlborough House on Saturday, accompanied by the Prince and her daughters. The Princess's movements have recently afforded considerable pabulum to the society journalists.</p>
        <p>&gt;in George Augustus Sala, in Lis "Echoes of the "Xeek "tells how he once missed meeting General Omit at the hospitable board of the late Mr. G. W. Childs, of the " Philedelphia Ledger." It was in Decerz,!_.er,lB79. Mr. Childs had invited Mr. Skda and several other visitors to dinner at the Continen- tal Hotel, and, of course, all were anxious to meet the great warrior and ex-President, so acceptances were the rule,- But" there's many a slip 'twixt cup and the lip."</p>
        <p>r Let- the veteran journalist tell what happened : ;ws. At noon on the day on which the dinner was to be 7en we left the hotel to see the passing of an im- --saa -Ise procession of political associations, militia cori. s, trades unions, and so forth in honour of Gem ml Grant, who was to be the Republican can- didaa. eat the next Presidential election. Alas and aladir I We were foolish enough to cross the main street. how many scores of thousands of people these vs, ere about I know not; but I do know and „memb€ •r to my sorrow, that the crowd was so dense t-, we were unable to cross the street again, OA and that '; was nine o'clock at night before we re- turned t i he Continental, half frozen, zlejected,and o t. dinnerless: Truly, George Augustus's fund of per- sonal reminiscence is practically inexhaustible.</p>
        <p>We may en )ect to hear a great deal in the next few days about the possibilisy of an early election. As far as I can icertain,however,more sober-minded al. men of the rank and tile on both sides would depre- catna dissolution at the present time. The Liberals who have been do \ wn to the constituencies are in most cases convinct. 4i that a campaign against the nouie of Lords won. id be in the highest degree po- pular, and they seek pretty confident that the /1 Government could cot •nt on increasing their majo- rity.m. But I believe t. hat it is the pretty general opinion of the party wagers on both'sides that resent time db•not want the constituencies at the P ,l the turmoil of a general tection, especially after a tsenting adjoining winter of bad trade. Two n lembers to whoml have , spoken on the subject, repr. con- stituencies in the North of E. gland, are -aweed on iitT this point, although one is a strong Consellative Liberal. Iha no I and the other an enthusiastic . ' doubt that the opinion is fairly representative' of , that of members generally on bot.h sides. Considera- tions of this kind are bound to ).lave weight with' the Government,and whatever may happen to their' bills thefe is little doubt that they Will—if no un- foreseen circumstances arise—go on wi.th their pro- gramme, at all events for the present.. They will be at least "heaping up" the indictme:it against the House of Lords.</p>
      </div>
