        <p>igr Dumas -Nrzum,, .„,„40tfuit mateciats weAre-to.irear-iththekimmenlif rellinie is-exercising. the minds of many-prevtdent tigsnis who are accustomed to look ahead. (Weans Sisit cre.ped fab4cs will, it seems, conitiiine ag liNsough the cdtning lemon the grist %giblets. *km black will find favour than colours', thoosilik tillM charming stuff is prepared in the &amp;Indira tlites, and of a thicker make than of yore. if* **pea go across • a Dtavers effect is the- techatilai Urns. These stripes are sometimes craped into t...k.se Material, but generally are of silk, BA though Nua-iuch wide ribbons had been run on, and then es-Aped.. Laat year they appeared to be tacked., U.l.s the stripes have altered their cb.araoter. They Noi.ll without doubt be made up with jet, an&amp; as r!lrirts with contra,3tiug bodices. The wear of these niaterials can he depended on. There is a gocab te:--acioy of thread, mohair being extensively-used titeir manufacture, and there is nothing more riarahle. The stripes vary much and the BiZ3 Of time creping, the fabric being crinkled all over. Strti.w hats are gf-dag to be very much worn this cneinj,priag at,d somtutr, but they are all of saariorPly f,ncy raLternl, and, from toe shape!, it ie clear that we shall have to wear our hair dressed very low behind, in order to keep our hats- at, the proper an;!c. With these very large veils will be 11,c-winery, and the makers of feecy nets are already producing in extra widtiv. Burderings are oho Leen upon them.</p>
        <p>A large hat is in dark brown felt, with the brim bent down to lie almost on tlie4curled fringe. Rosettes of bright green velvet aro placed inside the brim, and the outside ;1 trimmed Iwith shaded green wings and brown velvet laid in folds round the osown, and rosette' baok and front. This is a pretty model in beaver-coloured felt, with helio- trope and brown trimmings, or in all black, with Small.peony-coloured rosettes or the now shade of dull pink. Broad reps and diagonal cloths are much worn, particularly in shot colourings. A very pretty fashion of making such a gown is one which retakes six yards of black moire silk. The skirt is made in two parte, and the bodice has a shaped basque put on at the waist-line.. The opening at the neck may be filled in with coloured silk, covered with guipure, or a fancy jabot of lace may. De added to brighten the gown.</p>
        <p>A very simple but pretty outdoor costume for a child of five is a smock-shnpe dress with broad- brimmed hat, and if 50 or 54 inch cloth, such as the Bavarian or chamois makes, is used, two and a-half to two and thtee•quarter yards wi?l be ample for this costume' or three yards of ordinary double-width material. A pretty costume, more wintry in design, is shown ka one which has a combination of dark brown-faced cloth, with a panel and' sleeves of blocs sat-in and a tiny zonave of black velvet. The softest grey chinchilla edgeG this costume, and is further repeated round the tie,_llbr; _-":-,-</p>
        <p>~tticoats,.in the 6rat instance, should be a good and sensible length. hava seen many a pretty underskirt allowed to .cling ,round. tnundy booth, and thus gather to itself quaoities of mire, which, if brought into cur carelu;,-.llrpll ,12ouses in any other fashion, would, cause a vuo: terrible commo- tion,. but under existing circumst4nces is. tolerated . because, forsooth, "we cannot Islr it." Now this is all noruiense, and it is possible to go out on the rauddie3t day—in town, at any rate —end yet return home with a clean petticoat, guileless of all bab small sp7shes of mud on the outside, and not smeared all round the foot hem as above men tioned.</p>
        <p>Yiurs loving friend,</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Mr..oscar Wile's new play is said to be full of para- doxes. -The subject is an ideal husband. The Empress of Russia has just bought a sealskin mantle *hich has cost four hundred and twenty guineas. A marriage has been arranged, and will shortly take place', s between Lord Romilly and Miss Grey ~Egepton. • The Princess of Wales is sitting to Mr. Luke Fildes for her portrait, which promises to be one of the finest that has been painted.</p>
