      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>. The Empress of Russia tended her husband through- out his severe illness like a hospital nurse, and it Wa3 1 only on the serious representatiOni of the physicians she at last consented to take some sleep. -a 2a Acmes Larkcom has been appointed permaneatly ' • on the b..`aff of professors of singing at the Royal Acad- emy of Mt.:ie. It is only since the advent of Dr. women have been permitted to teach. Mackenzie that The Queen has g.•ven her patronage to the entertain- ment which the MarmH. on a ees of Tweeddale is getting up .n 2 aid of the Scottish branch at the Empire Theatre reventiou of Cruelty to . p of the Society for the i Children. Mrs. George Anthony Fen Mick, on her favourite hull: ter, Silver King, is, says the " Lady's Pictorial," a well- known figure with the North Warwickshire, the Pytchley, and neighbouring packs. It is now more than forty years since Miss Alice Ogle, of Kirkby Hall, in Northum- berland, was " entered "to fox hounds, yet to-day sees ; her riding ever a country in the same fearless way as ; when she was a girl. 1 The Hon. Logan E. Bleckley, Chief Justice of Georgia predicts that there will be a career for women on the Bench and at the Bar of that State. He declares that the presence of ladies on the Bench with their hushands would render the court-room a more decorous, refined, elevated, and dignified arena for the transaction of legal business. " Let all the judges," he says, "be marri ed men, and let their wives preside with them in all cases not unfit for ears polite."</p>
