      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Grey Hair.</head>
        <p>Grey Hair.</p>
        <p>When the hair prematurely turns grey, it is generally owing to debility, a lone, illness, fever, or skin disease. While, or during the time persons are suffering from fever, or directly after, I have seen, not unfrequently, nearly all the hair' on the head turn grey, even in very young children. This may sometimes be checked by moderate brushing (two er three times a day) with a brush made of split whalebone. This is tie best kind of brush, but is expensive. A stimulating, Ise., lotion should then be ai..talied to the roots of the hair by means of a piece of sponge. With care and attention tho grey- ness is not only checked, but the faded hair often re- sumes its natural colour. Of ceurso the cause of this abnormal condition of the hair must receive attention, and everything should be done to improve the general health. Sometimes a ferruginous tonic is essential or useful, but this dcpends upon the condition of the I person.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Packing a trunk well.</head>
        <p>Packing a trunk well.</p>
        <p>Do you know ho Wto pack a trunk well ? asics Ruth Ashmore in the" Ladies Home Journal." And if you don't how many people do you know who do? And wouldn't you gladly give a dollar for a large, and fifty cents for a small trunk that is properly packed ? The packer comes with dozens of sheets of tissue paper and several pieces of tape. You can it where your belong- ings are, and as skirts and bodices are taken down say which you want. Then the bodices have their sleeves stuffed with paper to keep them in sbape,the trimmings carefully covered with it ; the skirts are properly folded the bonnets and hats have tapes pinned to them, and these same tapes are tacked to the side of the hat box,so that no matter how much the trunk may be shaken not a feather nor a rose moves out of its place. Tnen when everything is done, there is laid on the top of the last tray a.list of the things that are in the trunk, so that you don't lose your temper searching for the pink bodice which isn't there, or the tan-coloured shoes which you expressly requested should be left at home.</p>
        <p>How to Keep Bouquets Fresh.</p>
        <p>A vessel of water is required ; the vessel should be large enough to allow the submersion in it of a plate or dish holding the bouquet to be preserved, and a bull glass to cover the bouquet. The dish or plate should contain no moss or other material ; the water should be limpid and quite pure. Place the plate at the bottom of_the water, and on the plate submerging it, place the L(,uquet, which is maintained in an upright position by a weighted base previously a.ttaelied to it. • This being done, the bouquet is covered with a bell-glass, the rim of which ought to fit exactly to the flat part of the plate the bell-glass should be entirely filled with water, and without toe least air; bubble. Then all are raised to- gether, plate, bouquet, and bell-glass, filled with water, and phial on a table, carefully wiping the exterior, but leaving Zila the plate around the base of the bell-glass a little provision of water, which preve.nts the entrance of air. The flowers in this condition will be preserved in all their freshness for several weeks, and their beauty .is increased by a great number of bubbles of gas pro- ! /lac:0 by the respiration of the leaves, and which attach thetrAt/t.s te the petals, appearing like pearls. The edge of the plate and the water that it contains should be concealed b 7 a light bed of moss, in which are set some other flowers. In the evening, by artificial light, a bouquet thus arratked produces a charming effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CAPTURE OF aTEWAY O.</head>
        <p>CAPTURE OF aTEWAY O.</p>
        <p>In fact, the place on which the nsolor and his com- rades now looked so eagerly was the Eivra ,Dvrasa kraal which Lord Gifford had discovered about the same time to he the resting place of the fallen king ;- and the major, ignorant of Gifford's intention and hooe,. decided on taking action at once. As mounted mei:icon-1d not roach the bottom of the valley without making a tedious circuit, Maid. Marter desired his troopers to relinsinish ' their steel scabbards and !all accoutrements that were likely to rattle, and led his squadron northward three miles, till a less precipitous face of the hill was rekched, ' while a small detachment was left on the mountain in charge of the discarded accoutrements and pack-horses. At the same time a company of the Native Contingent was ordered to make its descent down the steep hill-side' towards the kraal, but to remain closely concealed in" the forest till they saw the red-coated cavalry emerge from the head of the narrow valley. At a quarter to two p.m. the King's Drag,,«on Guards began to lead their horses by their bridle down the steep and perilous slope and by three o'clock they had reached the bottom of the valley, but with the greatest difficulty. They crossed the rocky bed of a stream, and re-mounted in a hollow out of siglat of the kraal. Next they had to circumven, Ihe barrier of a snake fence, a marsh, some long grasst and rocks, but after a two miles' gallop they succeeded in completely surrounding the place, while the Native ' Contingent dashed across from their hiding place, and formed up on some open ground to the south of it. In ' realty, they were the first men on the ground, as they were on foot, and could move over iii. taral obstacles more 'quickly than the horses. They rushed into the kraal shouting to the startled followers of the king, " The white men are here—you are taken !" Major Matter rode directly up to theentrance of the kraal, and called upon Cetewayo to yield. " Enter—l am your prisoner," Cetewayo was heard to reply. As he might have to encounter a snare or some madness born of savage des- peration, the major prudently declined this invitation, and again summoned the king to come forth. Then the unfortunate Cetewayo, looking weak, weary, foot- . sore, and very sick at heart, came out of the humble little kraai. With a CEatAirl ;11-11.9,int Qf indignity, he rc- . polled a Drageoraluardsman who wAS about to soizn him 1 " White soldter," 111, exclaimed, " touch me not—l sur- , Inder to your chief !" The few occupants of the kraal be,d.1:114 taken edmpletely by surprise, made no resistance, an Iv'ere all captured. They consisted only of the and • a gill, One of the men who was too king, a. chief named Umkosana, nine4men and a boy, five WOD..°I Infirm to t. .6 Br.itih Battles on Land and as prisoners of war.— Pavel was left bchind. The rest were removed Sea."</p>
        <p>• ~...-..,..--.opk,</p>
        <p>.ding in a wagonette A number of collierg Wgfe proft .swood, near Wigan, to their work at Park Colliery, Cht). ,eized-and the ee. on Tuesday morning,when ,the trap ck. cupants were thrown out. One colixrNr named Liptrot luple of hvurs ; was mortally injured, and died within a c, and three others sustained seriousinjuriens</p>
        <p>" Rich the treasure, Sweet-the pleasure, Sweetest pleasure after pant" Dryden Honzocea illeason "IT TOUCHES THE SPOT."— V, Aye that is what " Flomocra" does, AV And does it quickly ton—whether it's- a toothache or neuralgia, with all their shooting pains, or eczema, with its painful and clistrest-ing irritation—or piles that makes thousands of lives wretched. Rheumatism in the joints, or muscles has been cured even of years' standing— while for cuts, , Tr- U • •e H -...ff, ::. .. -4., Q burns, and bruises it is far ahead of any ointment that has ever been before the public. LORD CARRICK says "Home-EA" cured him of bleeding piles, when all else failed: that he gave some to a labourer who was lamed by a stone fa.llinc, upon him, whom it cured. A woman had a pdin in th'e elbow and could not bend it H,., for a year, and it cured her, : and anotherused it for scurvy ~, .\., , on her leg, and it was doing her good. One letter closes from him with the words, "It is the most won- derful stuff that I ever came across.", , LORD COMBERMERE says " HomocEA" did him more good than any embrocation he had ever used for rheumatism. Remember that -- L'llomfiamonicEA" atiosunbdauneas ~, s allays irritation al- .. most as soon as applied. All wholesale houses stock " Homocze." It can be obtained. from Chemists and others at is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box, or will be sent by post for is. 3d. and 36., from the Wholesale Agency, 21, Hamilton k•quare, Birkenhead.</p>
