      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PARTED BY FATE;</head>
        <p>PARTED BY FATE;</p>
        <p>OR THE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE.</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LYEBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of "lone the Pride of the Mill," &amp;c., &amp;e.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXIX.</p>
        <p>'I WOULDN'T MALEY YOU TO SAYE youE. LIFE."</p>
        <p>"Matters might have drifted on in this way for- ever, had not an unexpected event happened," sobbed Uldene, breaking down completely now, "and that was the announcemena that greeted my eyes in the paper one day—of my husband's approaching marriage. Only God knows what I sutfared as I held the paper in my hand. The words seemed to stand out before my chnsed eyes in letters of fire. My heart gave one great, awful throb, and I fell to the floor like one dead. It was many a day before I regained consciousness again, aud re dised what was transpiring aroued me. "'Was lin time to stop the marriage ?' I aaked!myself, wildly, for it must be stopped at any cast. I dared not offend God and man by lettins: the ceremony go on; and then—though revered from Rutledge as completely as though I were indeed dead—still he was ray husband ; yes, he was mine—rhino ! "I made my way to Black-Tor Irieht-liousee'' reaching there one hoar in aclvancesaf the ceremony. I had barely stepped upon the island ere a man came hastily up the path, and I drew back into the shadow of the trees until he should pass. As the moonlight fell across his face I saw, to my horror, it was he—the man whom you are holding yonder —be who hod wrecked my life by telling me the fatal story of the past—he who claimed to be my uncle awl guardian. "Another. step was heard, and be drew back motionless, among the trees—so near the spot where I had shrunk back that I could have put out my hand and touched him. As the third pereon advanced hastily, a terrible imprecation burst from my guardian's lips. "'lt is Rutledge Chester,' he cried fiercely, below his breath, yet thud enough for me to hear. He shall not marry the woman I love to-night. I—l will kill him first. I Owe him another grudge too. It was he who froced me from the ranks of society ; he it was who found me out and hunted me down discovering that I was a smuggler, a robber, and all that was in- famous to his virtuous oyes ; and he even traced to me the duel that took place at mid- night in the graveyard, bark of the old church in the suburbs. But he little knows that there and then I first met sweet Verlie Sefton, and held her captive in our rendezvous until she escaped. I would never have harmed one hair of her golden head. J. loved her too well, even though she abhorred nig; .1 paid back the old debt I owed Autledge CheateSS in...parting his firgt bride from him, but it has recoil on me. He is about to marry the woman I love;• Istatl say he shall die first—here and now.' - "I tried to cry out, but the sound died on in lips. The tall form drew nearer. I, who knew+ Rutledge so well, saw that it Was not he; yet the young man approaching was fatally like him. Ina moment Ise was abreast of the trees ; then the would-be aase.tin sprang from the shadows. I heard a cry, I' saw the flash of a cruel weapen, and Only the metcy of heaven prevented it from being buried ia the young man's breast. In the excite- nient • fallowing the wounded man's etartled cry, the mate yonder escaped. I saw Captain Lansing ehthe ' hurriedly up the path, stumble over the prostrate form in surprise, and there they came upott him, it seems. In the confusion I made my way to Verlie, •Wilibin I found alone in her bridal robes. She thought ma a ghost, at first, risen from the dead. I told her all, and she knew, poor girl, that her marriage with Rutledge could never be—he had a living wife. She could see, too, that I must never reveal myself to Rutledge, but go quietly away again. " Verile fainted, and I, with bitter tears, silently as a shadow, glided piftly away. That is why poor Verde has been 'tossing in the ravings of a brain fever ever since that night.. Heaven soften the sorrow that will be hers with the return of memory. I would have gone down to the grave withwt revealing myself if it had not been for the pitiful crime fastened upon the inno- cent prisoner here, and from which I, an eye-witnee.s, alone can save him. from the unjust sentence of a term el imprisonment for long years. . "I have told my story—saved from. infamy an , honourable name. I have done my duty. This is the fatal day—my eighteenth birthday—and hare and now let me die. My own confession has sealed my faze, but I implore you net to incarcerate me in an asylum. lam weak. I feel that lam dying—dying. "I have parted you from Vet lie, love," she sobbed, " but you will forgive me—fergive—l— loveyou—so." The words trailed off heavily from her white lips, and, for the first time, she turned her eyes, in affright, toward her handsome young husband. Should she flied horror and dismay on his face, or is'lnFr'eFsherrow because she had parted him from his love? "Rutledge," eho murmured, holding out he white arms. esse—Asais.aaaesa)...-- sea- But ere he could reach her side she had fallen be :k in 2 deep, death-like swoon. Of dourse, the greateA excitement reigned, and the stranger coon found himself in the prisoner's , box, in the place of noble Captain 'sawing, who had -bee!' honourably discharged. "1 ale now in the hands of the law, where yoU have always wished to. behold me," cried the stranger, turning fiercely', yet with a mocking smile, to Rutledge Chester ;' "hut I have cheated ' you, after all, from wedding tier Woman I love. I eish you joy with your maniac brkle,- for such slid surely be when she recovers froth this shock , returns to consciousness." cries and hisses from the crots'el: greeted • nark, and the judge, fearing per-aortal tins •raa n the prisoner, had him conveyed imme= ":"Pcc't n diately inner room, and the throng was sunee tents 'sed manly (13P4PA. . carrion Mel ene ed to a coach, and Rutledge mkt, gave the order.: `, hotel." "To the metre" Poor 1 Uld " "Poor Urse ldedl • , :ap less ene ! he )(!teatiful marble-white murmured, laytag'ttrhe 'zr ' "e face against his bosous, wld tears that were no shame Cis face. "How well to his ine,nhoori, stole. vn</p>
        <p>you have -loved In that moment bis• heatft - Feught a great battle with rh ki !ht and wrong.- emoak' Pit" Verlie out of hi: liffjfiirevcrrnere anti turn hioNe7T)* thought to poor jJlicne. He was only hual.7 „dt 'ar reader, ' .fift,., and if be ^.hed a few' bitter tears. &amp;rink vanished hope of Nvirmit,g his lost love for Mt; brik le' we must rernernber "to err is but human it,bee". Let said of him, he strug4led manfully to put all thoughts of Verlie' to fair face from him, and, learn to fo.-co the world without her, and rem-en. only I.lldalli, his young wife, who had been restoretl to him from- the very grave, it seemed-. An old physician was soon in attendance upon •Uldene. It was the same old physician, older and • greyer now, wtio had held Uidene in his arms when she wai a little bub,!, aud who had predicted such is atratnze, uncommon life for tee child as he gazed at the; wo, pink pdril lying like -a crumpled vise- leaf within bis own.</p>
