        <p>......eitog to oe her uncle, is, in I reality, a true descendant of the gipsy girl she spoke .I, cf, and that for k,eoerat ions past they have delibe- rately set about preparing this story, which haft been handvd •lowa from father to son, and setting it afloat to terrify and destroy the hapless daughters -of this ram These frail and beautiful women were so shoJked by the prediction, and brooded over it with stteh horrible anticipation, that con- "t brooding hi time turned their brains and stoe, ',eel raving in.lniaes. 1 The there- fairtzwipbc ,se:lausl malady _)efworethis . fete ialo4l. %-...-- : Ohili.'--. , _., .. ehilei ._.,,eaeb • 15610 d, .landed down to the no ta ...The man I:2A vah: in , an old offender against the P...ith.444 law*' g "No crime is too atrocious ::sepliecl ...e, for I,it' t° abLelrqi`'''' " I in'agme L - e ho 'a at th# end of his rope; he is v We many crirnesle ever again regain his wanted fo. freedom.'' - new this, for in Nble that the man 5.,1-. It id presum, 'eat he had, by there was news tk less than an hon.' en soul into 'vied his unfor&amp;ivs .. his own hand, .h. ehind him eternity. He left a .written oonfessionl\ word 44) say, it was almte," w however, and, strange . ''cted. for word the same as t.. doctor had prea,,l Ile was the last deseendam:. - 'girl, s2.nd of the gipsy , las back deliberatel) his people had for generath, ' ' A bonny race, and destroyed the fair daughters oi all for revenge's sake. , The man died as he had lived, un -repentant. ated, quite While this scene was being en enacted on another, and a sweeter one, was being no beautiful island tipped by the silvery 'waves of the glistening sea. Two persons sat on a mossy, fallen log; '-they were pretty, capricious, black-eyed Neddy .‘n gallant Captain Lansing. 1 "You will answer my question, won't you, Neddy, dear Neddy?', he says, winningly, edging up a little closer to the slender figure, and attempt- ing to take one of the little, restless hands that were toying with the wild flowers. "You have been my staunch, true, little friend through the darkest hours of my life. Be my sunbeam in brighter hours. Say that you will be my little bride, Noddy, darling." "I wouldn't marry you to save your life, Captain Lansing," she declares, starting up from the mossy log. " Will you tell me why, Neddy ?" be persists. "Because I—l=don't care very much for you, no, net a bit," she persists; but the blushes on the dimpled face tell him better. She does care for him.</p>
        <p>The smiling captain catches the wilful little beauty in his arms, and holds licr there, much against her will—until she has answered his question ; and the answer must have pleased him vastly, for, half an hour later, Noddy, blushing rosy red, slips into Verlie's room at the great, dark light-house, and holds up a little white hand, on the betrothal finger of which a diamond glistens like a star. "Oh, it's true, Vet-lie," she pants—" quite true, after all. Captain Lansing loves rile, and only me; and oh, Verlie, I'm so happy I have promised to be his bride."</p>
        <p>Verlie looked with quivering lips into the bright face so trandigured with beaming love; and she kissed the girl's ripe, red lips. "May you ever be happy in yelir love, Neddy," she whispers, softly. " itemeni%er, love is the sweet boon Heaven does not give to all." Then they talked of Uldane, in low, tender whispers.</p>
        <p>" S!4e is very low, they say," Neck-1y whispers. "May God grant her life instead of death." "Amen I" breathed Verb, uttering the word in whici a whole prayer was compressed with all her heart.</p>
        <p>And how fared it with Uldene at that critical moment? We bh%ll see.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XL.</p>
        <p>"LIVE FOR MY SAKE, DARLING !"</p>
        <p>All day long Uldene had lain in a death-like stupor, from which she aroused just as the bells in the far-off belfry tolled the midnight hour: Raising her great, dark, fathomless eyes, she raw the face of the good old doctor, whom she had known from infancy, bending over her. " Where am I? Have I been ill ?" she mur- mured, attempting to struggle up from her pillow, but the effort 103 too much for her, and she fell backward, half fainting. " Yeirlare—irery ill, my dear," said the doctor, gently ; "so ill that S'oar life hangs by a single thread. You must not exert yourself if you would live. Here, drink one drop of this,' he said, taking a small vial from the stand close by the bedside,- and dropping one drop into a wine-glass full of cold, clear water. " This will produce refreshing sleep.. Ten drops would be fatal. But you are to He held it to Uldeno's lips, and she drank the potion, and shortly after the white lids closed softly over the great, dark, piteous eyes. Believing she slept, the doctor had stolen from the room, and Rutledge had taken his place at her bedaile ; not at the side of it, where she might awaken, and, seeing him there, receive a great shock to her nerves, but at the head of the bed, where he could watch Uldene, while he himself remained unseen. The sound of his footsteps as he approached made no sound on the thick velvet carpet. A low moen broke from Uldene's lips, and peering breathlessly from behind the screen of silken curtains, Rutledge could see that there were tests on the long, dark lashes. "1 em to live," see moaned, feebly—" live to curse the life of the one being on earth I would die to make happy—live to be a barrier between Rutledge and the girl he loves—live to know that he ha tei me, and will rue the hour life struggled hack to the breast that should have been stilled in death. "Oh, Rutledge, love of my life, you will never know how my heart bleeds for you." A moan that was most pitiful to hear broke from her white lips. " What is my life and my poor, blind, wershipful love to you, dear? I, whose love has been your doom? But I will repair the terrible wrong I have done you in taking you from Verlie. You shall be free to woo and win her for your bride, love—yes, you shall be free at the cost of my own poor, worthless life." She put out her feeble hand toward the little , marble stand close by the bedside, and grasped one ol the vials, and held it up in the flickering light, murmuring, faintly: "He said one drop of this gave strength, while ten meant death, swift, sure, and with but one fierce throb of pain. Ten drops, then, shall be any portion; and as I drink it let the sweet thought sustain me that I am giving my life—yes, my life —to make my darling happy, for then he will wed Verlie, whom he loves. He will never know that his happiness was purchased with my life— titer !claw tsh my last words were, 'Oh, love of I my heart! my darling husbend, farewell !" I Uiciena raised the vial to her lips. It liquid eontopts flashcri_ like gleaming reSarTi in the flicUrlng but it Was dashed from the little 1 hand by a stronger one, and a hoarse, thrilling cry echoed through the silence of the room : "Uldene, any wife! live for my sake I for my /gar.' Tie voice and the words thrilled poor Uldene'e heart to the core. In that one supreme moment I Rutledge Chester's heart was touched and awakened as it had never Lean touched before. 1 When 6he moment of silence would hwee'given hiT3 his heart's desire, his freedom back again, he hat awakened to the truth. Uldene, his young wife; . was'. dearer to him than all the world beside.' in' that awful moment he had 'chosen between Vtlih, and Tll4cr-- yes, heart had •0120 Out to ii(:,or hapless L laeß72 who would have given her yoe:leg life to have Lelirchaeed his linMness. A.1,1.1t3, deep love, such as he had never felt Uhlenee'etien in the old "dare came to him now. The great strength and depth of U,ldene'e love had, in theSsetpreme moment when her life hung in the balance; Won his in return. , Ifs clasped The frail form of this per, desolate girt-bride in his erhng arms, pillowed her dark, curly head on his h'ihaast, inurinuring,, brokenly: "Live for my a sakeee Cldene. I have heard all. Live, and we will cod:nem:a life anew, and be all worhl to each other."</p>
        <p>, Two white arms stole aihstThil his, neck, and a joy, that he never forgot crime intO her beautiful face. "Is this a dream, Rutlect§;ts?'" she whispered, nestling closely, fearfully within the. shelter •of those Strong arms. "If this is' hut a ,dream, let me ...Ho dreaming Oars. On licttledge;• has my great love won you at fwd.?' As soon 6,s Tilden° was able to trif.-Al; Rutledge not: her abroad, leaving behind a farewell note to 'Hie, every !Lie of which U.dcrie* heartily en\a. rledge awl Tilden° remained abroad. two and returning at ti:o erd of that time, year, Ir, ,t persons whom they met as they landed the 1 were ,tain Lansing and his bride, picitiari4-,, Cal gay Nedc still 4 • / cgx„.oll !ht to have come a week earlier,' she •ng Uldene hearl-y htt* declared, 6, oil haA lob a 1.7...:And we :dia iniV,tthin,4-*ri,</p>
