        <p>"Jfe I" cried Noddy, aghast. "Do you think VA speak of myself perfectly lovely 7' I'll let other peop!e soy that," 4be added with a eauey little rognist'e "The dee6t;poten would ha by no means out of Nytira No,My," laughed Rutledge Chester. l3ait we.,n't me," declared Neddy. " You both seem determined not to guees right ; so, as l'in dying to tell, you may as well know that it ss—Vet lie. She has married my brother Dick. lie always adored her, poor fellow, but there were eewaye so many rivals in the path, and he, being isishful, always thought ho hadn't the least ghost • of a chance or winning her." Rotledge Chester and Captain Lansing looked at each other, blushed, and turned away smiling. Both were secretly plceaed that oweet Verlie Ii hecenee the bride of Richard Temple, for no betser fellow lived than he. Our story is ended, dear reader, unless it is to Fitaw that three handsome villas, side by side, ee one of the finest a.sevues of tie gay capital, , Lesi iu one of them dwell Rutledge and his idolized ' ~),ing wife, who is now the pride of his heart and ; in the next mansion lives Captain Lansing if,Teddy ; and last, but not least, is the impos- • une in which fair Verlie and her husband fug lA,. athhts En:Le the most deveted couple to each y at i,ezo ever r shone uthrpoene. e ' tie gay capital knowe the strange, laver one romantic story or thlievehsanwdeo"f3 entangled as one.tiree SC) fate; aud they tell, for in this world, Loo, how happy they are out of darkness, through te...' -°3 "Every heart finds its ovv-i:NrC6-riNi.: Some time in life ; for this is fate." • t a the -k Sefton and Nella aq welcome Saes s t , -eaters who was Ufa. tlire„, iaus lona, So Is Mi. Lennox, op poor, patient, C. ks truly, she will hour of nee " .iene never referreldne':3o \**i • and if report spea. friono. in her not be Miss in Ich I°Jagsr• each other as they has. the past bat Vedic and 131. „ ey twined once, and that w, t'c' murmur, as Va, I done in happened as it -ly their arms around . sunny childhood: pretty brides, and did, "It is best that every `thing Uldene. The mysery thi2t shadowed Your ea. life is a mystery no longer. s_k4id if Rutledge and , had not been parted by fate the very &amp;Mg' almost, I would never have been IldS brides"' And in their henpiness we will leay.them,, cleat? reader, remembering the course of truelove never' does run smooth, but, to quote happy, dark-eyed Uldene's words, "All's well that ends well:" THE END.</p>
        <p>DOING ALL SITE. REASONABLY COULD..</p>
        <p>"If," be murmured, as she gazed up into his • eyes, "if I had only a little more money to count on---" Then he stopped. "What would you• do ? " she asked. What would you do ? "Ii —I would have a home of my own. I would,. there ! " This seemed like business. " How much have you got ? " she said, and her voice sounded firm. "Fifteen shillings a-week." He hung his head. "How much do you give a week now for your room? ' "Five shillings." "Stuff; we can get a room plenty good enough for both of 11; and a pantry to cook in, for that. How much do your meals cost ? " "Seven shillings a-week." "Pooh! we can both live for that, cooking at home. What do you do with the other three shillings?" "Well, I—l smoke, you know." " Well, you can give that up, anyhow; you don't spend three shillings a-week on smoking." "No, oh, no. Of course there are expenses; other fellow and such—' " Well, you can stop those, tco. What else ? " •"I try to save a little." " That is talking. How much have you saved " "Eleven pounds." "That's plenty for the minister and a start, and some to spare for a necktie or something for you to stand up in. wen; ? " " ‘Ve!l, Mary?" "Go u-head. I can't de e-sery thing, you know. You've got to ask me."</p>
