      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WOMEN LIKE PRAISE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN LIKE PRAISE.</p>
        <p>"Jazk : "I'll tell you what's the matter, George. You don't praise your wife enough. Even if things don't go right, tliere's no use growling. Praise hr efforts to please, whether they are successful or not. Women like praise, and lots of it." George "All right. I'll remember it." • George (at dinner, same day) : "My dear, this pie is just lovely.! It's delicious ! Ever so mucu better than those my mother used to make." George's Wife: "-Huh You've made fun of every pie I ever made, and now" George : "But. this is lovely." George's Wife: "That came from the baker's.'</p>
        <p>"I PASSILD your house yesterday, sir." "I hope yon always will," was the response of Mr. Randolph. A LADY said to "Garrick : "I wish, sir, you were taller." Garrick replied : "My clear madam, I should be happy to stand higher in your estimAtion." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"JOHNNY," said a Sunday-school teacher, •i when your father and mother forsake- you, who will take you up ?" "The police," responded the youth. "SALLY," said a fellow to a girl who had red hair, "keep away from Jae, "or you'll set L,:c. on fire." "No danger of that," said she ; " are too too green to burn." PHOTOGRAPHER'S Assistant "Mrs. Van Per- kins complains that her pertraits don't look like her." Photographer—" Complains, does she? She ought to be grateful." " Tun sister of a philosopher asked her brother— " What is mind ?'' "No matter," was his re- sponse. She then asked—" What is matter ?" "Never mind," he replied. "TOM," you're the laziest man I ever met. You are always leonine, on a gate." "I don't think I'm lazy. I ieft my brother at home: he said he was too tired to lean on a gate." COLLECTOR: "Won t you subscribe something to our fund, Mr. Brief ? ' Lawyer : "Have you asked Bronson ? " Collector : "No." Lawyer : "Well, I'd advise you to ask Bronson. He'll give you something handsome." Collector : "But your subscription'?" Lawyer: " Ob—l ? Oh, yes. Well, I won't charge you anything for my advise about Bronson. Good-morning."</p>
