        <head>• MIL DOWN'S MILITA_RY , LAYD.</head>
        <p>• MIL DOWN'S MILITA_RY , LAYD.</p>
        <p>--- TUE, ANNUAL IV l'ileTEß. The second (3 ectater of Mr. T. J. Dawn's Zailitary bar .:I. Was:inl., 'onlt,t,t the W,orkmer2 id n Friday-evening last week a Club, and proved to be a great ; 'llecP4ds. Tiv4 military band—apart front the well- lv- . Aown orr,ineettra—numbers some 50 instrument a- \lists, of whom there there were 413 present at the repast. 'the respected conductor of the band, Mr. T.J. Down, together with Mrs. and Miss Down, and Mr. Down, th:i. were present at the invitation of the band, and among the senior members present were Mr. J. Taylor, . Mr. Dell, and Mr. Riley (Stockton Heath). The cater- ing was very efficiently carried out by Mrs. McOready, Of the White Hart, Sankey-st. Dinner over, the party did their best—and were successful—in making the after Proceedings entertaining and interesting. A'toast list had been prepared and it was interspersed with songs, Which were sung by various members of the gathering. Before the programme was opened, however, Mr. T. J. DOWN delivered a very interesting addrese to the band. He said : ely first duty is to thank you, 'Which Ido very sincerely, for inviting my family and myself to join you on this gay and festive occasion. (Applause.) We have the pleasantest recollection of last year's meeting and I have no doubt we shall carry away with us an equally agreeable sense of enjoyment to-night. For my own part I may say that I am never ao happy as when I am in the society of musical people, ' and I am happiest of all when I am surrounded by the members of my own bands. (Applause.) If I have a regret to-night, it is that the members of the orchestra are not with us. (Hear, hear.) If they only knew how Pleasant these evenings are and what a hearty welcome they would receive from their brethren of tie military band I feel sure they would only be too glad to frater- nize with you on these occasions. (Hear, hear.) Ido not anow,ancl I cannot imagine,why the two bands have hitherto kept so much aloof from each other. I know of no poesible reason why they should do and I am hope- ful that I may have the, pleasure and privilege of meet- ing the whole of my large musical family when you next do me the honour to invite me to your annual gathering. (Applause.) I think we have every reason to be proud of the present position of our military band. It has been in existence for over seven years and from the first has gone on most satisfactorily. Unpleasant- ness of any kind ie a thing unknown among U 3. and what I may call the affectionate regard of the members for their conductor appears to increase with the years. I often think of the great kindness I receive from you. and can assure you I thoroughly appreciate it. I feel it all the more because I am fully aware I have many faults,and am perhaps the only cantankerous man in the band. (Laughter.) At any rate Mr. Taylor says I am --(renewed laughter)—and I have no doubt he has some good reason for making that statement. But with all my faults I have the welfare of the band at neart and you know it, and you also know that whatever I may do cr say, I mean no harm, and that I am n the true and sincere friend of everyone of you. (Loud applause.) With your valuable assistance and cordial Co-Operation the band has become quite an important institution, and I have not the slightest doubt has had the most healthy and beneficial influence upon music in Warrington and the neighbeurhood. From time to time I hear the work I am doing and have I),.en doing for so many Years pa.stapoken of as a " fad " or a " pastime." I wish to state most emphatically that it is nothing of the kind, and if there is -e, a chiel amane us takin' notes " of what I am saying, I hope he'll" prent 'em." My musical work ia no " fad," but a genuine endeavour on my part to benefit my fellow-creatures, by doing what I can to give them an interest in an art which has been an untold source of pleasure to me and which I believe will benefit and elevate any person rich or poor, who devotes himself or herself te it. (Applause.) To carry on this work I have had to make many sacrifices. I have had to give up all the" fads "in which I formerly indulged. I have had to give up nearly all my leisure time. I have had to flake long, and sometimes wearisome journeys to enable nee to teZ preeett liA rtbear6al, I Pave given all my ;energy to the work, and I Leen epeni Mere thal VIQQQ , of ray money on it. (Renewed applause.) Gentlemen, x = -repeat it, the work I am (Icing at Walton is no "fad,' but serious honest hard work. A work so hard that I could not eiessibly carry it on or bear the strain of it, Were it not for the invalvable assistance I rec6ve from the members of my family and others'. (Hear, hear.) Prom first to laetilitave been animated by thorougilly disinterested motives. I have Sought neither honour nor glory. The cady reward I have ever wished for, I heve had, and that has been the success of the work I have Undertaken. (Applause.) When we commenced this band there was nothing like a military band in this town. , F)urs is still the only complete one, but several others ' have clarinets, and I suppose it is only a question of "nee for them to blossom into full military- bands. Then think of. the great increase in the number of brass bands h the town and the marvellous improvement in their Playing. At the present time the Warrington Association clelmateur Bands includes 13 bands and 250 or more Performers. Few towns of the size of Warrington (if any), can boast of such a large number et wind instru- &amp;eat players, and I honestly believe that the work we have been doing at Walton has had a great deal to do With the wonderlul musical development which has taken place. (Hear, hear.) Well, gentlemen, so much eor thepast. Now for the future. I don't see myself Why the future should net be as bright, as prosperous 'nal as successful as the past has been. The band IS still sound in wind, etrong and vigoreite. Only the toraluctor is growing older, but " there's life in the old dogeYet,"—(applauee)—and if Mr. Taylor will allow him to 'aye a growl now and again, and with a continuance of the assistance at present so kindly and cheerfully given, ~I° hopes to be of some service to the band for years yet to come. (Applause.) Mr. Dows, jun., submitted the first toast, which Ve_as "The Associated Bands of Warrington," and in the rune of a short pithy speech, alluded to the growing nli Portanco of the Warrington Associated Bands, and aitso the benefits which were likely to be derived from ; by young bands. He did not See why through the instrumentality of that association, Warrington should not soon turn out bands equal at least to Facies band, Which was so successful at last year's competition. (Applause.) . ;Ma. J. TAYLOR, as the secretary of the Association, responded to the toast. In doing so he alluded to the lt,,l'o'eeth in the number of wind instrumentaliets during 'lie Past 23 or 39 years—and especially in clarinet Players At the time he alluded to it was difficult to more than two or three clarinet players, but now, chiefly owing to Mr. Down's, there was a very ta:ge number of them. (Applause.) That the playing *ass instruments had greatly increased was evident fromn the fact that during the coming season they ex- liected to have 13, bands taking their part in the Park. 13Plause.) Each of these bands would have 21 instru- i e,nta--aut apart from that he felt certain from care- t investigations he had made, that there were close (13°11 a thousand brass instrumentalists in Warrington. f,kaPPlamee.) Much of this, it could not be denied, was 11,wing to the kindness of their respected conductor, who expended trouble and money in the good work to 5u eh an extent that they could not compute it. (Ap- agnsea Then again, there was the magnificent orchee- a: Yrhich Mr. Downs had organised and trained. What Lel influence that work was having upon the public ! t them observe the number of children whom they ielght see passing along the streets with their string eatrument cabs, going for their lessons, and let them '2 themselves to what agency this was due. They Wokuld find that it emanated from the keen interest 'a ltivated for years, at Walton. (Loud applause.) In ;-;onclusien the speaker alluded in high terms to the leatittk being done by Mr. Down, who received much as- el ante from his family and their lady president. (Sp- kna.use. ) DELL proposed "The health of Mr. Down and family."wara_, He did so in felicitous terms, and was e„"-HY supported by the party's applause. .ra-tr• T. J. Lows responded. He said he was deeply a... ,eeful to them for the kindly, and he was sure genuine 1114.__Q heartfelt good will which they had manifested to p'aq and his family not only that night, but for years et a (APplauee.) He was quite sure it was a matter ia a!ncere regret to them to see Mrs. Down's usual place the band room so frequently vacant last year, and he t"ha:t equally sure it was a source of pleasure to themn able night to see her so much improved in health and Warmer he with them. (Applause.) No one took a , A mer interest in the welfare of the band than she did. letav,Ppaslateaste.) As to his son and daughter he was certain arid all times their desire to deserve the friendship and to do all in their power to promote th" ou SguloccletWill, ata. ssof the band. (Applause.) Downan"d'rwe:.ec_and family left for home shortly after ten, cheered lustily as they left the room. A very up ab;e- t.time .Was afterwards spent, the party breaking tit midnight wrerrhe —Messrsose wh oa'ssis.ted in providing the entertainment T. J. Dell, G. Riley, R. Rigby, J. Shaw, .I,Y. Waterfe'd- t\:' J. Jean, and others ; a reading by Mr. H. raerson, ;rcl flute solos by Mr. W. H. Podmore. The tswere in the hands of the following corn rrtkt l°:—Messrs. Rathtaill, Molyneux, Riley, Lawton, n secretary), and Dell (lion treasurer).</p>
