        <p>Dleasan</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES—v - %CARRINGTON. " WHITTIER, THE POET Hop- .W THE LARGER SERMON AT CAIP Ia Cairo-street Unitar" e&amp;-STRNET CEIAPEL. the Rev. W. H. Drum • eat 'Chapel, on Sunday evening, " Whittier, the Poe, ..earead, 8.A., delivered a sermon on tleman took for eef 'the larger Hope." The rev. e'en- exceedingliroe . !tiis text " Thy commandment is was a son of ' ate Psalm exix, 98. He said Whittier homestear' . ciq 'people, and was born in 1807 at a small scenes - 4renitlaverhill, Mass. rt was amidst its rural Of be .e occupations that his early years even spent. mW -eViearning he received but little, but this was than compensated for by the religious training of ersequaker home. Of that -home Whittier had given awns a picture, drawn by a faithful and tender hand.in 'Lis Winter Idyll called " Snow Bound." The first con- scious awakening of the poetic impulse within him was due to the lyrics of Beres. His first literary patron was Wm. Lloyd Garrison, and this connection determin- ed the future course of his life. Through Garrison he , was drawn into the world of jeurna.lisin and political writing and then into the full tide of the anti-slavery movement. "The Voices of Freedom" were among his first fruits as a religious poet. Whittier was fully aware that in making the cause of emancipation his own he was shutting the door upon any certain prospect of literary fame. Like the hymns of Luther or of Charles Wesley the verse of Whittier appealed to the popular ear ; and it thus sowed far and wide the seeds of a great enthusiasm, which made the movement of all modern movements most like a religious crusade. Great causes must be based upon reason, but they were carried by religion, and it nu,s for this reason he had classed his "Voices of Freedom" among his religious poetry. With all their artistic imperfections they had little in common with ordinary political verse. They were full of the moral conviction, the appeal to unselfish mot ives, the broad human sympathies, and the reverence for free- dom as alone in accordance with the will of God, which were among the surest signs of religious inspiration. The elements of the faith that inspired Whittier were, firstly ; His intense belief in God, and his sense of com- munion with him ; secondly, this inwardness in his thought of God combined with his quick sympathies, made his religion very broad and very human ; and thirdly, his optimism. Whittier appealed to " tile still witness "of his heart as the ground of his trust. Thus the existence of God was for him given as an inward revelation; it was an ultimate fact of experience beyond which he could not go. Ho was above all the preachers of a large hope and a large charity. Looking elsewhere than to any human creed for the source ef his own faith. he could afford to treat time honoured statements of belief with a freedom which was impossible to the con- ventional orthodox position. Whittier had a supreme reverence for the person of Christ. He saw in him "the heart of God revealed" and the pattern of human perfectness. But it was net his belief, but his life which in VVhittier's view made a man a Christian. His chris- tianity was in fact a form of spiritual mysticism with the human figure of Christ at the heart of it, giving warmth and colour to his belief in God, and filling his service of man with a sacred personal affection. His optimism made men flush with high purpose and fall into step with the forces of progress almost in spite of their own aoubt and indolence. There was no better way of salvation than this for the moral cowardice which was afraid of failure, orthe moral unbelief which thinks nothing is worth while. Hopefulness. not loud and exultant, but quiet and full of conviction was characteristic of all his. poetry. It was easy to bring against the optimism of Whittier the objection that it was merely a matter of individual feeling, and therefore only valid for the man who felt it. This was true to a, certain point, but they might not unreasonably hold that the puret and chastened feeling of those whose lives had been in deepest harmony with God was no caprice of pious imagination, but rather the reflection of eternal reality, the divine wbrd mirrored in a human soul that "who-so runs may read." There was one feature about Whittier's religious teaching which was marked by its absence. He was affected very slightly by the religious difficulties of the day. In this -he was an excep- tion to most of their modern religious poets. From first to last he was the friend of freedom, laving his best gifts on her shrine. He was the Quaker poet through and through, with his subdued grace of manner, amid the inward light reflected on his face.</p>
