      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CAIRO STREET CHAPEL, WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CAIRO STREET CHAPEL, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The annual report in connection with Cairo-street Chapel has just been issued. The report is prefaced by the address of the minister, the Rev. W. H. Drummond. The Committee in their annual statement observe : " We have not yet been able to complete the arrange- ments for the land adjoining the chapel and school, but hope to be in a position to do so shortly. They would also call attention to the change made in refererm to the Choir Fund. In future they propose that all loose ' money collected shall be appropriated f4T this fund. By so doing a convenient opportunity will be given to those who may wish to contribute a little extra help for the musical portion of the service which the Committee are anxious .to make as efficient and in harmony with the spirit of the services. is very satisfactory to the Committee to find that Drummond's ministry is appreciated and KoMise:s to become a successful one in the building Up of our con- ' gregati(ml WdlYblUl chaeacter. The wants of the school so earnestly Di`..!..sed forward;by Mr. Freeston, during his ministry, are again calling for attention u.rider Mr. Drummond's guidance in a special form for meeting the spirituAl and social needs of both congregation and school. We earnestly hope that the efforts now h!,ing made will Meet with such support from the congregation and their friends, as will bring the work to a most sue- ceegnl termination in every way." The report states that the total number of books in the school library is 1,372. The total number of issues has been 1,243 to 100 members showing an average of 24 per week as compared with 13 in 1g92. The following is a ssi tied list of books, in the general library: Fo.-try and drama, 56 ; science, 72 ; theology, ; history and travel, 208 ; fiction, 664; miscellaneous, 287. The annual report of the Sunday school states that the number of scholars en the books is : 'gale, 120: female. 139, compared with, in 1892: 133 males, 162 females. The average attend- ance was 127 morning, 185 afternoon, being a decrease upon the attendances of last year. The financial state- ' wents show the chapel and Sunday school to be in a satisfactory Condition...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE LIABILITY BILL: FROTEST AGAINST THE LORDS' "AMENDMENTS."</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above was held on Tuesday,at the llorsemarket-st. Cocoa Rooms, there being a good attendance of delegates. The report of the deputation committee that have visited the Hairdressers who departed from the rules of their asso- ciation was given, and was, in effect, that several shops were called upon who were favourable towards re-joining again, while others left it over for further consideration. Suspicions of each other was the only excuse assigned, and future efforts will be made to bring about the de- sired result. After this the Conned took into considera- tion the House of Lords' amendments to the Employers' Liability Bill, the following resolution being passed : " That the inemb(4s of the Warrington Trades and La- hour Council beg to offer their strenuous protest against the House of Lords' mutilation of the Employers'Liability Bill in regard to the contracting out clause, and urge moon the Government the necessity' for opposing any attempt to destroy the effectiveness of the Bill in respect to this clause, and it requests the member for the borough, Mr. H. Pierpoint, to support the desires of the Council." There being no other business of importance, a vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>When you have bronchitis, look alive I Don't let it run into something more serious. T.,ke Scott's Emulsion of Cod Liver 01 at once. It will ease your inflammation and effect a speedy recovery. Scott's Emulsion is well adapted to all lung troubles. It supplies new lung tissue and stops west?. Physicians, the world over, endorse it. Chemists sell it. 2s. 6d. and 4s. 6d,—(20)</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the Council of the London Liberal and Radical Union, held on Monday evening, over which Mr. James Stuart, ALP., presided, resolu- tions condemning the House of Lords for their mutila- tion of the Government bills and urging the abolition of the Upper Chamber were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>• • L H FJA AMINIE,SA_TURDA: FEIB-11-ATA_' Y 17, 1894 •</head>
        <p>• • L H FJA AMINIE,SA_TURDA: FEIB-11-ATA_' Y 17, 1894 •</p>
        <p>Mr. Kent was disappointed that there was noffeitg finite in thepapor to debate i 7 Reference 'had been made to the persecution whi.dife heretics received, but he held that it was not Persecution to leave a an alone and not put him in th . me offices of a church with which he was not in agre',.ftnent. It only became perse- cution when attempts were made to injure the man i either in his charac;et or in his trade. Mr. T. Birchi, J.P., who, although not a member, was allowe'a to speak, said that previous speakers had said tha, heresy was not now penecuted. He was afrld that they were mistaken., There were new no " hot beds," but those who read the papers would see, there was still much persecution by the orthodox people. Of course the church had changed from what it was 50 years age. He would dare any clergyman to preach the doctrine of predestination at the present time although it was in the creed of the Church of England. He held that theology and religion were two separate things. Religion was the attitude of their souls before God, but they might speculate in theology, and he held that an individual had the right of private judgment on all theological matters. The Chairman said that the discussion had been a very interesting one. It was always interesting to have the views of people who took different sides and who wore sincerely advocating their opinions. With regard to the subject under discussion, he pointed out that chemical knowledge was largely gained first by theory, and afterwards when it was found that certain things put together always produced certain results, the truth was established. Chemical knowledge was very largely the result of hypothesis. Its accuracy depended upon the accuracy with which the laws had been observed. He took it that the same thing obtained in spiritual matters. The truthfulness of religion could only be revealed by the application of its principles in their own lives. The essayist in reply was surprised to find that there was not a single orthodox person in the room. It was a mistake for them to think that he had come to defend the Unitarian faith, although had it been necessary, he could have done so. He proceeded to answer in detail the various criticisms, after which the meeting ter- minated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DEATH AND FIINEITAL OF COUNCILLOR</head>
        <p>DEATH AND FIINEITAL OF COUNCILLOR</p>
        <p>BONNELL.</p>
        <p>It is with regret we announce the death of Coun- cillor John Bonnell which occurred on Saturday morning last at his residence in Folly-lane. His death came unexpectedly, and it was a painful sur- prise to his family and numerous friends. Although his health had been in a delicate state for some time he was enabled to go about a little during the week, and his condition did not give cause Lor any anxiety on the part of his family, and they were greatly surprised on Saturday morning to find that ,he had died during the night. His death was due to ' acute pleurisy and pneumonia. Mr. Bonnell was 65 years of age, and leaves behind him two sons and five daughters to mourn his loss. His wife, who was a Miss Maddock, died five years ago. Mr. Bonnell was a Liberal in politics, and wa,s respected and esteemed by all who knew him. He was one of the representatives of Orford Ward in the Town Council, and was elected to that position in November, 1891. His opponent on that occasion was Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, solicitor. Mr. Bon- nell polled 345 votes, and secured the seat by a majority of 115 votes. Mr. Borman with his long experience in engineering work proved himself a useful member, and his advice and assistance in many improvements in the town was sought. His voice was not much heard in the Council Chamber, but this is not always a true criterion of a town councillor's ability. The Town Council Committee's, on which Mr. Bonnell served, were the Sanitary Works Committee ; Longford Depot Sub- Committee ; Health Committee ; Street Improvement and Lighting Committee ; and Cemetery Committee. For some years previcua to 1889, Mr. Bonnell was a member of the Warrington 13€)-ard of Guardians, but at the election in April of that year, he was amongst the unsuccessful candidates. He obtained 3,863 votes, this number being the highest polled by ths unsuccess- ful candidates. //Mr. Bonuell's engineering works are in Longford-street, and he has for a great number of years earned on a successful business. He was a Wesleyan in religion, and worshipped at Bow- soy-road Chapel. He was also a merr of the Gilbert Greeriall Lodge of Freemason's. On his death becoming known the flags at the Liberal Club, and Town Hall, floated half-mast high. At the Weldeyau Chapel, Bew- sey-road, on Sunday, the Rev. J. Kirtlan made feeling allusi*n to the death ef the deceased, and the organist, Mr. E. 8, Garland, played tho Dead March in " Saul."</p>
        <p>TIIE FUNERAL. F.the funeral took place on Tuesday afternoon, and tb. large attendance 4f; the service iu the Bewsey-road Chapel, and at the Cemcstery were signs of the respect, and esteem the deceased gentleardn Was held. The officiating ministers were the Revs. JKirtlan, Owen Watkins and U. D. Mason. The furPi•al party arrived at Ilewsey-road Chapel about onq.b'clock, and an impressive service was held. Mr. R. S. Garland pre- sided at the organ, and at the close of the service played the Dead March in " Saul." A procession was forined and proceeded to the cemetery. It consisted et the hearse and sixteen carriages containing relatives ot the deceased, members of the Town Council, atfinisters and ofiicers of the Wesleyan body, 'arid a deputation from ' the Gilbert Grecnall Lodge of Freemasons. There was also a number of persons present at the Cemetery. The chief mourners were Mr. Frederick Bonuell and Mr. Jacob Bonnell (sons), Mr. J. C. Muir, A.C.0., Mr. Luker and Mr. Bebbington (sons-in-law). The Corporation were represented by Alderman Francomb (clepisty- mayor), Aldermen Ashton, Sutton' Blackhurst, Harrison and Wright; Councillors Beaman, Hutchinson, Dr. Can-. nell, T. Flood, J. Garner, J. S. Green, R. Heaton, J. Hill, H. J. Longton, F. W. Monks, J. Muir, G. Plumpton, H. Roberts, W. J. Shaw' J. Shiilcock, W. P. Skelton, J. Smethurst, and J. Wilkinson ; Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough trea- surer), Mr. Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr. Ralph Norman (sanitary inspector), Mr. F. Taylor (Gas Works). The following representatives were present from the chapel :—Mr. S. J. Davey and Mr. W. Winstanlev (society stewards), Messrs. A. H. Dunn, John Bennett, (chapel stewards). There were also present, E. Place, J. W. Hayward, J.P., and llsaac :Hayward Mr. Clayton (secretary to the Liberal Association), and Mr. Mitchell (manager of the Liberal Club) ; Mr. S. Hunt (chief con- , stable), Mr. G. F. F. White (deputy magistrates clerk), Messrs. E. Rose, S. Barlow, A. jolley, ,W. Dig,gle, J. Boardman, S. Johnson, T. Skelland, and a deputation from the Freemasons (Gilbert Greenall Lodge). The service at the graveside was very brief and was con- ducted by the Rev. J. kirtlan. A considerable number of beautiful wreaths had been sent. The coffin was of polished oak with brass mountings, and bore the follow- ing inscription: "John Bonnell, died February 10, 1894-, aged 65 years." Mr. Mounfield efficiently carried out the funeral arrangements.</p>
        <p>" WHEER'S PARADISE?"</p>
        <p>Yo ax inc " wheer is Paradise ?" Wheer ev'rythin's so snug an' nice, Well, aw con tell yo in a trice, I' Patricroft Theer, if ther's summut yo're to get, Fro' lodgin's to a heause to let, It's ready to yer bond, yo bet, I' Patricroft! Tiler's Parsons—plenty on um, too- 0' ev'ry shade o' thowt an' view; Thay even gie Os,vd Nick his due, I' Patricroft ! If pain shud fotch yo to yer knees, Ther's Doctors takken th best degrees, That's to be winniu' fees, Patricroft ! Ther's Ceauncillors i'th' district neaw, Sum honsum an' sum not quite few— But rale hard- workin' chaps, 611uz heaw, • I! Pat ricroft</p>
        <p>An' so good-nater't, that a mon May wheel a liondcart reet upon The foote paths—noan wad say" Begone 1" Patricroft th' Green Lone, wheer to strowl an' think, \\rot is it that Teetot'lers drink, Till in a foote-deep hole yo sink, I' Patricroft !</p>
        <p>Then, when yo're addin' to yer sins, Sum anxious mother, eaut wi' twins, Perambilates agen yer shins,</p>
        <p>Patricroft Thees:footebaw aw:the winter reaund, Close to a Recreation Greaund, Wheer limbs get fettl't wen too seaund. Patricroft Ther's trams on ey'ry workin' day, Run summut further than hauf way To th' journey's eend, on strict full pay, I' Patricroft But after aw, aw've lots o' friends, That very amply mak' amends Per aw the cares the divil sends, Patricroft</p>
        <p>So wheer yo are, vole hey a cross, It may be "Love," it may be " Loss " ; But things met be a greyt seet woss, I' Patrieroft !</p>
        <p>An' here aw tell yo, streyt an' free, That, if its allus lett to me, Aw'll be content to live an' dee, I' Patricroft TUM. 0' PEGS lose Bonk, 16, 2, 94.</p>
