      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"THE FAITH Of 'HERETIC."</head>
        <p>"THE FAITH Of 'HERETIC."</p>
        <p>PAPER BY TB.E REV. F. K. FREESTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. K. Freeston, ot London, and formerly of Warrington, gave a paper on the above subject on Tuesday night, at the weekly meeting of the War- rington Debating Sooiety held in the Masbuic Rooms, Bold-t. There was a capital attendance awl the chair was occupied by Mr. F. Monk', J.P., C.C. The Chairman said that he was sure tiara they were all very glad to welcome Mr. Freeston amongst them again even for a short visit. With reference to the subject for discussion he thought it to be the duty of the chairman not to talk, but simply to see fair, play between both sides. He would therefore take that course that night, although he personally was a very great heretic in many respects. (Laughter.) The Rev. F. K. Vreeston, who was received with ap- plause, said that he had been asked to open the custom- ary weekly debate in connection with the Warringtoe Debating Society. The invitation came about in the fol- lowine, way. He- happened, quite by chance, to be in Warrington en the first day of the present year, helping . to open the Ship Canal (laughter), on board Mr. Monks' barge the Helvetia. On board that boat, he was waylaid by the secretaries of the society, and in making inquiries about the society, as a former member, he was extremely sorry to learn that it had not been well supported and was going wrong. He hoped that the secretaries would regard the meeting that evening as a sufficient refutation of that melancholy mistake. The secretaries knew their business better than he did, anclehey did not like him &amp;rig away without asking hint to some and help the society by giving a paper Borne evening. Be sent down several subjects such as "The religion of a literary man, by Mr. Le " The Sunday Question," "The Chicago parliament of religions," and, as an after thought, "The faith of a heretic." He did not think that this subject would be chosen and he was quite sur- prised when it was. However, he was not sorry that this subject was to engage their thoughts that night. He thought that the society had two claims upon the inha- bitants of Warrington; firstly it was entirely representa- tive in the sense of being unrepresentative of any parti- cular religious denomination or political organisation, • and secondly it did not back the two topics of politics and theology as so many debating societies did. The subject for dleaussion that night was mainly theological. If tney turned to any of the religious newspapers, he thought they would all admit that to-day if not at all times there was heresy in the air. It was all about them. It was in the pulpit and in the pew. It was in the church and in the chapel. Some of it was confessed, more he believed was hidden. It was in the bible-class, in the Sunday school, in the newspaper, and in the magazine. He would not be surprised to find it lurking in that rosin that night. (Laughter.) The quiestion was therefore forced upon them as to what was a more perfect definition of heresy. What was this thing which was hunted down, branded and spoken of disparagingly? This thing which, if a man was found to be guilty of he , was thereby lowered in the estimation of most Christian , men and women. Instead of taking a theologioal answer, he would take a technical answer which would be more or lees inapersoiaal. In a dictionary that morning, he found that the definition of heresy was "the taking and holding an opinion contrary to the usual belief." Of course it had a special meaning in theology. But let them look at the terms of the definition. They found that there might be heresy in the realms of literature, of science, of politics, and of economies. If anyone held an opinion contrary to the usual opinion in these sub- jects, he was guilty of being a heretic. The term " heresy " therefore in the abstract mssaat the forming of one's own opinion apart from the rest. Is applied to theology, heresy was usually named heterodoxy. If, heterodoxy meant holding a belief contrary to the usual belief, what then was the usual belief ? The usual belief was called orthodoxy, which he took to be, the belief usual to a particular denomination. Each denomination and each movement, would have its own particular orthodox form, and as a consequence would call all those who did not accept its particular form of belief, heterodox. Let them look at the interplay of those two 'things, orthodox and heterodoxy. Let them go back to the early days of the Hebrew people, when religion consisted in offering sacrifices, and holding fast and feast days. That was their orthodoxy, but there arose the prophets who denounced this orthodoxy and who said that " The ford doth not want either your feasts or your sacrifices, but a clean heart." The prophetic. fire smouldered down in the course of centueies, and the priestly law became the orthodoxy of the days of Jesus of Nazereth, and was represented by the scribes and pharisees. Jesus of Nazereth then arose and said that this was riot pleasing in the sight of God. ltd required men to worship Him in spirit: ,:rIC.T. I tbl 14q go a little further. At the beginning of t!1. Clltidtian Church, they found the members clinging to ths Jewish forms and rites. A man Paul arose, and attacked this .orth6doxy and said that God required something higher and better, and after a time, the Christian church took his view. After the apostolic age, the church slowly stiffened and acquired the idea of the infallible authority of the church, and said that if a man did not accept this doctrine' tie was guilty of heresy and tivit be• pubisbea. There then arose Martin Luther, who said that he did not believe in the infallibility el the church, with the result that Protestautism was founded which rejected the infallible church, and ttok in its place, the infallibility of the bible. The Church of England also adopted the prayer book. This church attempted by the Act of Uniformity to establish a uniform religion, in order to stamp omit heresy for ever, bet in the year 1662, 2,OCD or moretlergymen preferrsd to be ejected, and thus the Nonconformist heretics were founded. From this sketch he felt himself entitled to three conclusions, firstly, what was heresy one day, became orthodoxy of to-morrow. secondly, as soon as heresy became or- thodoxy,it turned roand and persecuted the next heresy, forgettul of its own heretical days. Thirdly, that here- tics were men to be admired. They were men who had not taker' their doctrines to order, but had invettigated themselves. They were men who died far their opinions. Heresy was generally new truth, or at any rate new growth. It was very easy to he orthodox—to accept opinions as they were given, but to be heterodox it re- quired courage, even in the present day. In conclusion he thought it. was a mistake to believe that the usual be- lief was tne right belief, because it was diffieult- to find which was the usual belief, there were so many sects, and heterodoxy was inevitably so long as there was orthodoxy. Mr. Hall said that Mr. Freeston had scarcely taken the course he had expected him to take, and with nine tenths of the paper he agreed. But there was a small resideurn left. He thought he was illogical in saying that heresy was in the air. It would have been a more logical conclusion to hold that heresy was in the fashion. (Laughter.) It was now-a-days very much more diffi- cult to profess orthodox opinions, than to profess what was usually termed heresy. If he wished to be scorned and laughed at, he had simply to be orthodox. Heresy had been persecuted, but it Was not now, and he be- lieved never would be again. As to the statement that heresy was the truth of to-morrow,they must remember the number of heresies which died out and were for- gotten. Heresy would not become troth if it were not truth at first. It looked too much like glorify is, the success of the successful ones. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton thought that the faith of a heretic should have been brought mere clearly before thPlll. AS it was, there was very little to criticise. With much of the paper he agreed. Speaking from a churchman's point of view,he thought that the apostolic creed was broad enough for any one. It was drawn up in the distant ages before any of the sects were in ex- istence, and was, therefore, not aimed at any of them, and he considered that their vitality was it proof of their truth. The apostolic creed was the creed of the church universal. The Rev. W. H. Drummond said that lie did not think Mr. Freeston intended to give the creed of a heretic. The title of his paper was the faith of a heretic. at his (the speaker's) church they had no test of membership, and when a person signed the roll of membership, it would be the last thing in the world to ask what his be- lief was. I\lr. Hall had spoken of heresy being the fashion, but at army rate there were a great many peo- ple, who did not Hine to be associated beretia. They were now discussing the question of heresy, and he thought that Mr. Freeston was wise in not bringing in , his own particular belief. Thought should be growing, and stationary thought was dead thought. They must, therefore, be prepared for changes in the religions thought of the time. Mr. Westbrook also agreed with very much of Mr. Freeston's paper, but it was no claim to him to come to Mr. Drummond's chapel because there was no creed. He preferred to have one. He preferred to think by rule,but this did not prevent him from using .his private judg- ment. He did not like Mr. Freeston's reference to the Catholic Church. He (the speaker) was a Catholic, but he was not a Romen Catholic. He did not believe in the infallibility of the church, but he did of the bible. He preferred to believe of the apostolic of! the early church, and also the fathers. Mr. John Gaudy said that Mr. Freeston opened his essay by saying that heresy was in the air. He (the speaker) maintained thatehere was much heresy among the ministers of the present day. Fault had been found with the fact that there was no definite point to attack in the paper. Well lie would give them one if they liked One of the orthodox doctrines was the doctrine of eter- nal punishment. He did not believe in that at all. Much mischief had been caused and very groat misery to child- ren by the thought of this. The faith he believed was the raith of freedom of thought which be thought would be the greatest blessing to the world. Mr. A. Birch referring to Mr. Carletou'is speech said that in the Church of England there was the most diverse opinions. There were high church, low church, and broad church' from Bishop Colenoo and away to wher% they thouelitfit. Most people believed what they were . brought up to. He believed in people being free to form 'their own opinions. Mr. Horton said that there was a great deal said of freedom by Mr. Drummond, hut suppose he were to preach at his chapel the doctrine of the Trinity what would his congregation do? (Laughter.) With regard to creedS,he considered them neceasary,for the majority of mankind had no time to think of themselves in these I matters, and a guide was therefore neeessexy for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>INQUESTI</head>
        <p>THE INQUESTI</p>
        <p>Considerable sensation WaB aillied in Warrington on Monday morning,wlin it was learned that Mr.. W. Marsh, cabinet maker, in Bridge-street, had put an end to his life by hanging himself. He had got up on Monday morning to light the fire, at about 6.20, and when about an hour afterwards his wife came downstairs,she was horrified to find him hang- ing by a rope from the ceiling. The body was cut down and Dr. White tailed in, but life was found to be extinct. The deceased had been in a somewhat despondent condition for some time, and this had doubtless tended to affect his mind. The deceased gentleman was a member of the Town Council from 1883 to 1890, sitting for the old South East Ward, and was a member of the Watch, Street Improve- ment, Paving and Sewerage, and Fire Engine Com- mittees. He was a Conservative and a Churchman. The inquest on the body of the deceased was held in the Parade room of the police station, on Tuesday, by Mr. Brighouse. The foreman of the jury was Mr. T. Moorestand Mr. A. E. White,represented the widow who 'was also present. Mrs. Marsh on being called,said that her husband was a cabinet maker, living at 1, Mersey-st-. He was 54 years of age, and she last saw him alive on Monday Amorning when he left the bedroom. No one was in the diouse but her husband and herself. When she went 'downstairs she found him hanging from the ceiling. She went out for the police, and her husband was cut down. r She had noticed nothing peculiar with him when he went 'downstairs, nor had she suspected that he intended to take away his life.—Cross-examined by Mr. White : Her husband had been depressed in spirits lately. The lease of the shop expired last year, and the Corporation had bought it to widen the street. He worried himself very inuch in connection with the posSibility of the Corpora- tion turning him out at short notice, and of his having kis stock on hand. During last summer he took the lease of a dwelling-house in Latchfiard. There was con- siderable expense entailedsthereby, in fact more than he expected. He had also complained of loss sustained owing to the smallpox epidemic in the town, and also from the coal strike. His business was principally a credit one so that he would feel particularly the bad state of trade.—ln reply to further questions by the coroner, the witness said that although depressed, he had never shown any disposition to make away with himself. He had not been cheerful. Constable Monks said that he was called into the house of deceased on Monday morning by Mrs. Marsh, and found deceased hanging in the scullery with his back to the door and his feet a short distance from the ground. There was a step ladder near which iike had not kicked away. When cut down, the body was warin,and Dr. White was immediately sent for but he pronounced life extinct.</p>
        <p>The Coroner in summing up said that he was very much obliged to Mr. White for his assistance. The jury would have to find whether, if deceased did take away his life, he was in a right state of mind or not. The deceased appeared to have been depressed in mind al- though he did not show it very much outwardly. A great many men did not show outwardly what they felt inwardly. The jury returned a verdict of suicide during tempor- ary insanity, and expressed their sympathy with the widow of the deceased. Mr.Brighouse asked Mr. White to convey not only the sympathy of the jury, but also his own to Mrs. Marsh. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The remains of the deceased were interred at the War- rington Cemetery on Thursday afternoon. The ortege left the house in Bridge-st., shortly before one o'clock. On the route to the cemetery large numbers of persons lined the footpaths of the principal streets, and many of the shopkeepers had their blinds lowered. On arrival at the cemetery the coffin was taken into the chapel, where a short service was conducted by the Rev. H. V. Pigot, vicar of St. Paul's, after which the body was lowered into its last resting place. The chief mourners present were—Mrs. Marsh (widow of the deceased), Messrs. James and Frederick Marsh (brothers), Mrs. Barker (sister-in-law), Mr. Kean (brother-in.law), and Messrs. Robert and James Marsh. There was a large number present at the graveside, amongst whom were Alderman Francornb, Alderman Sutton, Councillor Dr. Cannel', Councillor Hutchinson, and Mr. Longdin (bor- ough surveyor). The bearers were Messrs. C. Russell, A. Clark, J. Elitchmough, and G. Eaton. A large num- ber of beautiful wreaths covered the coffin. Messrs. S. g. and R. Jolans,)n, Bridge-a., were the undertakers. THE WARRINGTON FLOR&amp;L AND lIORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The second ineetin,9: of the above Society was held in the Town Hall on Monday evening. There was a good attendance and the chair was occupied by Mr, W. Skelton who, after a few introductory re- tharks, announced that Mr. J. W. 11a3,ward, J.P., had promised a special prin in the cottagers' class, for window plants. (Applause.)—Mr. James Itya,n, the secretary, said that the Committee horeq ca goon as possible to publish a schedule to place before the public giving the names of the officers, and the rules, tsc., of the Society. He hoped the members would do their ut- most to forward the Society by getting as many new members as possible as the subscription fee was only 2s. 6d. perannurn. They had already two sl)eciß.l prizes offered, and he hoped that they would get a; good num- ber more.—Mr. Sutton asked if the society was formed simply to get up shows and give prizes or whether it was also intended for the members to give a.ssistence to each other. If this also was intended, he was -sure that a good number would join because it would,ht an induce- ment to do so.—The Chairman replied thhat had been his idea of the Society. He hoped thatlthe members would communicate their knowledge oresb'another, and also that the professional gardeners would give some as- sistance to the amateurs.—Mr. faltoncSmith said that at present the Committee were feeling their way, but they were prepared to give every iraormation in their power to .the members. (Hear, hear.)--Tice Secretary inti- mated that it was their intention as time went on to get a number of the leading books ,on gardening, and use them as a library.—This concluded the buFiness of the meeting, and a vote of thanks to the Chairman was passed on the motion of Mr. ellandalls, seconded by Mr. Sutton. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CAN PEOPLE SEE THE INVISIBLE?</head>
        <p>CAN PEOPLE SEE THE INVISIBLE?</p>
        <p>I was says a writer in " Cassell's Saturday Journal," a witness recently of a most extraordinary experiment. In this instance the subject was a lady of marked sensi- tive temperament. The wilier was a gentleman of rac- ing proclivities. He had conceived an idea that it might be possible to turn this power to a practical issue. Tak- ing a racing programme for a certain meeting, he put the lady into a species of trance, and represented to her that she was in the grand stand at the races in questicn. In a few moments she was all alert, and began, to des- cribe the whole scene in a vivid manner. Now they are at the starting post !" Bow many horses are there P" said my friend. "Three, four—no, five ! Now they are off! No, its a false start ;" Then a pause. "Now the flag has fallen! Oh, what a dust they make ! I can't see. Now one horse is leading ! Oh, it's a long way from the rest! No (following the imaginary race with her eyes from one side of the room to the other), now the second horse is catching him up I Ah, now they have passed the post !" 64 Which horse has won ?" asks my friend " I can't quite see; it's all so dim ; they are making such ,a dust." Then she began to spell out the name of a horse. My friend read out the pro- bable starters. Suddenly she hit upon one, and nothing would move her from the fact that this was the winner. The whole affair was so uncanny. that my friend was sanguine enough to believe he had hit upon a short cut to success on the turf, especially as the horses she gave were nearly all favourites. The result was that at, the end of the day he found that not a single horse she bad named had been successful ! But now comes the most extraordinary part of the business. In the evening of the same day he took a race-card with the winners all marked in pencil. Again he took her in fancy to the race-course ; again she minutely described the episodes of the various events. But this time, strangely enough, not only did she name the exact number of starters, but she also gave every winner correctly, without a single exception !—From" Cassell's Saturday Journal " for February.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>COAL TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Coal Trade Board of Conciliation was held on Wednesday at Westminster. Mr. A. M. Lhambers (Thorncliffe) presided. Letters were read intimating that the Speaker had nominated Lord Shand as chairman of the Conciliation Board, and that his Lordship had accepted Me office. A resolution was ad3pted thanking the Speaker and cordially accepting Lord Shand as the chairman. A vote of sympathy was passcd to the family of Mr. John Knowles. of Manchester, a member of the Board whose death occurred recently. The next meeting of the Board will be held at a date to suit Lord Shand's convenience after the first week in March. The Press Association says the miners will ask for the chairman's decision on three points—a minimum wage, a minimum i selling price for coal on long contracts, and, f there is an inquiry into the question of wages, whether he will also include the subject of profits.</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION IND COUGH.—Soreness and dryness, tickling and irritation,inducing cough and affecting the voice For these symptoms use lipps's Glycerine Jujubes. In con- tact with ne glands at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, the Glycerine in these agreeable confections becomes actively healing. Sold only in boxes, 70., tins Is. lid.. labelled " JITtES EPPS &amp; Co.. Ltd., Homeeopathio Chemists, London," Dr. Moore, in his work on" Ness and Throat Diseases," says "The Glyeerine Jujubes prepared by James Epps and Co., are of undoubted service as a cura- tive or palliative agent," white Dr. Gordon Holmes, Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat and Ear Infirmary,writes: "After an extended trial. I have found your Glycerine jujubes of considerable benefit in almost all forms ot throat disease."—.advi.</p>
        <p>THE NATIONAL LIBERAL FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>Many delegates went to Portsmouth on Monday to attend the annual meetings of the National Liberal Federation. In the afternoon the members of the Society of Certificated Liberal Agents were entertained at lun- cheon by Mr. Marjoribanks. The Liberal Whip was unable to attend, being on duty, at the House of Com- mons, but he sent a kindly message, and expressed the hope that the Society would tend to the solidification of Liberal work. The Home Secretary (Mr. Asquith) wrote pointing out to the agents the importance of a thorough knowledge of electoral law. Dr. Spence Watson responding to the toast of" The Liberal cause," said ' the party was never in a better condition than it was at the present time—even their foes were fighting for them. The day for the mere mending of the House of Lords had ; gone by,and they could no longer forget the iniquities of ; that body,even though the peers were repentant sinners. At the meeting held later Mr. Woodings read a paper on objects and scope of the Society of Certificated Liberal ; agents, and a lively discussion followed. On Monday ; night a meeting was held in the Town Hall in favour of ; Welsh Disestablishment. A resolution was carried urg- ing that the question should have a prominent place in , the Liberal programme, and Mr. D. A. Thomas, M.P.,. ; suggested the disestablishment of the Church in Wales, aud then the disestablishment of the House of Lords. The sittings of the annual Conference of the National Liberal Federation commenced on Tuesday at Ports- mouth. About fifteen hundred delegates attended the ' meeting in the Town Hall, and were heartily welcomed by the Mayor, who is a Conservative. Dr. Spence Wat- son presided, and moved the adoption of the annual re- port. The report was adopted. Mr. Arthur Acland, M.P., was called upon by the Chairman to speak, and dwelt at length on the legislative and administrative achievements of the Government during the past Year. With reference to the Lords' amendments to the -Local- Government Bill, he said he thought all Liberals had made up their minds as to their immediate duty. So had the Government. The more important of the amendments the Government called not amendments, but destruction, and their course was clear. They would negative all those amendments. The bill should not be maimed or injured at the dictation of the House of Lords. This statement was received with prolonged cheering, the delegates rising in their places and waving their programmes. Resolutions thanking the Govern- ment fer their work and in favour of Welsh Disestab- lish's-lent were passed. Dr. Spence Watson was again chosen as president, and the Conference adjourned. The Women's Liberal Federation also met at Ports- mouth on Tuesday, and passed, after discussion, resolu- tions condemning the imposition of theological tests on Board School teachers and of restrictions upon women's work, and in favour of poor law reform and the Local Veto Bill.</p>
        <p>The Conference of the National Liberal Federation concluded on Wednesday at Portsmouth. A. resolution was adopted calling for a thorough reform of the regis- tration laws. Another resolution assured the Govern- ment of the support of the Liberal party in any action they might adopt in regard to the House of Lords so as to secure that the House of Commons should be the paramount authority in the State. Mr. W. F. Robson, Q.C.' the mover of this resolution, said they did not hide from themselves that this demand meant a graw constitutional struggle; but it did not mean, as some people thought, a revolution. for with the power of the purse in their hands the Commons could if they would either coerce or extinguish the Peers. Resolutions were also passed reaffirming the Federation's declaration in favour of direct popular control of the liquor traffic, calling for a reform of the land laws, and endorsing other items in the Newcastle programme. At a meeting of the Women's Liberal Federation resolutions were passed expressing the hope that the Government would deal with the question of the Parliamentary enfranchise- ment of women, proceed with a measure for the dises- tablislitnent and disendowment of the Church in Wales, and increase the number of women factory inspectors ; and at a public meeting in connection with the Associa- tion similar resolutions were adopted. and also one condemning the action of the House of LGrds, which, it was declared, threatened to render futile all the labours of a prolonged session.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>AN ECCLESFIELD MIRA_CLE.</head>
        <p>AN ECCLESFIELD MIRA_CLE.</p>
        <p>REVIVED INTEREST IN LIFE.</p>
        <p>From the " Sheffield Weekly Independent."</p>
        <p>The "Sh effield Weekly Independent recently con- tained the account of an interesting interview which a reporter had with Mrs. Hcoton, of lAtercliffe, whose miraculous case created a marked sensation in the neighbourhoo d. The same journal has since been made acquainted with a case even more striking in character, in quite another quarter, and in order to verify the particulars, a representative of the " Independent" journeyed to Ec.elesfield--one of the most ancient of the lla•* ilamshire villages; The ease is that of Mr. T. W. Malli soh, of Ecelesileld, whom the reporter found at home. He is a tall, healthy looking man, of 35, well known and rcSptscted in the village on account of his high reputation for busi- ness integrity. He is a dealer in coal, lime, and stone, and also runs a small colliery, 'occklpying, therefore, a good commercial position in Eccleslield. It appeared that the malad} from which Mr. Mallison suffered was of the sae character as that of Mrs. Flooton. He had enjoyed good health up to five or sin years ago, when he began to be subject to billiena at- tacks. Realising that the proverb " a stitch in time" applies with peculiar force to .matters of health, he lost no time in ca lling in medical aid. Instead, however, of the treatm eat preventing the recurrence of the attacks or modifying them, they became more frequent and severe, and were accompanied by still graver symptoms. A change of doctors .had no perceptible result. Mr. Mall is f n's condition at this time was most deplorable. " S taped to the eyes in business "—as he put it—twice and sometimes thrice a week he found , himself lying helpless on a sick bed, racked with pain, and so ill as to cause the gr avest alarm to his friends. What made matters worse, the attacks were liable to come on without warning.</p>
        <p>The symptoms weretof such a character, Mr. Malli- son said, th at he could not recall them without a shudder. Every attack utterly prostrated him. The vomiting, diarrhcea, and headache were of the most severe type ,while cramp nearly all over his body caused him at times to shriek with pain. Mrs. Mallison added that her husband's appearance during these attacks was so horrible that often she dared not look at him. Every seizure she expected would be his last, and she really believes that if his cure had not been effected when it was he would have died under one of them. His condition between the attacks was most unsatisfactory. He was unable to do much work, was always tired,and sleep failed to refresh. He lapsed into a low, nervous state, became emaciated in body, believed himself to te beyond recovery, and lost all in- terest in life. Altogether four doctors were tried, none of whom were able to afford the slightest relief. In June of last year he decided to discontinue medical attendance. He had drunk bucketskil of medicine to no purpose. Shortly afterwards he noticed in a newspaper the report of a cure effected in an almost hopeless case by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale Poople. and was persuaded to try them. He accordingly bought a box from Barnsley. Befcre he had taken half the quantity he felt a decided improvement, and continuing the medicine he gradually made progress, which resulted in his complete recovery. "I have never felt so well in all my life as I dc now," said Mr. Mallison. " I go to business at six o'clock every morning, and can do a gocd day's work without feeling tired ; I enjoy life, I enjoy my work, and have a good appetite and digestion. I feel so grateful for my restoration to health that I deem it my duty to make known the facts of the case as Widely as possible, for the benefit of the public." It id not alone in cases like the alsave that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have a miraculous efficacy. They are also recognised as one of the greatest modern medicines, and a perfect blood builder and nerve restorer, and an unfailing cure for rheumatism, . sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St,. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, prostration, etc. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restore pale and sallow corn-</p>
        <p>plexions to the glow of health, and are a specific. for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, while in the case of men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry, overwcnit, or excesses of whatever nature.</p>
        <p>The Pills are sold by all chemists, or may be had direct from the Dr. Williams Medicine .COmpany,of 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, England (and of Brockville, Canada), in wooden boxes bearing the trade mark and wrappers at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 435. 9d. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold lcose, and any dealer who offers substitutes in this form is trying to defraud you ; the box must be in a pink wrapper bearing the full nanit.,Dr. Williams's Pink Fills for Pale People.</p>
