      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. ; \f/</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. ; \f/</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons reassembled on Monday Sir E. Grey, replying to fir E. .1. Bartlett, said British ships at Rio now enjoyed the tams facilities as ships of othert nations. The House reenmed consideration of the Lord*, amendments to the &amp;a Fisheries Regulation (Scotland/ Bill, and, the whole of the clauses having been gone - through, a committee was appohated to draw up reasons/ for disagreeing with come of the Lords' amendments. The House of Lords considered the Local Government " Bill on report. The Marla BILBOUNE moved an amend- I. tnent that the /Pariah Connell of a rural parish should be elected from such of the parochial electors of the parish- t as were personal!, rated to the poor rate. The ame,nd- ment was oppooed by the Government, but was carried; by 84 to 42. Aa axendment moved bv the Earl of MORLEY, that all cpp:Aped provisional orders for the cen- ' pulsory acquialtion of land should be referred to a sect committee,te a committAe of both Houses, or to a stand- ing was carried by 108 to 25. THEiDAY. In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Sir W. HARCOORT, replying to a question by Mr. Goschen, said there was no inteation to reopen the Indian mints to the free coinage of silver, or to Muni to the minimum rate for the sale of bills. It was not intended to impose a tax on the importation of silver, and no change in the preaent policy of tba Indian Government was in con templation.</p>
        <p>The House considarad the Lords' amendments te the Employers' Liability Bill. On Lord Dudley's amendment 4o clause 4 allowing contracting out, Mr. COBB moved an amendment to insert the words "until the expiration_ of three years after the passing of this Act," his object, he said, 'being to suspend the operation of the Act for three years with regard to remit that were at present in existence. Mr. Asaurru, while asserting the adherenee of the Government to the vital principle of the bill, said the Government were dispoced to advise the House bo accept Mr. Cobb's amendment. Mr. Balfour declared his intention to "resist to the utmost the present illusory pretence at the compromise.' Mr. W. M`Laren opposed the amendment. Mr. S. WOODS expressed regret that an attempt shouici be made to shelve the whole question for three years. After Mr. Chamberlain had spoken in opposition to the amsndraent, Mr. Burs invited the Gcrernment to out tihe three years limit down to two years, or ereu to one year. On a division the numbers were—for Mr. Cobb's amendment, 215; against, 213; majority, 2. Mr. AsQurria moved the omrnission of the Lord's amendment in sub-saction 3 which enacts that the Act shall not apply to any costracting-out agreement - made after the panalfr, of the Act, and the motion was carried by 219 to 17. The clause? as amended was then agreed to, and the House adjourned.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, co the :notion to read the Local Government (Par:sh Councils) Bill a third time, the Earl of Wimtvss moved that tho bill be recommitted for the further consideration cf the poor-law clauses. The Earl of Krunzurar opposod- the motlon. The Marquis of SALISBURY thought the proposal of Lord WEIIYSS would lead to no prsctical rtsult, but he made a final protest against the changea which tria bill would effect. Several. amendments Wer3 Won oersiclared and agreed to, and the bill was read a third tire arid passml. The Earl of KIXBERLEy annousead that the Commons' amendments to the Employers' Liakility LIB would be considered on Monday next,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TRCZSI)4Y,</head>
        <p>TRCZSI)4Y,</p>
        <p>In ihe Hate of Commoes ca Thursday ivening Mr. Burrow, questioned respecting reports of the shooting of. prisoners in Matabelelmil, said they were anonymous and as such not entitled to much Weight, while, more- over, signed statements bad been published denying the • truth of the reports. Mr. KturenDanr,L-litfttt;Trz and Mr. W. D. Smint questioned the. risme Seeeetary about processions of •‘ tine.anployed " in London under the leadership of a: man named Williats, and asked whether this mam ! was to be permitted to be at large. Mr. Ascltnra said no doubt Willie:me had 03 several occasions used lahguage such as would render him amenable to the 'criminal latv,, but he was advised by police officers whose knowledge and experience was second to none in in the world that the prosecution of Williams would do • more harm than'good. The Muse proceedsd to amsider the Lords' amend— ments to the Local Government Bill. On the amend- meet to raise the limit of population fer compulsory parish couneils.from 200 to 500, Mr. FOWLER said he had no hesitation in askiag the Eouse to disagree with it. Mr. COLLINS said he should support the Government.. The Lords amendment was rejected by 260 to 191. On the next o the Lards amendments—that providing that no one should be eligible for election to a parish council' who was wit personally rated to the poor rate—Mr. FOWLER said it struck at the very root of the bill, and he moved that the House disagree with tlac amendment. Mr. Balfour said be did not think it worth while to divide the House on this question"and the amendment was rejected •ananimotisly. The next amendment, de- claring that a parish councillor must reside in his parish was defeated by 283 to 167. The amendments constitu- ting district.counciliors: ezviteio members of the parish council and placing restrictions on the use of school- rooms were disagreed with without a division. The House adjourned shortly after midnight.</p>
        <p>There was a brief sitting of the House of Lords, at which the Commons reasons for disagreeing with some of their Lordships' amendments to the Employers' Lia- bility Bill were brought up and laid on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ALLEGED WIFE ASSAULT BY A ST. HELENS ELECTRICIAN.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED WIFE ASSAULT BY A ST. HELENS ELECTRICIAN.</p>
        <p>t St. Helens en Monday, Joseph Slavin, an elec- trician, of Gladstone-st., and Sr. Helens Market, was summoned for assaulting his wife (Elizabeth Slavin). Mr. J. 0. Swift defended. Complainant said her husband got iato a passion on Saturday. He gripped her by the throat, and threatened to choke her. She got away from him, and ran into the back- yard. He then threw some water over her and drenched' tier. He fastened the door, and would not let her in not- give her any clothing. She went to a neighbour's house, and had not seen him since.—By Mr. Swift: She had a deal to contend with when he took those moods. She did not have any moods. She bad been married thirty- five years.—Mr. Swift : Are you ntit. willing to gO home and start afresh for another thirty-five years ?—Witness No, I cannot. He won't be comfortable with himself. There is nothing in the wide world to make him uncom.- fortable except his temper.—Mr.kSwift : He says he will not throw water on you any more, and will try and make you comfortable. -Won't you go back P—Witness: • I don't know. It is not the water I complain about. He gets himself into a viclent passion, and if I don't speak he says I aru,ducub.—Defenuant :She is lobouring under a mistake. • She wants to be the missus (laugh- ter).—The case wig adjourned for a month to allow the parties to come to terms, but Mrs. Slavin remarked that she would never go back.</p>
        <p>NEW MINES AND MINES ABANDONED.</p>
        <p>Daring the month of January it is reported (says the "Labour Gazette ") that 32 mines, including 23 collieries, were opened or re-spened, onthree less than in Decem- ber; and that 32 mines, including 18 collieries, were die.. continued or abandoned, or 15 more than in December. Of the new collieries seven are in Lancashire, four in Glam)rganshire, three each in Yorkshire and Glouces-. tershire, and two in fireconshire. The new mines in, Lancashire are Higher House, Rivington ; Deplay Bacup ; Wirkenhall (three-quarter yard seam). New Hey;. Salmonfield (little seam), Reyton ; 'troches Clough (little seam), Shaw ; Losteelc, Bolton (sinking shaft) ; and Barn- furlong (two-feet seam).</p>
