      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BOILING WATER OR MILE</head>
        <p>BOILING WATER OR MILE</p>
        <p>THE speech whicT Sir WlL=atit in Effective HAROOURT deltifered at Ports- mouth on Wednesday, in connee- fon with the conference of the Ifational Liberal Federation at that town, will taka rank as one of his finest efforts. It is full of rod things. Ate usual Mr. CHAMBERTAIN comes in for some of the keenest thrusts. The autocrat of tirmingham is described as "a gentleman who only eight years ago was a spick-and-span Radical of the very new- est type, a gentleman with his poclieta full of unauthorised programmes and the apostle of the ransom school," who now endeavours to cover his desertion of his party by pretending that the party had altered its creed. And then with what felicitous phrasing does Sir WILLIAM ridicule Mr. CIIAMBERLAIN'S'SpaSMOdiC* attempts to galvanise a " National " party into life. All these efforts are described as due to the whilom Radical's reluctance to finally don the Tory uniform. He wants, therefore, to invinst a new uniform, which shall neither be quite blue nor quite yellow. As Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT points out, this has been from time immemorial the last reso-arce of politicians out in the cold who, enjoy the confid- ence of neither party. It was a favourite dream of Lord BOLINGICROOICE, who was a great genius, and it has been the dream of many inferior men since who will make nothing of it. In fact—and this is a rub which Mr. CHAISMBRLAIN will no doubt appreciate—it has always been found that parties invented to suit the personal convenience of politicians out-at-elbows turn out miserable failures. Sir WILLIAM' HARCOURT pokes fun, too at Mr. BALFOUR'S rather juvenile device of praising the old Radicals at the expense of the new. Ho points out that the adjective " new " is appro- priate to the party of progress. "Progress io always new." Their opponents might just co well address the reproach to an advancing and 7ic- torious force, that bscause the ground that, it camps on to-day is difiiirent from that upon whick it bivouacked yesterday it is a new army. " Tory- ism," said Sir WILLIAM; "is nevu-r new. always antiquated. Its- history is monstrous. It is always the same, it always resists, and in tho long run it is always beaten." This trenchant criticism of Toryism and defence of Radicalism, whether it be new or oldraltogether demolish XI% BALFOUR'S superfine subtleties and nice distinc- tions. The comparision of Lord &amp;laminar to FRONT DR BOEUF is appropriate enough under pro" sent circumstances. The insolent tone and style of his recent references to trades unionists and agricultural labourers might seem suitable enougb in the Case of a feudal baron addressing bio Saxon churls but are altogether out of place in thcoo times when the ultimate power of the State rests very largely in the hands of these two classes.</p>
        <p>THE small majority of two by which A New Mr. COBBS' amendment to the Vl' Issue. ployers' Liability Bill was carried ill the House of Commons on Tuesday evening will? we may confidently anticipate, end all attempts at . compromise with the House of Lords in regard to the mutilations of the Urovernment Bills.. Tho voting on that occasion indicated that Liberals in the House of Commons, like those in the count* have fully realised that they are now face to face with a new issue of vast importance. it 15 ne longer a mere question of "give and take" between the two parties who have the making of English laws between them—the usual " toneing down," to speak, of democratic provisions to allay the fear of timid reactionaries, while substantially sale—,' guarding the main objects-of .the measures under discussion. The resistance which the Peers have' always giveri to progressive legislation has, duria.: ' the present Parliament, assumed a more aggressive and dangerous form. They are no longer centeo with the usual eliminations-and qualifications; the/ have begun to assail the vital principles of tb° legislation which is demanded and voted for by the electorate of the country and which has received the ; sanction of the House of Gommons. "Are self-governing people under a Liberal Go vern-: merit ?" asked Mr. ACLANa in his fine speech °" the Liberal Federation Conference on Tuesdaili evening, and the vehement cries of "No " wit which he was answered, bespoke the indignatiee-:, that is felt in all parts of the country. The isse°, that has now been raised is whether the legislati°°': Chamber which is elected by the votes of °dal' people, or that Chamber which is filled with Lc° SALISBURY'S hereditary items is to have the Pre; vailing force in the constitution. It is, rr have said, an issue of vast importance, but it eq; not he shirked,. and,, fortunatly, there seells be no disposition to shirk it. After Mr. STONE no one could speak with gree authority of the intentions of the Gocer went than Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT, 911 upon this point Sir WILLIAM pronounced absolute clearness on Wednesday. As , pointed out, Lord SALISBURY had thrown dclro the gage.. The quarrel was not of the a°ve ment's seeking, but, he adde, the Governraelo: would not shrink from the contest. Thb scene of enthusiasm which followed the eniPL.",,,, announcement, could not fail to impress uP011;',..: mind of the CHA.NCELLOR of the ExeramQvnlt. he he and his colleagues in the Cabinet were rig, interpreting the temper of the Party of they are the leaders. 10-</p>
        <p>THE plea that was most -if Work and mi Wages. nently put forward oal be" the ta:l of the coal-owners during cent lock-out was that they we compelled to duce wages in order to lessen the cost of Pr°dll,s tion. But, if there be anything in the arguraell'.l: and .let it be added, the hard facts, of a writer l fre the " Edinburgh Review," the coal-owners sc.r the victims of a fallacy. According to this scilted neither, low pay nor long hours cheapen labOirl'„,ll.,, it Is a mistake to assume that English rflan°37-ri turers are handicapped in their rivalry with fort-4 competitors by the lower wage and longor "111'3 of labour of foreign workmen. What constittit°</p>
        <p>,1</p>
        <p>- I 4 .1 1</p>
        <p>ekeap labour ? That is the question Which the writer deals with, and the Answer he arrives at by a comprehensive intrvey of the " hard facts" which are adduced ity reliable and painstaking investigators is that elleap labour is highly paid, well educated and akort-houred labour. This is a knock-down blow UT the familiar bogey of continental coin petition We are told that Dngland has nothing to fear kora the long hours and low wages of foreign workmen. Paradoxical as it may seem, we are usarsd that these are really a protection rather lhott menace to our industrial supremacy. What xi to be feared is a shortening of the hours and a ?tieing of the wages with a consequent enhance- 'tent of the efficiency and quality of the French *ad German workmen. "Experience in all parts !I( the civilised world swells the chorus of agree- "tent in the statement that shortened hours have !Ignited in greater production, and consequently burs been to the gain of the employer as well as of Vaa employed" This is the comfortable conclusion "'rich is come to by the writer in the "Edinburgh Itinview " and it seems to be warranted Isy the Itperiences of all induitrial nations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE TEA PARTY. no popular tea party in connection with Runcorn Go-operative Society will be held in Runcorn Public Hall en Wednesday evening next. Particulars are adver- tised. AMBRIDGE-STREET, RUNCORN, GOSPEL MISSION. At the Bible and discussion class in connection with tile above mission, held on Sunday evening, under the presidency of Mr. George Marshdl, a paper was read by Ur. David Bisbrown on" Divine Providence." SOLLICER'S use over ten tens of Floo7every</p>
        <p>ALBERT-ROAD CKAPEL, WIDNES. ea Thursday evening a concert was given in the Schoolroom connected with the above chapel by the Wid- *ea contingent of the Crystal Palace choir. The various letections were rendered in an admirable manner, under tire leadership of Mr. J. Sephton. Joeirra PRITCHARD, 51, High-street. Runcorn, Imps ihiagrabt.zs to suit all sights, from a shilling a pair.—diett. BitIONSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVIMENT CLASS, RUNCOEN. At the weekly meeting of this society, held on Wed- Iseeday evening, at the Brunswick Wesley School, under the presidency of Mr. T. Simister, a paper was read by r. James L. Pali, on " The novels of Jane Austen." There was a good attendance. The paper was one of high literary merits. PRIM IRisH BUTTE.B. is. per lb., at COLLIEICO.—Adet. WIDNES PARISH CHURCH FOOTBALL CLUB. On Thursday evening, a concert was given in the St. Paula Parochial Room in aid of th: above club. In the absence of Mr. Stuart Deacon, who had been unexpect- edly called to London, the chair was occupied by the 'Vicar (Rev. W. Hodgson). The programme had bean arranged by Mr. A. J. Paton, and was well sustained. lINXICIOTS CRISP CRUST round (34n.mfba's kread.—Adat.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. Another of the Pleasant Sunday Evening services in connection with this church to.i,k place on Bunday,when there was a large congregation. The Rev. J. Simpson gave a short address entitled " What Christianity has done for the workingman." Miss Fleatherington gave a recline interpretation of "An Evaning Hymn," and Mr. Worrall was very successful in his rendering of "Thy Will be done." The choir sang the anthem, " I will lift IV mine eyes." Mr. C. C. Le Couteur presided at the *man. Oyer 6,00 e delicious leaves sold every week at CoLtimee. —diet.</p>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE, RUNCORN. At the usual meeting ot the above society, on Satur- day, under the presidency of Mr. George L. Sutton, Mr. Daniel Bisbrown gave a report of the proceedings at the Qrand Division, recently held in Runcorn. Fie expressed himself disappointed that the order had not made more progress in the district numerically,but in Great Britain they had done well, there being a considerable increase irt the membership. There was no intention to slacken their efforts, but to push en their propaganda. Much regret was expressed that Mr. Thomas Cooke had given lep the position of secretary for the district, having held at responsible office for 17 years—There was an inter- dating discussion, in which the following officers and Atetnbers took part :—Messrs. Samuel Henderson, kldward Key, Thomas J. Holt, S. M. Temperlay, and Thomas Cooke. DYzarratsci is sold for smallest profit at COLLIER'S ,92,6treen- War-rd..lialtan-rd.,Egertcon-st. , Bride -st.6,Churah-st.—.divt. SAD SUICIDE OF A FARMER NEAR WIDNES. _ .</p>
        <p>A distressing case of suicide has occurred at Ditton. A fanner named John Edwardson, of Hough Green-lane, piton, fifty-five ycars of age, drowned himself on Friday in a pond of water in a field acljAnin.g his farm. Since the end of November last the deceased had been in a 'el"' desponding mind, and was under the treatment of Dr. O'Neil, of Farnworth, and Dr. Starkey Smith, of Warrington. Serious symptoms were observable, but lately they took a turn. On Thursday night the deceased retired to bed, sleeping with a man-servant named Alfred Lyons. The deceased's son locked the bedroom door, but on Friday morning about six o'clock the door was found open and the man Lyons was asleep by himself in bpd. Search was made for the missing man, and even- tslly his clothes were found near the pond of water in the field. Later in the .day his body was recovered from the water and conveyed to the farm.—An inquest was lat.td by Mr. Brighouse, on Tuesday evening and a ver- tint of "Suicide while temporarily insane" was tett:trued.</p>
        <p>IPLINDID NEW CURRANTS 2d. per lb. at COLLIBI4.-4ist. TELE LNIGIT ESTATES OF WIDNBS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justic-e-iCioTiE,-in the Chancery Division, London. ett Saturday, had before him a petition under the Leigh Estates Act for the sanction of the court to the sale of a Piece of land at Widnes, another at Liverpool, for tke Purchase of one piece of land adjoining the Luton 800 !setate, and for the exchange of another.—Mr. Methold, 111 support of the petition, said the piece of land at Wit- was a little less than an acre in extent, and for that tbey had been offered £7OO. There was evidence to show that that was a fair price. The land at Liverpool elinsisted of 1334 square yards, and for that they had been offered £2,000. The land had not been occunied for seven years, and there were some dilapidated build- Inge upon it which were fit for nothing, and would have ' to be pulled down. As regards the land it was deeired . to purchase, the chief clerk had authorised them to bid Q 490 for it,and they had been fortunate enough to secure St for £2oo.—His Lordship gave the required sanction. • eirearwe's Dynes are relax nigialt and day.--Adot. bfle,t• PRIIVEA`P.D'S, 51, Ifigk-st., Runcorn, for Moo best, !rlr OVIXEd WATCH a working max can have. -Advt. RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB. Mr. J. W. S. Celtic, secretary of the Financial Retorra &amp;Saociation was announced to deliver a lecture at Run- ' corn Liberal Club on Wednesday night on the subject of "The French Revolution end what it cost us." Through glotrie unexplained cause Mr. Callie failed to put is ."'Pleearance and Mr. IVilliam Lewis of Liverpoel, a mem- net of the Peace Society, volunteered to give an address 411 " The demands of militarism. Mr. John Clarke °coupied the chair. The lecturer dwell upon the heavy 11,zPense ef maintaining the army and navy, and argued 'mat all money spent ii this way and in war was in reality so much treasure lost to the country. Not only , 7, as war contrary to the urinciples of the Christian re- "gion, nut as a matter of fact,it was ridiculous to spend 14111i/ens sterling and sacrifice thousands of valuable lives and then hold a court of arbitration afterwards as was 11,,ew. the practice. The lecturer edvocated the forme.tien . • .4.4 a board of international arbitration and quoted metier 'lttstances to show that such a board was practicable anil : 4 'would be successful. A discussion followed and a cor- e gial vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Lewis. Ton.want your WITCH HATAIP.EIi by skilled workmen -0 it 1" PRITCHARD'S. • Adtt. A CRUEL HUSBAND AT WESTON POINT. GeergeFryer, a watcrinan, of Weston Point, was sum- binned before. Dr. Steele and Mr. F. J. Norman, at Run- corn Petty Sessions, to answer a charge of assaulting, Wife Mary. The defendant had been away with a Itearaer for a fortnight and returned home at midnight •?la the sth February. When he obtained admittance to the dwelling he ate up the food that had been obtained ler the children on the previous day, made ins wife pro- vide him with a clean shirt, and then he ilitreated her _something scandalous." One of his brutal blows was Iltslirected,and fie struck his nine-months old baby. ears. -!.4:reer said she was afraid to live with him. On more than one occasion he had turned her out at night with laothing but her nightdress on, and he had threatened to _altf. her life. He was lazy, and had not worked for six Sae had to work very hard to keep her four '.llildren, and she did not want a lazy man, added to the burden She asked for a separation order.—The defen- latit, who appeared to treat the matter somewhat in- ;lttferently and made no effective reply to the charee,said elie wanted the 'same.—The Bench fined him ss. and OSS 14s. 6d., or in default 18 days. They further .gra 0_ the a separation order,the wife to have the custody fe he children, and the defendant to contribute 103. a ek towards their support.</p>
        <p>HOLY IRINITY CHURCH, RUNCORN. This church has just undergone a thorough repelling and renovation, and the interior has been suitably painted and decorated. The work has been carried out under the superintendence of Mr. C. A. Timmins, Vicar's Warden.</p>
        <p>RBCRABITRS AT RUNCORN. In connection with the four Runcorn " tents "of the order of Rechabites, a public meeting will be held in Bethesda Schoolroom on Tuesday evening next. An admirable musical entertainment will be interspersed with speeches from Councillor J. Dimond P.H.C.R., of Southport, and the Rev. J. Pulford Williams. When In want of a CHISTE and BLEGAITT Wssarso or PRE3.IINI go to PRITIMAIRD'S, 51, I(lgia-otreat.- 414.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO AN Ex-P.C. AT WIDNES. An interesting gathering was held at tho Widnes Police Court, on Wednesday, under the presidency of P.C. Worafold. On behalf of the force, Supt. Koighley pre- sented a splendid morocco leather arm chair to P.C. Darkin, as a token of the respect in which he was hold after 26 years' service in the force. P.C. Darkin has re- tired en a pension. Previous to joining the police, he had spent ten years in the army. PIITCKARD'S have now stood the test for My years, and quality and first class workmanship are still guaranteed.— A 64,</p>
        <p>WIDNBS CHURCH INSTVIDTB. On Tuesday evening a very enjoyable musical evening was held in connection with the above at St. Paul's Parochial root*, Mr. Stuart Deacon, 8.L., presided, and there was a large attendance. Songs, kc., were rendered by Mrs. Hutchinson, Misses Hilda Deacon, Molly, Waizbom, Kyle, Messrs. S. Beacon' Leach, Pilling, Broughton, and Chadwick. The Rev. W. Stanton Jones presided at the piano. MB. THOMAS LBA, RAKBB AND CONFECTIONS*. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The" British Baker and Confectioner "for the pre' sent month contains the following note :—" Mr. Thomas Lea, Baker and Confectioner, Church-street, Runcorn, has submitted a sample of milk bread made by him for our examination. It is in every way a nice loaf of good cobur and texture, and the flavour is quite of the 'cut and come again' order. Mr. Lea tells us that he is do- ing well with this bread. The good people of Runcorn evidently know a good loaf when they come across 1t.,,</p>
        <p>HEARTS OF OAK SOCIETY, WIDNES. The annual meeting of the above society was held on Tuesday evening at Mr. Law's Railway Hotel, when a presentation of a loather cigar case, a case of silver mounted pipes, a cigar holder, and a silver match box was made to Mr. Charles Whitfield, in recognition of his untiring energies as secretary of the medical aid and electoral society connected with the Hearts of Oak Society. Mr. Whitfield has beensecretary of the society for upwards of eleven years.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD. The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Wed- nesday afternoon, at the offices, the Cemetery, when there were present—Mr. Coleburn (in the chair),the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. W. Mellor,and Mr. W. F. Salkeld (sec.) The Secretary submitted his statement, which showed that the interments for the tf n months to January 25th numbered 452, against 384 in the corresponding period last vear,an increass of 68. ['he fees received amounted to £420 Bs. Bd., compared Rh £375 9s. Bd. to twelve months ago, increase £44 10s. SATURDAY NIGHT CONCERTS AT WIDNES. The weekly concert under the auspices of the United Alkali Company was held on Saturday evening, in the Baths Assembly-room. The first part of the programme was devoted to a sentimental solo competition, in which the following took part : Messrs. W. Ainsworth, G. Norris, W. Hackney, J. Pilling, J. Ramsey, D. Hanlon, and H. Pilgrim. Mr. Norris was awarded first prize (one guinea) for his splendid exposition of "The Village Blacksmith ; "and second prize (10s.1 was handed to Mr. Pilgrim, for an excellent rendering of " Marguerita." The judges were Professor Ainsley, Messrs. J. Haddock and G. Faulkner. The Farnworth C.E.T.S. Minstrels took up the second part of the programme with a capi- tal entertainment.</p>
