      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>SBRIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SBRIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, about 7.30, a servant of Xr. Gerrard, of Helsby, sustained serious injuries whilst descending the steps at. Runcorn Bridge. When she approached the last flight of steps, she evidently missed her footing, and fell down to the bottom. She cut her forehead vary severely,and was also badly bruised about the head and face. Mr. E. Newton, the chief booking clerk at Runcorn Railway Station, chanced to be in the ticket office and being a certificated member of the St. John Ambulance Association, he stopped the profuse bleeding and bandaged the wound. Dr.MacLennan after- wards examined her and replaced the bandages, which he said had been skilfully applied. She lost a large quan- tity of blood and was rendered very weak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PARENT'S WISH AT_RIINCORM,</head>
        <p>A PARENT'S WISH AT_RIINCORM,</p>
        <p>Before Mr. P. Speakman and Mr. F. J. Norman, at Runcorn Police Court, on Monday, William Plant, aged 28, was brought up under a warrant taken out by his motteer charged with having stoles a quantity of wear- ing apparel the property of his father, William Plant, of 4 Prescot Terrace, Halton-Told. He was further charged with stealing a shawl the property of his sister Mary Plant. It appeared that the prisoner had not done any work for six loonths, during which period he had been drinking heavily. The articles had been missed during that week, and he had handed his mother the pawn tickets for some of them. He had been a constant source of trouble to his parents for some tune and his mother had had to run from the house to escape his violence. Evidence was given showing that the goods had been pawned at Mr. Lightfoot's, Mr. Craddock's and Mr. Imi- son's establishments.—ln reply to the Bench, the mother said it was very hard to have to take those proceedings but she did net want her son near her any more.—Super- intendent Atherton said it was impossible to live with the prisoner through his drinking habits and violent con- duct.—Mr. Speakman gave the prisoner:a severe repri- mand for his unfilial conduct and said he hoped the position he that morning found himself in would be a lesson to him throughout his life. He would be bound over to come up for judgment when called upon. The prisoner was bound over and promised to behave better in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN ENGINEER DROWNED._</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN ENGINEER DROWNED._</p>
        <p>On Mond—ay morning the body of Thomas Houghton, aged 54 years, engineer on the " buoy-boat" Preston, who had been missing since January 4th, was found ! floating in the tidal basin at the docks, and it was removed to his late home, Lowfield Cottage,Canal bank, : Runcorn. On Tuesday, Mr. Worsley, coroner, held the . inquest, Mr. Robert Wright being foreman of the jury. Martha Houghton, Lowfield Cottage, •Widow of tiie deceased, said her husband was engineer on the buoy boat Preston. On the evening of the 4th January at 7.15 she left deceased in the house while she went to do some shopping. When she returned he was not in the house, hut she found his watch and money on the plate shelf. He had suffered from nervous debility for five months, and was attended by Dr. MacDougall. He had been able to follow his work. He was depressed, apd complained of severe pains in his head. He had never threatened to take his life. She could not account for his death at all. They all had tea together then the children sang, and when she left,his boy was reading to him.—Thomas Houghton, a boy, who said he worked on a " packet " gave evidence that he last saw his father alive at ten minutes to eight on the fourth. His father told him to go to bed as they would have to be out early in the morning. Ile went to bed. His father did not say goodnight. He did not see his father take his watch ! from his pockets. They had been singing hymns. There had been no quarrelling.—By Mr. R. Wright : The packet was lying at bettom locks.—Mr. Wright That is close to where he was found.—Joleph Rowe,dockmas- ter, deposed to finding the body in the tidal basin.—The widow recalled. said the doctor had not told her to watch her husband.—P.C. Williams said there were no marks of violence on the body of the deceased.—The Coroner said there were two verdicts which the jury could return. One was " found drowned." If they could connect the state of his mind and the placing of his watch and purse on the shelf with his death, they could return a verdict of " suicide whilst temporarily insane" but he should ask them to seriously consider the evidence before returning such a verdict. It was possible that the man might have fallen into the water whilst on his way to Ins ship. It was a very sad case.—Mr. Robert Wright, foreman, said the jury were in favour of a ver- dict of Found drowned."—The Coroner : I quite agree • with that.</p>
        <p>IRISH CHURCH MISSIONS. On behalf of these missions, the Rev. W. J. Graham,of Leicester, delivered a lecture on Catholicism, to a good audience. The Rev. Dr. Preston presided.</p>
        <p>HAIGCLEY'S DABISIPITS Nip. for Id. at GoLuvaili.—Advt. AGED PERSONS TREAT AT_ WESTON POINT. _</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the aged people of Westoa Point were entertainedto:a substantial tea. Afterwards a mis- cellaneous entertainment was given and the old folks highly enjoyed themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>EX A MINE 1Z , SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 17, 1.894.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINE 1Z , SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 17, 1.894.</p>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>What is evidently a ease of determined suicide was brought to light en Thursday afternoon. Two lads named Hughes and Clarke were going into the Widnes football field, WI tell they discovered a man hanging by a rope fastened round his neck,and suspended from a beam. They at-once gave information to the police, who went and cut the body down. Life was extinct. It was sub- sequently discovered that deceased was a married man, named William Wilstoncroft, Gerrard-et. The poor fel- low left home about a quarter to six on Thursday morn- ing, apparently to go to his work at Messrs. Gasket! Deacons', where he was employed as a fitter. He has been in a desponding state of mind for some time, and attended to by Dr. Lerman. The facts have been com- municated to the County Ceironer.</p>
        <p>Joann( PEWPCIELIt., Watchmaker awl Jeweller, litaaoera.-411vt.</p>
        <p>IRISH NATIONAL LEAGVN.</p>
        <p>The usual wee-il-i—m-eeiW,Titlie Widnes (T. P. O'Con- nor) Branch Irish National League was held in the League Rooms, St. Paul's Buildings, Victoria-road, on Sunday. Councillor Hugh O'Donnell, president, in the chair. There was a good attendance of members and friends. After the minutes of previous meeting and also of committee meeting of 7th 'net., one of which referred to the conduct of a certain Widnes landlord to his tenant, were read and passed, an interesting discussion in refe- rence to proposed extension of Town Hall and increase of clerks' wages took place, there being a resolution on the books to the effect that for the information and guidance of the real Nationalist Councillors municipal matters should be discussed at the League meeting pre- vious to the Town Council meeting. Several members took part in Vie discussion' and some good, sound argu- ments were advanced on both sides principally against the proposed increase and extension,as it was considered there were more urgent and necessary improvements being put aside while these were being put to the front. One of the members contended that things were being done in a peculiar fashion, giving as an instance that in lower Church-st. off Waterloo-road and several streets in Halton-view, where there was not a single house, the footpaths were flagged, while streets in Simms Cross were left unflagged and in a disgraceful condition. On a vote being taken at the end of the discussion, it was found that with one exception all were opposed to the increased salaries and Town Hall extension. The Chair- man announced that the proceeds of the all night dance to be held on the 17th inst., would be devoted to the Evicted Tenants Fund. The usual vote of thanks ter- minazed the proceedings. DEATH OF FREDERICK CUTE NEWCOMB. •</p>
        <p>Northern landscape art has sustained a severe loss in the death of Frederick Clive Newcome, at Conis- ton, North Lancashire, on Saturday evening. The deceased, whose family name was Suker, but who preferred to be known under his painting name of Newcome, was born at Warrington forty-seven years ago. The family removing soon afterwards to Birkenhead, it was there and in Liverpool that he first practised his art. Although he spent a short time at the School of Art in Mount-street Liverpool of which John Finnie was master, attending the evening classes there, he was practically self-taught and, owing to his success in selling his pictures, started at an early age for Bettws-y-Coed with coloured box and canvas in hand, meeting there many of the leading artists of the day. After rov- ing ahout Wales, Devonshire, and Yorkshire for soma years, he went to Keswick in Cumberland, and thenceforth the Lake Country was his head-quarters from which he made excursions to the Highlands and West Isles of Scotland. From 1875 onwards for several years he exhibited at the Royal Academy the Walker Gallery, Liverpool, and other provincial galleries, but many of his beet pictures round their way at once into private collections without being exhibited. Mr. Newcome had no ambition for re- wards of any kin:l, and never sougut artistic hon- ours other that those his paintings brought him. His work his distinguished by its breadth and truthfulness. Lustrous with colour as many of Newcome pictures are, they convey the idea that never step beyond the boundary of Nature, being faithful transcripts of her many changing moods. His health had been failing for some time,and a low months ago he determined to be once more amongst his beloved Lake Mountains, in the midst of which he has peacef ally fallen asleep,leaving a widow and daughter to mourn his loss. Mr. Newconie was, on his father's side, a cousin of Walter Crane, tho eminent artist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>-4,- TIUNGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>-4,- TIUNGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>••••=uppw,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL COIIII2BPONDIMT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL COIIII2BPONDIMT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Brunner, 11.1',, paid a visit to Runcorn, oa Saturday, and those who had the chance to see him were glad to observe that he had recovered from his illnesg, and appeared well and hearty, In the afternoon he went to the football field, and witnessed the second half of the game with the Old Edwardians. He has promised to " patronise " another match, and "kick off," and it is probable that this function will take place on April 14th, when Runcorn play Salford at home. In the emen- ing, Mr Brunner attended a social gathering at the Gladstone Club, Northwich, and delivered a capial speech.</p>
        <p>The principal business which brought Mr. Brun-' nor to Runcorn was the purchase of St. John's"' PresUyterian chapel and school, and the matter wal completed, I understand. The building is to Ile' converted into a Union Hall, to replace the building which is now the Liberal Club in Regent-street. Considerable alterations will be needed. The ground floor is to be divided into convenient offices to accommodate the various Trades Unions and Friendly societies, and the upper storey will also be made available for offices but by means of move- able partitions, it will be possible to make it into one large room suitable for tea parties, social gatherings and public meetings. If hidden in a back street, the building is in a central position and will answer the purpose intended admirably.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn people owe a debt of gratitude to those gentlemen who have been instrumental first in organising the University Extension Lectures, then the popular lectures, and lastly the Gilchrist lectures. Besides passing many hours pleasantly away, they afforded to numerous persons a highly intellectual treat, and created impressions that will probably never be effaced. It is gratifying there- fore, to find that the Popular Lecture Committee is to be a permanent association or body, and the first labour this committee as now constituted is under- taking is to clear off a debt of £35, created by the extension lectures. With this end in vie*, Mr. M. E. Sadler of Christ Church, Oxford, is to deliver a lecture on Monday the 23th inst., on Oxford, its history, and great men." This week a meeting was held to advocate the claims of this lecture and of the popular lectures genetally, and it was stated that Mr. Sadler's lecture is one of entrancing interest, and illustrated by many beautiful lantern slides.</p>
        <p>In the course of a speech, Mr. Imison mentioned a visit to the celebrated seat of learning, and quoted a writer who speaking of the beauties of Ox- ford, gave a discription of High-street in the follow- ing words :—" The visitors here behold the finest sweep of street architecture which Europe can ex- hibit, Antwerp may have quainter places, Edinburgh more striking blendings of art with nature, and there is architecture more pi cturesque in Nurem- burg and Frankfort. But for stately beauty that same broad curve of colleges, enhanced by many a spire and dome and relieved by a back ground of rich foliage, is absolutely without parallel." The writer further says "It is a despair to see such a place (Oxford) and ever to leave it ; for it would take a lifetime, and more than one to comprehend and enjoy it satisfactorily." Mr. Sadler will enable the Runcorn people to see and hear something of this noble place, and its glorious historical associa- tions, and there will be disappointment if Runcorn Public Hall is not crowded when the occasion ar- rives. The lecture and its objects ought to form to- gether an irresistible " draw."</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Wiswall of 'Runcorn,was one of the guests at the opening of the dry dock in the Manchester Ship Canal at Mode Wheel on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>By the action of Widnes Town Council on Tues- day, the extension of the Town Hall is shelved for the present. It is far better this way than to have carried out a temporary scheme at a cost of £2,000. This would practically be money wasted. There is no doubt that the stagnation in trade is seriously affecting all classes in the Chemical borough. When better times come there will be no objection to carrying out the complete scheme suggested by the General Purpovs Committee at a cost of £12,000.•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>A.P.A.</head>
        <p>THE A.P.A.</p>
        <p>The above mysterious letters are to be seen just now in most of the afew England papers. Their first appearance caused consideiable curiosity, as they were usually connected with some statement as to direful proceedings that were in some under- handed way seeking to compass the downfall of the American Republic, or national school system. When the secret was let out, it seemed that a feel- ing was spreading amongst these good New Eng- land Puritans,that the Roman Catholic Church in- tended by hook or crook to lay her hands on the public funds. She was seeking to establish het ohurch,and to accomplish this she had determined, with the public funds, to endow her schools. Of course, if all this were true, which is some- what doutttul, organise was the next word, as no- thing can be done without organising, and at once sprang up a society to be known as the Americanl Protective Association. A 'very much shorter title I might have been found by these alarmists, only it would not have been strictly American. Yet it may and does do duty when in secret conclave met. It is neither more nor leas than an Orange Society, aid those who know something of its English moribund parent know full well that the work it will accomplish will be just as useless. On my first arrival here it was taken for granted that as an Englishman I should be a Tory, and I was invited to one of their meetings. I found a nice gathering of old ladies, a couple of parsons, and about two more lug men. Of course I listened I even went so far as to recite a little, but when it came to talking Orangeism—well somehow things didn't run smooth, I fell to talking Home Rule complete religious equality, and so on. This didn't suit by any means, so I was called on one side, and argued with, the result as usual, an ignorant par- son trying to lead ignorant women boycott accord- ingly. Since then I attended one Sunday evening at one of the churches, where I found a converted Roman Catholic was to lecture on the sins of omis- eion and commission peculiar to the Church he had left. The lecturer was an Irishman, he had all his brogue with him, and what is not so common in the etates,he was dineely ignorant. He eommenc,ed by laying his right hand across his breast, and say- ing that he " felt fullof the love of God. lie loved all men. bet he knew that his Roman Catholic brethren, if there were any present, would like to thrust a dagger fo his heart." I thought that good torn start, but, before he got through he had me on the rack. When he had done I rose end asked him if lie" believed in the ultimate triumph of truth." Ile answered " Yes." I then asked him if it was not proof that Roman Catholicism was true, if it was as he said it was,trinuiphing. Well, well, what a scene we had. He lost his temper, raged, and romped around, and tiaished by calling me a politician, and a jesuit in disguise. I,lore than one half the voter 3in this city are Roman Catholics, and presumably Irish. Speak- ing to a lady acquaintance about this feature, soon after the above incidents, I referred to the great disparity existing in the size of families of , the Irish, and Americans. "Yes," she acknowledged, ' "it was so." The Reuaan Catholic Church en- I oouraged large familes seeking by those means to gain her ends, always jesuitical. I see nothing so clear as that the Roman church is likely to grow into the strongest ecclesiastical power in the States. Her utterances on the labour question are given with studied effect, and carry the crowds. Her utterances on home life are always delivered with fervour, and are received with satisfaction by her devotees. In morals she is, confessedly, suffi- ciently lax to give forth an idea of full liberty. Such being the case she will grow, and wilt govern. Look at the other churches, 14.1piscopalian,' &amp;c„ &amp;c,, what are their teachings? On labour questions, undoubtedly they are on the fence. In morals,much like the 11. C. church, because " morals will never take a man to heaven." In domestic life, afraid to touch it, with this result, Dr. Edson says,—" an inceasingly large proportion of ,the women of the American race are unable to perform their functions as mothers, and these women include the mentally best we have among us. In Mass, the birth rate fell from 25.61 per thousand in 1860 to 19'22 per thou- sand in 1890. At this rate the deaths would soon exceed the births and the native born American be well on the road to extinction. Of course if one is to look for a reason for the growth of the R.C. church in the States the view ilust be wide as possible. That this Church moves there tltAti be no doubt, to stand still would be to At the Parliament of Religions it took a fore- most place. The speeches of its Archbishops and Bishops were marked by a wonderful amount of breadth and liberality, amounting to an absolute I dental of written history. Archbishop Ryan said, "The mission of the Congress was 0 bring the Catholic and non-Catholic worlds nearer together— to meike men fully understand each other,—first of all Iv appreciating the great truth that the non- Catholic, world is not opposed to the Catholic world at all, but to something which it thinks is the Catholic world, Yli,e very doctrines on which tkis is foliteled ou doctrines that we 'reject as 'sti,tantlidtlyr,eiateleitntdhigenina:ntly.Reaasditnhge taerliumlelnoiplahobas:aottivithce3:aillinleyt,twaesorerigh t at the four propositions put foretted by Mr. Gladstotiei in his Vatican Decrees ode fetissomethat amazed. I have attempted to pUt forward some reasons feef the growth of the 'is chiirch in this country. 11 hat it is growing an Undoubted fact. That it rtliesenta truth is *ts possini'asit is for Mahometanieget or Buddhism, or any other of the isms up to date. Let us noev tairdagain to the A.P.it.e or to the religious bocliee of America who are outoide of the .Roman chiertsh. The movement has a politico- religious undercurrent in it that manifests itself in sulkienly-called caueng meetings, at whict are selseeted men pledged to remove all Roman Catho- lics, or those who favour Roman Catholics. These are alactpledaed to remove lukewarm officials, or those villa display a desire to act fairly amongst tte contending-religionistse From this it may be seen that a very pretty fight is going on. The petty , theocracy Which the Puritan of the past sought to establish has passed away. He seeks again with , very different' material to force himself into ascen- dancy, and by Ways that are dark, and means that ; are questionable, the A.P.A. has established a court of inquisition wanting only in power to be 83 great I a curse to liberty as the one it holds likes a bogey. This Association had its origin in Kansas. All sortsl of tales are told of its origin. One to the effect that a large package was delivered at an express office directed to a Roman Catholic priest.. Suspicion rested upon it, when opened it was found to con- tain small arms and ammunition. another consign- ment consisted of coffins all containing small arme and ammunition. I can well believe that aft these: absurdities were believed from my own experiences{ of the credulity of people generally. The Associa- tion was formed and grew like a weed. Its mem- bers were pledged to absolute secrecy, and it has, become a force in the city and State. Striking its blows in the dark, it was not very easy to detect the striker, and it at first received few blows in return. The purpose of the A.P.A. is to antagonises Romanismolot as a religion but as a political power., How well this declared purpose is carried out will be, seen. In its work of exterminating Romanism it proposes to get hold of the ballot box. Its mem-. hers are induced to join, that they may pledge themselves to vote, and vote against R.C.s. The canvassing tor members is done with caution, and with a good deal of Yankee cuteness. The candidate has handed to him a set of five principles, touching the protection of the public Ischool system, no public funds or property to be used for sectarian purposes, the preserving and maintaining the constitution and government of the U.S., the restriction of immigration,and the extension of time required for naturalization. Having signed this innocent look- ing document the candidate with dollar in hand presents himself to the lodge. The questions now take a different hue. Are you a Roman Catholic? Is your wife a R.C.? Are any of your relatives R.C.s or have any of them been R.C.s ? Do you believe in a Deity ? The candidate's answers to the first, three questions are watched pretty closely. The fourth is not so important, as these valiant defenders of the Faith are on a little on the twist among themselves. Being inside the lodge, a set of oaths are next placed before the newly made member of which I give one. They are as follows, "I do most solemnly promise and swear that I will not allow any one a member of the R. C. church to beeome a member of this Order, I knowing him to be such, that I will use my influence to promote the interests of ell Protestants everywhere in the world ; that I will not employ a R. C. in any capacity if I can procure the services of a Protes- tant ; that I will not aid in building or in maintain- ing by any resources, any R.C. church or institution of their sect or creed whatever, but will do all in my power to retard and break down the power of the Pie; that I will not enter into any controversy with a It. C. upon the subject of this Order, nor will I enter into any agreement with a R.C. to strike or create a disturbance whereby the 11.0. employe may undermine and substitute the Pro-</p>
        <p>f: •</p>
        <p>testant, that in all grievances I will seek only Pro- testants, and counsel with them, to the exclusion of all R.C.'s, and will not make known to them any- thing of any nature matured at such conferences; that I will not countenance the nomination in any caucus or convention of a R.C. for any office in the gift of American people, that I will not vote for, nor counsel others to vote for,any R.C.; that I will endeavour at all times to place the political posi- tion of this government in the hands of Protestants. To all of which Ido most solemnly swear, so help me Sod." I must give the following denunciation. It smacks so much of the church, and reveals so much of the true inwardness of the Orange parties deal- ings with their fellow Christians that it would be a pity to witlaold it :—" I hereby denounce Roman Catholicism. I hereby deaonnce the Pope sitting at Rome or elsewhere. I denounce his priests and emissaries, and the diabolical work of the Roman Catholic Church, and I hereby pledge myself to the cause of Protestantism, to the end that there may be no interference with the discharge of citizenship, and I solemnly bind myself to protect at all times, and with all means in my power, the good name of the order and its members, so help me God. Amen." _ _</p>
        <p>.It is interesting to be in the company of the men and women who have joined this society. They will gravely tell you that all Roman Catholic insti- tutions have stored away in their cellars, arms and amunttion, and that Roman Catholic temperance, literary and social organisations are being drilled in military movements in anticipation of an armed uprising against, and the ultimate capture of, the Federated government, and tbe annihilation of Pro- testantism, is a favourite topic of discussion. This sort of wild talk reminds one of the famous speech delivered by Lord Rosmore, in Camla, Orange Hall, wherein he likened Mr. Gladstone to a mad dog, and to that other Past Grand \faster of Orangemen who saitn. "If Gladstone had lived in the days of Harry VIII., or bloody Mary, or the heroic Queen Bess,he would have speedily been sent to the tower and decapitated, or else the:only place he is fit for, a lunatic asylum." It has been often said that our liberties depend largely on the differences, and disagreements of the Christians, were they ever to be united, the inquir- ing spirit would have a abort shrift. H. WHITHAIL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The new road which is being constructed from Wil- derspool Causeway to the Chester road, is almost com- pleted. The svork,which is being carried out by Mr. Griffiths, has employed some hundreds of men for a number of weeks. The completion of the work has been delayed for some time, owing to the dredging which is being carried out on the new river deviation,which runs alongside of the road. Many parts of the road have been—and are at present—occupied by cranes and other "tackle" used t 3 facilitate the dredging operations. The question of bringing the' dredging to an end this week is at present under consideration, and if it is decided to discontinue it, the road, we are informed, will be com- pleted next week. If it is decided to continue the dredg- ing, however, partitions will have to be erected round the cranes, &amp;c, and the time of opening will thus be fur- ther delayed. The construction of the lock in /rpley meadows is progressing rapidly. A. new road is being constructed between the Ship Canal and the old Runcorn Canal,all the way from Wigg Bros.' works at Runcorn, to Moore. This road is being made for the purpose of opening up the adjacent land for building purposes, and the work is being carried out by the Canal Company. Several other smaller roads are also being constructed near Moore for Sir Gilbert Green- all, t 9 accommodate his tenants. This work is being carried out by the company, in accordance with agree- ment. Many complaints have been made lately by the resi- dents in Stockton Heath, about the lengtq of time they are delayed by the opening of the swing bridge. The time allowed in the Act for the opening and closing of the bridge is 15 minutes, and we understand that this has only been exceeded on one occasion. Nevertheless, 13 minutes' delay is not at all pleasant for those persons who might have to put up with it. The time taken in opening and closing the bridge however, is gradually being reduced, and it is stated on good authority, that certain improvements are in contemplation which will minimise the delays to four and five minutes,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TEIREATENED STRIKE OF FLATMEN.</head>
        <p>TEIREATENED STRIKE OF FLATMEN.</p>
        <p>The dispute between the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany (Bridgewater undertaking) and their flatmen culminated on Saturday morning in the Upper Mersey Watermen and Porters' Association serving the company with notice that the men would strike work on Saturday nevt unless their demands were satisfied. The men claim the following increases in the tonnage rates from Manchester Docks :—To Water-street, 2d. to 2d.; to Castle,field, 2d. to 3d.; to Chorlton-street, 31.; to Roch- dale, sd. to 6d.; from Latchford to Warringtob, 2d. to 3d. ; and to and from Warrington and Liverpool, via Rixton, 4d. to sd. They further ask that there shall be a minimum weight of 30 tons from Manchester Docks to Water-street, Castlefield and Chorlten-street ; and of 20 tons, or travelling light, from Runcorn to Latchford and to Andorton. At present the men are only paidovertime en Sundays; but they now claim sd. per hour after six p.m. every day except Saturdays, when the overtime must comMenCe at two pm. A further demand is that flatmen shall receive 43. for navigating their flats on Sundays; and that crews of vessels having worked the whole of a night shall be entitled to six hours rert. One great grievance is that at the Manchester and Salford docks the flatmen have to load and nload their boats, labour which they have never previously performed at Liverpool, and they strongly insist that in future they shall 110 t, Pe asked to handle cargo. About COO men are affected by he notice. The association is in a strong position, and th 6 men evince a disposition to make a determined fight i what they consider their rights are , not granted.</p>
        <p>Bi/pilaf/7' POD THE PLAIItgN. The grecutive Committee of the Federation of Tradqg and Labour Unions of the Mersey District, have unani- mously passed the following resolution: That, we the Mersey District Council of the Vetieration of Trades and Labour Unions connected with shipping, carrying and other industries, consider the action of the Manchester Ship Canal Co. the Bridgewater Canal Undertaking in the attempt to impose upon flatmen work hitherto done by dock labourers, is detrimental to the interests of everyone concerned and dangerous to the lives of the flatrnen in particular; and in case they persist in the present demand we call upon the federated trades to as- sist by every means in their power to resist the unjust demand. THE FLA.TMEN'S COMMITTEE SPEAK.</p>
        <p>Last week end a communication signed by Mr. Joseph Billam, president, and Mr. Ellis Gatley, secretary, on be- half of the Executive Committee of the Upper Mersey Watermen and Porter's Association, forwarded a coni- munication to each director of the Ship Canal in which it is stated that " The relations between the men, their representatives (although not officially recognised) and the officials of the Company have been cordial and many flagiatit grievances have in consequence been removed, and generally the agreements in this way concluded have been respected and adhered to. We regret however to say that in one matter of very great importance to the men, the agreement has not been faithfully kept by the Company at their various wharves and stations, namely that of Sept. 10th. 1890, when " It was mutually agreed that the crews of flats having worked the whole of the previous night are entitled to six hours rest. We res- pectfully submit that in the interests of the Company and the health af the men that this period of rest from labour is.abselutely necessary and ought to have been strictly adrered to. The opening of the Ship Canal has ia some measure changed the conditions under which tile- men labour and has added very much to such labour without a corresponding addition to their wages. It hasealso caused work to be forced upon them which does not come within the duties of flatmen, and which is really' " dockers " or " porters" work, and is re- cognise&amp; as such by the company and ether firms at all other ports. The men contend that the present tonnage rate of al. is inadequate, and they also consider that as a minimum weight operates as between Liverpool and Manchester, that a minimum weight should also operate as between the docks and the various stations named in the printed circular (quoted above). All other matters mentioned in the circular are those which have been in past or wholly conceded, or the justice of the claim ad- mitted, but which from some cause have not been ad- hered to by the company, who in this matter is expected to lead the way to other firms awaiting their action, and which generally accept the condition mutually agreed upon between the company and its employes, as those which are to govern their own employes. We trust, therefore, that you may very carefully consider the en- closed proposal of the men, due notice of which having been submitted to the company will, unless otherwise agreed upon, be enforced by the men from the date of such notice, Feb.l7th, 4 pen.,1894. We hope to have the co-operation of the company for an amicable settlement of this dispute, thus averting measures, which alike in the interest of the company and their employers, and especially in the interest of the trade and commerce of the city of Manchester would he deplorable, if not disastrous. On inquiry yesterday our representative ascertained that no reply whatever had been received from the Man- chester Ship Canal Co. with reference to the matters in dispute. During the week meetings have been held at Liverpool, Manchester and Runcorn. The men have attended in large numbers and have expressed their determinatian to strike should what they deem their just demands be not conceded. The Association is strong in funds, is well organised, and there is help promised from other unions and from trade councils. Three carrying firms are in negotiation with the AsaociatLn and it is ' probable that terms will be arranged with two of these. ' In such an event the men will be allowed to work for</p>
        <p>:••</p>
        <p>theee firms. It is argued en behalf of the carrying com- panies that the demands are excessive, that it has always been the custom for watermen to store and sling their cargo and that it was intended, before the issue of the men's notice, to make a concession in the tonnage rates. The average wages, it is said of the captain's is 303. to 323. per week and of the hands 263. per week, besides which clothing, coal, oil, cooking utensils, kc., are pro- vided.</p>
      </div>
