      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WEEK'S EARNINGS.</head>
        <p>A WEEK'S EARNINGS.</p>
        <p>"Liverpool Journal of Commerce "says :—We_are informed that the gross reverme of the canal, tolls, ac., for last wetk was under £2,000. Shonld this be correct and it is given on apparently good authority, the canal will have much headway to make to pay its fixed charges. We would suggest that the Canal Company adopt he system of the various railway companies and publish a weekly traffic return with the statement of their earnings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN AND THE SHIP tANSL ACT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND THE SHIP tANSL ACT.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning, a mecting of the General Purposes and Parliamentary Committee of the Runcorn Boarel of Commissioners was held, to consider the 14Ianehester Ship Canal Act, 1894 The V4Ojs clauses of the Act were gone through and the subject was postponed to a uture meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WIDNES FERRY.</head>
        <p>WIDNES FERRY.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at the quarterly meeting of the Widnes Town Council,the General Purposes Committee submitted the following minute which was approved :—" The Borough Surveyor reported that the Manchester Ship Canal Company had erected the floating pontoon, and carried out the other works in connection with the ac- cess to the ferry required by the Manchester Ship Canal Act, but that the London and North-Western Railway Co., had not re-established the ferry as promised. It was resolved that the Town Clerk again write to the solicitor to the London and North-Western Railway Co requesting that the ferry may be re-opened -without fur- ther delay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ASSESSMENT OF THE CANAL</head>
        <p>THE ASSESSMENT OF THE CANAL</p>
        <p>The representatives of the Assessment Committees of the various unions through which the Manchester Ship Canal passes held a conference on Tuesday to arrive at a proper valuation on which to assess the undertaking. Ultimately it was resolved that the figure should be £1,500 per mile. The decision was conveyed to repre- sentatives of the Ship Canal Company who were in attendance, and by them it was considered excessive. With a view to the subject being omsidered by the Board of Directors of the Canal Company, the meeting was adjourned for a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NO CONCESSIONS TO LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>NO CONCESSIONS TO LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>A Liverpool firm which wrote to see if the traffic be- tween Liverpool and Manchester, via the Ship Canal, should not be charged less than the meal. -going vessels, received a reply, signed by Mr. Marshall Stevens, of which the following is an extract :—" This undertaking is the Manchester Ship Canal and not the Liverpool Barge Company,and our Directors have not the slightest intention of handicapping ships trading to Manchester by giving lower tolls on traffic to and from Manchester and Liverpool. The same tolls are in operation whether the traffic is going to the most distant pert or to Liverpool."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN TRADE</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TRADE</p>
        <p>The remission of ship dues on vessels calling at Run- corn for cargo on their voyage from Ruacorn, has already had its affect. The Norwegian steamship Erling, of Christiana, arrived at Runcorn lay-by from Man- chester, and operations were at once commeneed to place a thousand tons of salt into her holds. Messrs. Verdin, Bros., agency, of the Salt Union, are supplying the salt and their energetic Runcorn manager, Mr. Greenway Millington, had charge of the arrangements. The loading proceeded with such expedition that the ship was ready yesterday evening to set out on her voyage to Aarburg, Denmark. This is the largest vessel that has ever been loaded at Runcorn. Plans of a coal tip at Runcors, lay-by have been submitted to the Ship Canal Co., and hope ia entertained that it will be fixed. There are also rumours of . other developments at Runcorn Dock. It is quite clear that those who took pessemistic views of the future of Runcorn shipping trade will have to change their opinions. QUICK DESPATCH AT RUNCORN DOCKS. • The ss. Cygnus arrived with a cargo of 360 tons of China clay on Friday afternoon, and got into discharg- ing berth about 5 p.m. and with one gang of men her cargo was placed in the warehouses. She took in a cargo of 355 tons of coal on Saturday, ready to leave by the evening tide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL TEA PARTY. 1 The small schoolroom adjoining St. John's Pres- byterian Church, Winwick-at., was well tilled on the occasion of the annual tea party on Thursday even- ing. About 150 persons sat down to tea. Trays had been given by the following: Lady Greenall. Rev. J. Warnock, Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, R. W. Fran- comb, T. H. Sutton, W. D. Houghton, Jas. Tinsley, W. J. Richardson, James Platt and Son, Thomas Gray, J. Macdowell, Jas. Thompson, P. Walker and Sons, W. Fulton Smith, and Mrs. Barlow. The tea tables were presided over by Mesdames Barlow, Blaekburne, Leask, Lockerbie, Shearer, Blair, Miss McLarty aud Ms Longdin. The usual entertainment followed, presided otter by, Alderman T. H. Suttee. He was supported en the plat- form by the Rev. J. Warnock (pastor) and the Rev. 1. IDunn (Risley). The Cnainstax said he appreciated the honottir which the minister of the church had conferred upon hini by again asking him to preside on the occasion of their annual tea party. He had always been pleased to at- tend those meetings. He had been to a great many tea parties in his life, and he had been to many in that room. Mr. Sutton afterwards referred to his long ac- quaintance with the former pastor. He was very glad to hear that the church was prospering, and in as good a position as they could expect. When they came to consider the difficulties to get people to attend churches sod chapels at the prevent time it spoke well for that church and congregation, and many other churches and Chapels in the town, that they had kept them on and supported them in a satisfactory way. (Applause.) The Rev. J. Wenszotx said he was glad to see so many ' of their own congsgation there that night. He also extended a hearts' welcome to friends who were present from other chneTies. 'Many thanks were due to their hairman. TOM was not the first occasion on which Alelerman 3rfrin had presided at their annual tea meet- ing. And he hi to the elusion that be made an adinfraZ,la chairtnaZ. One of the Advantigts of a tea meeting. was Mist it gave him an oppOr:unity of doing a little pastoral visitation. He could not fay anything in reference to their church affairs but what was already known to .mist of them. Thy had had revera; Josses during the past year, hut at the nixie time he thoughi, be was right in saying that their membership was very much the same substantially, as what it was a year age. (Applause.) They had the misfortune to lose two of their members by death—Mr. Patterson who was with them for many years, and seldom missed attending their annual gathering, and also Mr. Campbell, whose vigour for his years was very remarkable, he having reached the patriarchal ago of four score years and ten. With reference to their finances he need not say that owing to the industrial crisis, it was not strange that they had suffered somewhat. But by keeping down expenses the treasurer had been fairly well able to make ends meet. (Applause.) It was quite true that they began another year with an adverse balance of DI, but they need not be afraid of an account like that. Some cougeregations whose collections were far more than theirs would he very glad to have as little a balance as that. The progress that they had to report had been brought about by a good deal of work and co-operation of workers, and he took that opportunity of tendering to them his hearty thanks. In a populous neighbour- hood like theirs they had a great opportunity of doing good work if they would only do it. Where there was a will, there was a way. If any isf their friends would like to engage in more active work he should be glad to find them something to do, either in district visitation, sabbath school work, band of hope work or in many ' ether ways. (Applause.) Thal-tee. J. Dllltilq, of Risley, also spoke. lathe course of a short address he gave his hearers some excellent advice, and wished them " God speed." A programme of vocal and instrumental music was gone through. The soloists were Mr. T. Longdin. Miss Pemberton and Miss Rawlings and each met with a hearty reception. Mr. Grounds contributed a comic song, and caused much merriment by big ventriloquial entertainment. Mr. Hoyle rendered with considerable skill selections on the violin, and Miss A. Longdin played a pianoforte solo in a creditable manlier. Mr. H. M. Ashton well deserved the enthusiastic applause which followed his recitation. Mr. Wadsworth, the organist of the church, was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>- ,d</p>
        <p>IEI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>F RODSH A.M.</head>
        <p>F RODSH A.M.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMPANY. —The meeting of the share- holders ot this company was held on Thursday afternoon to receive the report of the directors. In this they re- commended the payment of a dividend at 10 per cent.on A shares, and 7 per cent. on B shares. This was ap- proved. TIIB WATER SUPPLY—There has already been con- siderable progress made in the construction of the water works at Frodsham. Those employed by the contractors Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons, of Runcorn, are local people, and thus work is heing Mond for a number of men who for some time had been disengaged. FART,IERS' AucrioN COMPANY.—A meeting of the share- holders of this company was held on Monclay,at the Bear's Paw Hotel, under the presidency of the chairman, Mr.H. E. Heywood. The report, showing the result of the year's working,was of a very satisfactory character,and ' it enabled the directors to recommend the payment of a dividend of 10 per cent. for the year, and increase the re- serve fund by £5O. The report was adopted, and the dividend declared. The directors chosen were—Messrs. Heywood, Horton, John Frith, W. Harrison, Ralph Bate, Win. Ellison, S. B. Chadwick, and Wm. Youd. A vote of thanks was accorded to the chairman, the directors,and the hon. auditor, for their services during the past :Tar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, the members of the Bold-st., Wes- leyan Chapel were favoured with a visit from the Rev. 'J. 11. liopkine, who has done so much good work in South London. lie made such a good impression that he went back to London again with some very substantial tokens of esteem aid regard in the shape of subscriptions to the amount of abcut, .E 25 to help in the carrying nut of his mission_ - work. This SUM will be all the more welcome as it was totally unexpected. The rev, gentleman had _:, come to preach and lecture in aid of the Bold-st., Sunday school, and the usual collection had been • held, when at to instigation of the Rev. Owen Watkins, a subscription list was commenced and it ; speedily rose to the above amount. The amount realized thus unexpectedly, was "not bad," as Mr. ; Watkins said, considering the evil times Warring- ; ton had passed through recently. At the same time , II am disposed to think that " impromptu " sub- scription lists, are not a likely means of popalarie- ing lectures. Some folks do not like variations of the programme—not of this kind at all events.</p>
        <p>Someone haq been having a "rise" out' of the inhabitants of Warrington in their new born dig- nity of being within hail of the ships which "do business in great waters." A message from the sea is rather a novelty, as there are not so many of these mesaages which get so far as Warrington, se that when on Sunday, a bottle with a note in it was found in the river near Mersey Mills,there was some conjecture as to whether it was a genuine "message from the sea," or only a hoax. Conjecture was, however, soon set at rest by the discovery that the ship mentioned in the " message " was safely docked at Liverpool in January. The inventor of the hoax, therefore, whoever he is, was rather clumsy in his work. Why, for example, did he not make it a message from the " Naronic," or some other vessel missing. When people try hoaxes they should be sure of their ground. From the look of the bottle, it was as likely as not thrown into the river no nearer the sea than Warrington is. It fa rather a large order to think of the bottle coming from the Mediterranean to the weir of the Mersey at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Debating Society have every reason to congratulate themselves upon the number present at their meeting on Monday night to hear the Rev. F. K. Freeston's paper upon the "Faith of a heretic." The paper was a high class one, and Mr. Freeston argued very ably in- favour of the " heretic." The majority of those present were evidently "heretics," to judge from the responses to the various speakers. The rev. gentleman's paper was put in such a fashion that even his opponents were compelled to admit that they agreed with the greater part of it. In fact, they found a great deal of fault with him for not finding them sufficient points of attack. The most able opponent of Mr. foreesten was Mr. Hall, whose debating abilities are out of the ordinary order. The debate throughout was of high order, and if the society attained the same level every week,it would. have no cause to fear any falling off.in the atten- dance.</p>
        <p>I am told that some of the worst fears as to the effect of the Ship Canal on the navigation of the river are heiug realised. The prediction of Council- for IL T. Fairclough that we should probably have no more floods at Warrington is being verified. It is calculated by some experts that under the condi- tions that formerly prevailed, the late rains would. , have deepened the river at Warrington by about six , feet, but I am told that, as a matter of fact, it has. i not been deepened by more than three inches. The 1 effect of the Canal upon the tides, too, is proving , to be very much as Councillor Fairclough predicted, I,for the average reduction upon the water coming from Liverpool iii something like 20 inches. These two changes in the character of the river are al- reedy ek,rio44 ergnigh. in their effects upon the (navigation, but it is said 6ai, riitict worse develop- rents are anticipated. The sand which is brought ! up by the tide, is beginning to "silt up" the bed of . the river at W arrington,which is not now "scoured" as it used to be by the strong flow of water from the upper reaches of the river.</p>
        <p>In a few months, according to statements made by certain trade's of the town, the river will be al- most totally unnavigable to barges and steam boats at Warrington. A rather unfortunate develop- ment, I hear, is also being encountered at the river deviation at Arpley. According to the Acts the deviation was dredged eight feet, but already it he,s begun to show a diminished depth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MERLIIt.</head>
        <p>MERLIIt.</p>
        <p>RENOVATION OF FRIAR'S GREEN CHAPEL,</p>
        <p>boring the past month this chapel has undergone a therough renovation, and the work is now almost com- pleted. Mr. William Fletcher, painter and decorator, of 15. Winwick-street, was entrusted with the work,and the inside of the aliapel now presents a bright and cheer- ful appearance. The outside of the building has keen thoroughly overhauled and pointed by Mr. J. Longshaw, builder. This has been readered necessary owing to a subsidence caused by the chapel being built over an old burial place. It may be stated that this chapel is the third which has oecupied the site, the preceding chapels dating back to Wesley's time. The building is used both for Sunday school and chapel, the first storey being a schoolroom, and the second, the chapel. Both have been 'painted and decorated. The ceiling of the chapel is painted cream colour picked out in dull blue and the walls have been painted a salmon colour. A deep frieze, stencilted with a conventional rendering of the honey- suckle, supports the ceiling. Over the pitchpine dado, there is a band, the pattern being a twisting ivy leaf, but the most striking feature of the decoration is the wall behind the rostrum. Here a scripture text lettered in gold is painted on a back ground formed with a diaper of lily pattern. Imitation pillasters enclose the panel, giving It good effect and harmony. All the old gas fittings have been removed, and new " sunlights" have been fixed by Mr. J. S. Garland, Bridge.strort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LONDON MISSION WO/t.</head>
        <p>LONDON MISSION WO/t.</p>
        <p>On Tkarsday afternoon and evettitig, a sennott and lecture were delivered in Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel, in aid of the Sunday school fund, the preacher being the Rev. J. H. Hopkins, Superintea- dent of the South London Wesleyan Mission. There was a fair attendance at the afternoon service. In the even- ing, the body of the chapel was filled. The subject of the lecture was "The bitter cry of outcast London." The chair was occupied by Mr. John Hartley. The lecture was an account of *he work of the Rev. J. H. Hopkins in Southwark and 1; reiondsey, of which he is superin- tendent, and where be has been working for five or siK years. This mission forms one of the great missions in 'London, the best known of which is the latest Central Mission, where the Revs. Hugh l'rice Hughes and Mark Guy Pearse are engaged. These missions have proved marvellous successes in every instance, and it was to give a story of his mission that Mr. Hopkins visited Warrington on Thursday. In opening his lecture, he said that he was glad to have the opportunity of telling his story at Ward ngton at the time when an old celleaguse and former superintendent, Mr. Kirtlan was stationed there. Although so much work Was done in the great missiona, still the mainstay of Met.hoclism was eircuit work. Coming to his work in Southwark, he said that when he was there five years ago, there was a chapel to seat 1,282 people, but there, were rarely more thau 10 people present at the services, buW now it WAS filled to overflowing. They had also taken a chapel in hexfield which was also crowded every Sunday, thanks to the energy of his colleague Mr. Meakin. The Sunday school Was likewise a great success. They had , taken Bermondsey Town Hall for Sunday afternoon ser- vices and this was as successful as the other two places. He explained the great success of the mission as due to care of the bodily as well as the spiritual need of the people. He had at present a scheme for which he re- quired £4,500 towards which £1,500 had been obteined. When this sum was raised he would be able to have a Sunday school large enough to hold the Children com- fortably. The lecturer gave a number of touching in- stances of the work of the mission which had come under his personal notice.—At the close of the meeting a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer and the chairman. A large sum was also promised for the mis- sion by members of the congregation. The meeting closed with the pronouncing of the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. S. LA.URENSON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. S. LA.URENSON.</p>
        <p>Many readers will regret to learn of the death of Mr. S. Laurenson, of Wilderspool-road, who has for some time been employed as book-keeper at the United Alkali Company's Offices, Widnes. Mr. Laurenson's death is all the more sad because of its suddenness. He was in good health apparently upon leaving work on Wednesday evening. During the night, however, he was taken ill and died about half-past eleven. He was about 50 years of age and leaves a widow and a number of children to mourn his loss. Mr. Laurenson was formerly secre- tary to the old Haybridge Iron Company, which carried on business in Dallain-lane some years ago. The deceased was chiefly known by the public of Warrington as a very enthusiastic advocate of pro- tection, and he carried on several ptthlic controver- sies on the subject.</p>
        <p>4 • , ,</p>
        <p>t:</p>
      </div>
