      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAUI ST RATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAUI ST RATES</p>
        <p>A GREIF STECCIEN BVSBAND.</p>
        <p>A middle-aged man, named Frederick Joseph 'Brown- ing, was charged at the Boreueli Court on Saturday, before Mr. John Surgese (in theetiair) snr. LSVoisey, with having attempted te conemit suicide by cutting. his *throat with a razor on Saturdeyethe 27tlaganuary.—The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the defendant was a-Ship Canal labourer, and dived in Ship-yard. For some considerable time prior to the date in question his wife was ill, having been dorrhnedaf a child. She died -on the23rdJanuary, leaving behind herher husband and two young chgeren. Defendant became very much de- pressed during the week, and followed his work as well as he could on the Thursday, rridayeand Saturday. On Saturday at noon, when he returned from his work, he inquired eery anxiously if a letter had come frem his sister. Be was told that none had come and he appeared 'very mush depressed. Be was seen to go along the yard hanging his head down, as ifthe were-in deep trouble. At 3 quarter-past two a. woman heard moanieg .proceeding from an outhotise, and looki eg irs elle -saw the defendant with blood issuing -from a weeinel in his throat. Assist- ance was procured and the man was taken into the hause, where he was attended by Dr.Tox, and was after- wards removed to the Infirmary. Be had been in that institution since. The man had a good character for being sober, steady, and industrious, and well-behaved' to his wife and family.—Defeadant's sister said she in- tended to look after defendant and his children for the future.—The magistrates, after addressing, a few en- couraging remarks to the defendant, discharged him. , ALLSGED THEFT OF A GLOM—P=I:II,IAR CASE. Joan Stokes, 343, Winwick-road, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, with stealing from the dwelling-house of Joseph Pilling, at 10.30 on, Saturday night, two clocks, valued 6d. The Chief Con-, stable said that Pilling was a greengrocer, reeiding in I Antrobus-street, ol Winevick-road. On Saturday, he ' and his wife went to Widnes hawking vegetables and on their return, Pilling went to the stable to put-up his- pony. leaving his wife in the house. The two clocks were in the house at the time. During this time, Ben- jamin Sheppard who lodged at Stokes' house, came into the house. Mrs. Pilling went outside and on returning the clocks were both gone. She reported the matter to the police some hours afterwards, and Inspector Bowen and Constable Evans went to Stokes' LIOUSO., and on get- ting inside, they heard the ticking of some clocks in the cupboard. They asked Stokes where he had got them' from and he replied that he had bought them from Mrs. Pilling for 9d. On taking the_ clocks to Filling's house, Mrs. Pilling at onee ideatified them, and denied selling them to Stelees.—Josepti Pilling, the complainant, said that he did not think the prisoner had anything to do with the theft. His wife had a drop of drink on the night in question, and as she had sold things before, she might have sold the clocks.—Margaret Pilling' the wife, also said that she did not think the- prisoner had stolen the clocks. She did not know whether she had sold them to him or not, although for anything she knew, ahe,might have done.—lnspector Bowen said that on Saturday night, the wife was very positive that she had not sold the clocks, or the police would never have taken any proceedings.—The magistrates decided to discharge the prisoner. ALLMGED OTTRAGE OM A LITTLM GIRL. At the Warrington Borough Court, yesterday, Walter Lee, a soldier, stationed in Galway, Ireland, was brought up on remand on the charge of committing an outrage upon a little girl named Olive Ethel Antrobus, aged seven years, slauehter of Mrs. Lee, Academy-st. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) prosecuted, and Mr. A. Browne defended. Mr. Whittle said that the prosecution was instituted ender the Criminal Law Amendment Act. The defendant was thebrotber of the little girl's stepl ether, and he had been staying at his brother's house ler some time previous to the time the offence was alleged to have taken place. On the night in question, the father and mother had gone to the theatre, and left a girl named Mills in charae of the house, and the daughter was also left at home. The defendant alter they had left, went upstairs to pack his box, and the little girl complained of being tired, and went to bed. While she was in bed, her uncle, it was alleged, committed the offeece. Nothing was said of the matter by the girl until about a week afterwards, when the defendant had gone back to his regiment. Ber mother then wee led from her appearance tie ask her what had happened and she told everything. Die. Peacecke and Starkey Smith examined the child,and the police were then communicated with, the consequence of which wes that the prisoner was brought from Gal- way.—Evidence was then given by the girl, On. Pea- cocks and Smith, the girl's mother, and several other -witnesses, after which the defendant was,cemmitted to the assizes. It should be mentioned that the defendant has a good conduct badge on his sleeve.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY —Before Mr. J. Fairelr Messrs. J. Geddes, and .1 A DESERTER. —Sattlll6l Mark Jugh (chairman), being a deserter from her M• ~ H. Fleming. ment, stationed at Jersey /Knox was charged with Wheatley proved the'- ,4esty's lit Yorkshire to Strangeways Ga Regi- , since the Bth January. P.C. • DRUNK AND P. AnS.—Tbe prisoner was remanded 5s and cost' • -.A ter await an escort. previous -isoonwatr.—Rdward Jefferys was lined . tor tiais (atm:me in Stoney-yard, at 5.20 the Jay. '-war.--Before Alderman Sutton. if ON WARRINGTON AGAIN.—John Warringtoa was charged with being a convicted thief found frequenting Dallam-lane, with intent to commit a felony at 11.25 the previous evening. P:C. Turner said -that he was on duty in Dallam-lane. and- saw Aire prisoner. climbi the yard door of Mr. Pendflikiry. Witness caught hold of his legs and. palled him down again and took him to the police station.—lnanswerto Mr. Sutton the prisoner said that he was getting his living as best he could. No, one would employ him, and the police were always watching him.—Mr. Siaten said that the police had very good cause to look after kim,and he wcntid cemmit prisoner for 14 days' bard labour. Warrington was taken to the cells vowing vertgeasce upon the police, telling them that he would "swing for it."; FRIDAY. —Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Ter."Gornall, Mr. J. W. tlayward, and Councillor Roberts. Gircurmy TO &amp; HORSt—William 'Crosdale, Rochdale,, was charged with cruelty to a horse by traveliling it while in an unlit state on FebruarySrd. EC. Corbett, gave evidence to seeilng the horse being attached to a. rope behind a furnitere van in Buttermarket-street. The horse was very lame, and witnessstopped the defendant and drew his-atteneion to the hone. He said that it had hart its tsot at Rainhill on the way from Liverpool to Warrington. He taid that he was goingtto Rochdale- Vile horse wastaleen out and detained -MU° General Wolfe.—P.C. Lewis. Inspectortuff, R.S:P.C.-A., and J. Dixon (veterinary surgeon), gr.veevidence in support of the chsage, and. magistrates ;fined defendant in -the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MIN olt CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN olt CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>llowDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Mr. J. W. Hayward, and Mr. S. Vi'riglit. DRUNIE AND DISORDERLY.—WaI. I,7thgoe, 33. Sharp- at., fined ss. • Peter Hank,inson and Mary Ann Rankin- son, 76, Cockhedge-lane, net: 53. ; James Muikearn,ann John Butler, 12. Percival's-fold, fined ss. and costs. DRUNK IN CHAUGE OF A HORSE AND CART.—Andrew Clarke, 12, Barry-st., was fined ss. and costs, for being drunk in charge of a horse and cab, in Bridge-st ,on Saturday night. P.C. Clarke proved the case. BRRACII OF THE PEACE—Thomas Baguley, Heath Side and Patrick McGrath, 35, Hill-st., were bound over for six months or seven days for fighting in Queen-st., on Feb. 4th. P.C.s Bell and Newey proved the case. The two defendants had been fighting together, McGrath's shirt being torn to pieces during the proceedings. TDESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. E. J. Guest, and Dr. S. Smith. THErr OF A SIIIRT.—Rose Ann Macdonald, a stranger in the town, was charged with stealing from Use shop of L. Craik, 38, Buttermarket-st., between twelve and one o'clock the previous day, a woman's black skirt value us. 6d. Prisoner pleaded guilty. From the Cilia Con- stable's statement it appeared that the prisoner went into complainant's shop and said she had come for a pair of stockings. The prisoner selected a pair, but afterwards said she had left her .money at home, and promised to call again. The skirt was afterwards missed and from inquiries it was ascertained that the prisoner had offered it in pledge at Mr.Jack3on's, Church-st.,who refused to have it. The prisoner afterwards pledged it for two shillings at Mr. Greaves. The prisoner was apprehended in Hall-st., in a state of drunkenness. The magistrates committed her to gaol for foueteen days hard labour. DRVICK AND DISORDEItLY.—Wm. Atkinson and his wife who said they came from Sowerby Bridge were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Sankey-st., the pre- vious night. The magistrat4 -discharged the prisoners on promising to leave the tiswn. Mrs. Outram, provi- sion denier. was fined 55. inoluding costs, for a similar ; offence in Lyme-street. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASE..—Mr. G. Riley nsosecuted in the Yellowing cases of irregular attendance at school. ' Thomas Chrimes. brewer, Portland-st., ss. ; Wm. Finch Pierpoint-st., two boys ordered to be birched ; Joseph Lightfoot, wiredrawer, Wakefield-st., 25. 6d. ; Wni. Mason, wiredrawer, Priestley-ct., 2s. 6d. ; Jos. Poul- ton, forgeman, Bewsey-read, 55. ; James Smith, forge- man, Winwick-road, 2s. ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>V</p>
        <p>'NEWTON PEtTY, SESSIRNS</p>
        <p>SAICADAT.—IIefore Mt. T. `O.- Grimlie Drayton (chair- man)., MessrS. J. E.:: Wood, and R. Stone. Zeutin.-19lichae1 Collinti -was fined 4.4. 6d. and costs for this offence at Win wit 4, on'the-fith inst., and Robert Wynne wastaned 2s. 61 (or a simitarnffeace at Lowton, on the-27th January. DRUNK AND Dtsommrs.v.—The fallowing were dealt with for this'effence: Jos. Cullen, at Newton, 28th ult.,; 525. 6d. and costs ;'John'Grady, at"Fewton, on the .15th1 July, 1814,55. and costs; William Tracey, on the 30th, September, 1893, 'ss..t.nd costs; Wm. lienyon, at Ray-. ' dock, ontlie22tid ult, 24. 6d. and costs; Martin Man- nine, fit liaydock, on the 29th ult., ss. and costs; Michael Owens and Michael Fin&lt;an, at Raydock. on the 29th ult., ss. anti costs ; 'Pe'iriclrLyons, at the same time and place2s. 13d. and costs. ANMHER BATCH el? DOG OVIVCRS FINHD.--,FOr having their dogs unmunled the following were. summoned: .BAmuel Deakiu, Newton, 75.6 i. ; Samuel Bent, at Tlur- tonwond. 104. ; Wm. Winstsn)ey, t Raydock, st.a. ; Adam Edwardson, at Golhorne;ls. 6d.; Win. Lowe. at tGelborne.ls. David Boutton. at Golborue, 7a. 4i.; John Stott, at Haydeck, dismissed; John Lawrenson, Paw-read, St. Releus, two dogs as large at Ilaydoc*,. -2s.td. FFOHTlNO.—lokwrence Dunn and 'James Noon' were charged with firbting at Geiberne on the 26 January. Dunn was dismissed And Nixon was bound over in t'the, sum of £5 to keep the peace for three months. TOACHERS•IN Tacanstv..—Milliarn Paul and .5..037.0tnan Hindley were charged ,kith poaching on the land of the evecutorsof the late Thomas Stone. on the 18th danuarr, in search of conies. Mr:-Rtuart represented the -defend- ants. Evidence in support of the charge was riven by Henry Squires who said he saw Paul shoot a rabbit which was i7-a field of the complainant's. After he had etot the reloti.t Hindley got over the fence anti brought' the rabbit out: Witness stopped Hindley • and took the' rabbit from him.—Mr. Stuart for the defence coutended that -the defendants shot the rabbit on the highway, and that there was no actual trespass of land in pursuit of -game.—The magistrates fined the defendants s. and ‘COStS.</p>
