      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>'RUNCORN rErTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>'RUNCORN rErTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY . —Before*Dr.-Steele and Mr. F. J. Iltermaii: TRF,I7 OF COAL AT BUTTON.—Sarah Jauion, of Fred- •sham, Was charged with having on the Ist inst,l stolen A.Otllns. of coal, value:Bd., from Sutton Dock., tile pro- perty of Mr. Philip Speakman, J.P.. C.C.—The woman said there were eight of a family, and her husband had .done no work for 16 weeks. They were " starving and .clernming:”—Superintendent Atherton said he had bad to put men in plain clothes to watch this dock to detect this sort of •Vbing. Times had been very hard, and he did not suppose that Mr. Speakman wished to press the case. All that was wanted was that these people should keep away from the dock.—The Bench bound the wo- man over to appear fori.:;udgment when called upon.. COWARDIX ASSAULT &gt;ON A WOMAN.—John Morris. fish dealer, was summoned for assaulting Mary Jackson, keeper of a milk shop in Mersey-st. Mr. Burton ap- peared forthe complainant, and said on Tuesday, the inst.,the defendant,entered the shop and asked Mrs. Jackson if she would have a drink, although he bad no previous acquaintance with her. She refused, and or- dered him to leave the shop, and she was holding open lothe door, when he strudk her about the head and body, making her nose and with bleed. He got hold of her hair and pulled 'a considerable quantity out. In self- defence the woman struck him on the head with a pint cup. The complainant's zigter went for thspolice, and the defendant ran away. A lodger in the house named Rowan, could not releasethe woman from the defend- ant's grip.—The defendant said he never struck the woman, but he seized her after she had struck him with the cup. Ile lost a pint of blood.—The defendant was fined 40s. and costs El 6s. td. DRUNK AND •DisouDgma.--Samiael Walton, John Ed- wards, Frank Lightfoot, Mary Fahey (two charges), were each 'fined for drunkermess.—Mary Ushin and Emma Ford were charged with using profane language in Mersey-st. Each defendant was fined :53. and costs.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TIIIIESDAY.—Before Mr,T. S. Timis and Councillor W.</p>
        <p>W. Gossage. DRUSIC AND DISORDERL-T.—Fnr this offence the follow• ing were each fined : Ann Shea, Arthur MePartland, James O'Donnell,John Lee, Thomas Connington, Michael Devine, Thomas Curley, and Annie 50ne.5. For commit- ting a breach of the peace the following were bound over to be of good behaviour : Thos. Devine, Nickolas 'Wailing, Thos. Robinsoa, Joseph McCormick, and Sarah Walsh. CHIMNEY ON Finv,.—A fine of as. and -costs was impos- ed upon Thomas Darlington, Wright-street, for this offence. Fourpant OBSTRUCTION.—Robert obeats, John Whe- lan, Robert Whitehead, James Andrews. and Andrew Brown, were each fined 25.43 d. and costs fcr obstructing the footpath. UNMUZZLED DOGS.—A fine of is. and costs 7s. 6d.,was imposed upon each of the tfollowin, for allowing their dogs to he at large unintizzled :—Joseph Woods, George Ta.nsey, James Lunt, George Whitby, lifted Midwood, Annia Sutcliffe, Wm. Leatherbarrow.; Abel Smith, and Harry Hilton. TOCNG THIEVES.—AIbert Dillon, aid Alfred Ellis, living at 1C and 48, Terrace-read, were charged with having on the 12th inst., stolen 51. from tile shop till of Mary Ann M'Donald, confectioner. -Church-st. On Monday evening, about six o'clock, alion went iato the shop in the absence of tlit prosecutrix, and taking the money from the till,held it up and showed it to Ellis and another lad, who were outside. He gave Ellis 31 and had the rest of the money. Ellis was fined ss. or seven days and Dillon was e immitted for 14 days. .SHOP ROBBERIES.—Win. Gyx, a rougla looking man.of no settled abode, was charged with having on the 3rd February, stolen a bed quilt value 53. 6d.4 the property of Horatio Gred, Waterloo-road, four pairs Lf stockings, value is. 6J., the property of Wm. Millor,Waterloo-road, and one jersey, value 28. 91., the property of Thomas Wood, Waterloo-road. The prisoner pleaded guilty, and he was committed to the Quarter Sessions for trial. IitiSTAIIPED 'MEASURES AT THE CATHOLIC BALL.— James O'Hogan, Liverpool, was summoned for having in his possession unstamned glasses, O'Hogan is the landlord of the Crown Hotel, Duncan-st., Liver- pool, and en the 5,11 mat, supplied the refreshments at the Catholic Ball in Widnes Drill Hall. Mr. Kilpatrick, inspector of weights and mea.surrs,paid a visit and found that two measures (a half gill and a quarter gill), were unstamped, and also ten dozen half pint measures was in a like condition. The whole of the measures were ac- curate. A fine of ss. and costs 123., was imposed.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT MEDICAL AID SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Committee of the above Society was held at the depot, Queen-street, on Wednesday afternoon. The Rev. T. R. Matthews presided, and there were also present Revs. J. Carson, E. W. W. Kaye, F. J. Lennon, T. Wynne-Jones, Drs. Meuncey and Valentine, Mesas. E. T. Evans, E. Driug and A. reake (secretary). Apologies for absence were received from Rev. J. Allatt and Mr. J. E. Worsley (treasurer). The minutes of the last meeting were read and confirmed, and the accounts were adopted. The Secretary reported that there was a continued increase in the use of the Society's appliances, scarcely a day baying passed during the year,with- out an application. The articles were generally in good order and repair, and additional appliances were required. The Secretary reported that he had received £l5 from the Tradesmen's Soiree Commit- tee, towards the district nurse fund: It was pro- nsed by the Rev. T. R. Matthews, and seconded by the Rev. F. J. Lennon, and warmly supported by the Rev. T. Wynne-Jones, that the thanks of the Committee be forwarded to the tradesmen's (km-</p>
        <p>rpia</p>
