        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. BROTIG 11TON.</p>
        <p>The Warrington tears journeyed to Broughtcs en Sat- urday to meet the " woodert-spoonists " of the league in very disagreeable weather. There were oaty about 300 spectators present as the rain wa3 falling in torrents at the coramenoetnent of the • game. The visitots were slaort-handed. John Bate and Barber being aw ay,tand thes; local fifteen were represented by the same fifteen which has fought so hard to raise the club from the low level ' which-at present is the case.„ Broughton kicked o' towards the city goal, and capital returns by Carey and Evans at once gave the visitors the advantage. Smith repeatedly cleared, atid though the • •gacialluctwated between the half and qtarter flags, thesplay generally' was conined to the tome triton. Thorniley and Dakin ably led the way to the line, hely to see Piercy ss,ve,. and then Cravemessayedto getaway, blitlitiled tie .acconfit for Potts. pine pasting by Bete„ Clark, *Potts, and Evans saw Broady makiara bolt bid for the line: being held a yard inside. • The visitors Continued tti attack, and looth Tay.lor.and Turner were within in 3.1.4'7 of scoring, Hessen saving-once in rattling style. Pine forward work by Auty was well backed up ivy. Shepherd.: and Reesom promptly bedt.the lecl; btck. a:pOndero ire kick landing the oval at the Broughton 7.5 flag. The • . Wavringtoniens again made a hold hid for a major point and a try seemed a certainty when E vaus .zot clear, but he kteld the oval a little too long, when Pctta and Carey might have turned the moveitivnt to adTantage. Tate • again threw to Evans, but this tune thevass wapoled • ty the elements, and an effective dribs:lo by Cru-ven .for the ArAtime saw Broughton . in tie WarriaAton Equarters. It only 'required:the least energy on ticapart ' ot the 'visitors to regain the lost gretwd.and this being speedily forthcoming, good Work. by Jolley, Taylor, and , Turner -soon had %he "wooden spooni44" 01)39 more</p>
        <p>hard on tili3 defence. Smith was speedily floorsttin try- ing to clear, and then a sharp_ burrs; of passiugvoy the Warrington backs saw Potts aocepting in true style, and a vigorous effort landed him a scorer nearer tie..., pOsts, the-Same player augmenting the 'try by two -points. Fallowing this reverse came some ,mpital a.ll-round play by the Broughton backs, and bactiresi up br'the• earnest play of the forwards,the Warrinegonians were/3r a few minutes takenierback by the. prrs:itency of Ursughten's efforts to notch somethit4 sutatantial.ly fer Warrington the tackling was e.ccurate, forr-a 'capital round of passing started by Mahnock and ended up by Pioref might have resulted disastrously. aroady an 4 grimed th 'noels in true style; bb auetiber sequence of transfers saw Ttrraer actirely•engaged mwietting the movement. 11..assom get the oval and a, billia.nt-run by 'Jolley wus followed hy'73ate kick- ing4ldiciumity, and 'Warrington again renewed the at- tack: Next followed a series_of exchangea ort the re-</p>
        <p>serve touch, which ended to gratis' credii. and Manock: being penalhked for too strict attention towaris Bate.,7- the visiting forward§ went down in a body and matters'. .loobed painted, indeed when Hassall .77 11,8 floored-. on is line. A spell of manotcmons loose fer- ward play followed,,. ind though Shvhk.rd ' par- tially cleared, a clean tit of wore by CAstii again gave his side the advantage: The hoiii.! team aefensied well, and the Visitors' passing at times being ill-tined a long pass intended tor Broady fait-Ad to reach him by yarda, aud ample opportunity was affe2ded Craven to effect relief. Heesom man well, and thec,Bate and Clark passed well out to the three-guar- ters,!but the.chances when accepted had no finish, and the liroughton line deservedly escaped. Near the close Piercy made.= excellent dribble and crossed the hat'- way itine, but Berry, Donohue, and Dakin wit had the Broughton men back again on their lines, and Smith was held, the latter incident bringing half-time, with the</p>
        <p>Bcore.---IWarri2gton, one gealißr-oug,hton, nil. On re- suming the V4i.rringtonians started their aggressive work from the,onset, and after:the opening exchanges Broady made a grand effort to get through, but the holding nature of the turf prevented Min giving a true exposition of his capabilities. As it Was the. Warringtonians at- Lacked in strong force, and then the whole body of the visiting forwards rushed the oval to the line, Jolley, Nevhls, and 'Taylor, leading, only to find the persistent ill-lack still ding to them, the ball rolling into touch.in- goal. Bate atoliDonohue 'returned to the attack soon after-the drop-out, but alucky flying kick changed the venue to half-way. A sharp pass from Bate to Broady saw the latter again make a bold bid, but luck was against him. The Mareingtoniatis got to the line once more, but a miserable exhibition in their finish- ing touches sent 1.-43 groans of disappointment from the smell army of -spectators. Whiteley intercepted a gags, and his progress was not interfered with until he had crossad the half-way, line, whilst Broady in trying to clear 'had ids kick charged down by Gibson, the latter being injured in 'this latest essay. Fine dribbling by Joiley and Nevins saw Smith again tested, and though the Eroughton custodian was found wanting, his efforts compared •favourably whea placed alongside those of Warrington in trying for substantial points. Thorniley and Nevins dribbled to the line, and the latter hid the hardest of luck, the ball being kicked from underneath him when in the act of grounding. :Rain now came down in torrents, and though the play was continued little or no interest was centred in the game. Once Broughton got dangerously near the line and Alannock almoA got in.; tut this.was the only tinsii the home team were dang,erous, for the Warrington for- wards rushed in a body to the other end, and numerous</p>
        <p>chances were allowed to escape. Jolley made one fine run near the close, but like all preceding efforts it came to nought, and the finish came with the score—Warring- ton, one goal (fivepoints) ; Broughton, nil. Teams :-- KVarrrington : F. Heesona, full buck ; P. Carey, C. Potts, W. Broady and J. W. Evans (captain), three-quarter backs; James Bate and H. Clark, helf backs; J. Joll9y, W. Nevins, Fair. Turner, J. Taylor, G. Dakin, J. Berry, .1. T. Thorniley and T. Donohue, forwards. Broughton: J. Smith, full back; G. H. Piercy (captain), S. Hassell, W. Whiteley, and C. B. Craven, three-quarter backs; S. Mannock and D. Roberts, half backs ; A. L. Auty, F. Frost, T. Gibson, J. E. Shepherd, F. F. H. Stott, J. Munro, R. Tomlinson and J. Waltoa, forwards. Ifeutr.4l touch judges, Messrs. J. Turner and J. S. Turnbull; referee, Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes.</p>
        <p>lIHNCORN v. OLD EDWARDIANS.—The second match of the season between these organiz ttiens was played at Runcorn on Saturday during the windy weather, before a small attendance. The following sloes turned out : Edwardians : Callicott, back ; Adatyis, Sherrey, Nie,ol, and James, three-quarter backs ; English and Goodman, half backs; Morris, Slater, Keenan, Burgess, Denials, Turner, and Steele, forwards. Runcorn : Houghton, back ; Warder, Richardson, Robinson, and 13rewn,three- quarter backs; Faulkner and Abrams, half-backs ; Faulkner, Hughes, Farmer, Reece, Stulehs, Antrobus, and Moores, forwards. The cAptains decided to divide the time in tour fifteen minutes owing to the wind. Faulkner won the tess,and Adams started against a strong win, Warder running and finished with a strong punt. The pressure was maintained, and owing to the oil-side tactics, Runcorn were awarded a penalty kick, which Warder made into a goal. After the restart Abrams ran, but Adams and English tackled safely. The subsequent play was very keen, the home forwards carrying the scrimmages. Tne high wind interfered with the attempts to kick out, and made matters dull for the spectators. At the end of the quarter the teams changed ends, and with the wind the visitors held the grnuod strongly, James and Nicol trying up the home defence. Several attempts to score were Oaken on by Richardson and Warder, but the passing being at fault all chances were neutralized until Faulkner got way and passed to Antrobus, who got over, the goal failing' . Mr. J. T. Brunner, M.P., at this junction catne en the stand and was received with cheers. tip to the interval the score was not augmented. Half time score :—Run- corn, 1 goal one try; Edwardians, nil. After the inter- val ths play continued lfl a most desultory fashion, the Old Edwardians showing improved torm and playingta strong defence. Robinson should have scored under the posts, but delaying allowed Abrams to hold him eutside. Some passing by Warder, Richardson, and hobinson caused some excitement, Sherry and James getting the last man on the line, and it remained for J. Faulliner to score, the goal kick failing. The general play of the Runcorn team was below usual form, still the attack was kept up. Richardson made a dashing run right behind, and Houghton kicked the goal. Final result : Runcorn, two goals twc tries ; Edwardians, one try.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN' T. OLD EDIVARDIANS " A " TEAMS.—On Sat- urday Runcorn " A " team journeyed to Birmingham, to play the return engagements which took place on the county ground at Edgbaston, before a moderate gate. Both teams were fully represented, especially the Edwardian, ho had the bell, of Prosser, Nicol, and Tap lie, of the first team. The following were the players :—Runcorn: Butterworth, back; W. Gayter, Gregory and Myers, three-quarter hacks; Robinson and H ollingwortk, half-backs Webbe, Clarke, Moores, J. Pandlebury, C. Pendlebury, W. Evans, J. Evans, F. Gayter and Shaw, forwards. Edwardian' : Cox, hack; Walford, Nicol and Dent, tares-quarter backs; Prosser and Lovitt, half-backs; Taplia, Roe, Brookes, Teeson, Fletcher, West, C•chafer, Burman and Bolton, forwards. In the end, Runcorn added another victory to their already long list, the final result being—Runcorn A, 2-2-9; Edwardians A, nil.</p>
        <p>BLACKLBY V. WIDNES.—At Blackley. The home seam started:well, but a mistake by one of their backs let in Barber, who pas,ed to BrEnnan, the latter scoring an easy try for Widnes.. Blackley played well throughout, and it was with difficulty they were kept from scoring on several occasions, Ashton especially having hard luck. Radford droppel a fine goal for Blackley, who won by one dropped goal to Widnes one try.</p>
