        <p>WagretvcrrOx v. BIRIMAIX.—PIared at Arpte-5. on Satiarday. • Birkde.le had manila...l to best the horr.e club away., that being the first thsethe Warrington clot') had suffered defeat. Squally weather• prevailed on Shttirday but •dthervrise the eonditioces were•favourable. During :the.first half Warrington defended the railway goal. Both ends were visited. anci then the home ,for wards woithed their way into the visitors' territory. 4Sere Nor- man gained possession, and after a short run passed to WhO soared wit n a grand shot. 'Poe visitors played bard 'atter this, and afteetvans andrather had ditoinguished themselves, the visitors' go.ilimOuth was , ; the scene of •action. Here one of the visitors handled , the ball, and a perialtvlkiat :being awarded 'Connor. put 1. or. a second ...sal. Aiterthis the-visitors' left inside wino given a fine &amp;mom., but he shot over, and vane almost?, immediately afterwards was • siting in his usual ittyie.:l The home team now made another invasion, and 'shot right 'across the goal =oath. Conner turbhar 'merited the score in ,vir.iital stie.. the visitors' front ,zank was now showing 'op :tnely, and Kenyon ant', Williamson got through an amount of work in capititi tstyle. Flowever, Leiter -wls given a chance, of whichhe availed himself by Inating Forrest with a geod stet. Ilalf-timsarri wed witkont any further scering. On re- starting the- visitor' " right wing-rnade' traeks past an- ttionv,but Mather averted danger ln somevrhat ttrilliant fashion. Then the .home loft wing 'teak play irstorthe visitors' half, and a•cortier was conceded.. Fantle/1y put the ball nicely is the goal mouth, itd, Connor headed his fourth goal through. 'Norman net-ct shot threngh, but the whistle badigone, for &amp; free kick against the visi- tors. The ball was sent out to Williamson, and,that player scored with a good shot. Shorkly before time Connor again sorest, the final score beitig-Warrincton, 6'goals ; Birkdate,U.</p>
        <p>But, as I was saying, madam, wc are a •arsek nearer ttiat consummation devoutly to be shunned. Another 'week gone; at this rate when wiil my chapteron gratitude be written. and that on stains and thit other on humbugs'?, Ay'! ye humbugs of the weed this procrastination has given ye another, week's respite from the pilloryTif not total annihi- lation. Caveat „I-fur/bin. We all know from our "Dine Williams" with whem time arables, &amp;c., but the "Swan of'.A.4en "does not tell'us the great difference between the retried and the single man in this :respect. Now I 'hold the Benedict to 1!1; eingularly blessed in this matter of fugatices time. To see this, my dear , Madam, we have enty.to inark the huge difference in tiler home-cola...hag fro= business—always busi- ness thongh its .(unregistered) office .neay have been a club-at the" -wee sea&amp; hours sfyont the twal.' l&amp;et us assume thrit it it the earks and cares of this weary world that Weigh so .heavilyupon both the 'Benedict and tactielor as they wend their devious way to their respective 'places ',of abode. After Overcoming certain defgenitieB i me.tinglhaker; ' with the leteli--;-mark the °outcast of the reception accorded to ,each.. Nut nn! let 'vs rather draw a veil over Ow cold and venal reception; given to tke un-Benedict-ene and turn to participate in the de- light experie'need by his more fortunate Convive.'</p>
        <p>Pesinirra Faressiss Settoor--This mat &amp; was played on the ground of the latter on Saturday, in squally weather. The School wassfairly represented, while the visitors were without the services of Iliott Whitfield and Roswell Reynolds kicked eff for the School, and soon the Visitors' defence was tested, the home side having ,hard lines in not scoring ia the first minute. Play became eves, but the high wind spoiled good football. Bowyer opened the score for Ponkstla after half an hour's play,but this was the enly point registered in the first half. rseatartiag, the visitors payed wall to- gether, and Bowyer put on two more goals to tha visi- tors' score, while Reynolds, ssfter a brilliant runsscored for the School. The game became fast and exalting ; Lewis and Williamson played a grand defence, put their forwards on the attack and,caused the home full back to kick through his own goal. Whea I.ime was called the score Stood renketh, four goals:; Friend's School, one goal. , PArnsit CIETTRaII 'N. ALT:. faINTL—ThiS match was played on the ground of the-former cn Saturday. The heme team kicked aff, and after a few minutes' even play,Lawrencd with along shot, scored the first goal for the Parish Church. Bradshx.w Lud Nicholson soon. added another two goals. Ms visitors now began to press, and from ecornergor•cl their first got]. I'lay wao Pow confined to the visitersr,:quarters and after sone neat play by the battle fotvards Garner scored. Half time waseallodothe :score, being Parish Church, four goals ; All Saints, ono goal. Soon after the restart the visitors, now having the wind in their favour, scored three goals in quick succession, but Titling got away with a fine dribble and scored for the Parish team. The Saints again began to press, but Daintitl: in goal saved several times in fine style, until the visitors were stewed a penalty, from which they scored. From the kick off the Parish forwards took the WI up the fleld and Bradshaw from a lbw; pass by Garner odored, the same player a few •milattas later- rushing the visitors' goalkeeper through before hecould got the ball away. Time was soon after- wards called, the score being—Parish Church, 7 goats; All Saints, 5 goal's. WAREpIGTOIC GRAMMAR, SORQOL v. KIN" Scvlo" CHawrea.---The above match was played on the &amp;hoot- ground, Arpley, on Saturday ;afternoon. The final re- sult was Grammar School four oats., Zing's Sahool,,nil. Tie goala were shot b C. P. Gougn, Pier Pois" R. Silcock. and F. Gornall. WARRINIIITOM BASE BALL BESERVE /RAM T. r ADetATE IST THAM.—This match took place on Saturday last on the ground of the former, Gorsey-lano. Padgate winning the toss played with the wind in their favour, scoring 2 goals to Ease Ball nothing, up to early on in the secasnd half when the home team soored 3 goals in fine style. The game qgded in a win for the Base Ball club by 3 grialslgainst"Padgate 2. • ST. PETERS T. Tun STANDARD.—This match waS played on the ground of the latter and resulted in a victory for St. Peters, W. Gandy, If: Gandy and J. Worrall being the scorers. Final' result :—St. Peters, 3 goals'; The Standard, O.</p>
        <p>' Ye gods Icirbo p re,3i e over matters matrimonial, and tlibti" sweet little elle-rid) that sittest up aloft" and condectest the coniugt!te conilubialities,saphow his patient meek-eyed mate opens her armsocand her lips) to'rebeive the long eapected one: ! Tell,too, how Stle GOOtkIS his spirit,nerturb.ed by the harass of the world—Lhow her voicentte awthe cOoing.fof the cushat orr: the Woods—spreads O''er his soul ,aealm delight, and is sung to sleep ,by the lullaby of her love.* The sight and' sound•of cool waterel to the thirsty soul in the desert nor the sweet sammer song of birds is to be compared with her warbl- ings on -such an occasioa I And yet such is the con- trary. nature of man, thati have actually known in- stances where even this was accounted as a marital inlliction—an added evil to life. But„ surelyiSiadam, such men are unworthy such spouse 4 I .</p>
        <p>;Yes ! Iv.quite agree with yoti,Pir„' this is a grave question, an absorbing question,lu).l of abstraction, and if I don't mend my .pace it will be as difficult to get over as the sth Proposition o the 1. Booli, of Euclid and as empty as the celebrated ,XIV. chapter of the veracious, it vacillating h istory of "The Life and opinions of Tristram Sher tdy, gent." But get on I will, despite all -temptatio n to digression. Ye powers of industry, sobriety and application de- fend me, nor let me ado( another s stone to the Tar- tarean pavement—if Hadeuis pave d with good in- tentions, let us consider the jab d one, and that it will not require any materials fr cm Ta 3 to com- plete it.</p>
        <p>" That was an excellent sermon or yes, rester- day" said Eusebias—upon the text —" .ry people are destroyed for lack of knowled; e.""" I -really thiik with you, Yorick, that most of the silt:add sorrow of the world is caused by our lack6of I mow- ledge' "And yet "—interposed saw f ather " of the fruit of the knowledge-giving tree man wes forbid- den to eat." " Providence Moves iis my,st(rious ways," quoted my Uncle Toby, in a subdued roice. Quite so," said my father, " very myteriusly, in ,this case—but the truth is "---" What is toutl t?"— interjected Eusebius, following the le ad of I- Date. "what is truth r " Truth," said-my fat 13 er, -"is'. that _which we see through our own spectacles. It :is the name in which we baptise all our own comeipti iens; and it varier with the climate, with the la:tit-ad( t and longitude, but -mere especially with the tire,, 'for truth is largely a matter_ of Chronology;- wisa at -was established truth to, our forefathers is, to us. _d :ises- tablished error. They thought the earth to la Ban immovable plain, with the sun, moon and.sta r s, as accessories, making what we now know; to b e bun possible journeys round it—to minister to the t on , venience of one class of its inhabitants—the lot d, s of creation. Appearances are so deceptivo,and men Ih...'ere long upon the face of the earth ere they,-lika 3 the, wrecked Sinbad upon the back of kis whale—la n and it to be in motion, and •not the centre of all moi i on. Truth then.shifteci her quarters some 93,000,0 C a miles, and from being geocentric became ht lio- centric."</p>
