      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HIFT OF • MACH.</head>
        <p>HIFT OF • MACH.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk read the following communication: I Widnes,. January 23rd, 1814. , Dear Mr. Mayor. Now that the Corporation through your generosity possesses a Mayoral Chain, I think it only consonant . with the ,dignity of the municipality that it should possess also a mace. This want, with the permission of the Council, It will afford sae much pleasure to supply.—Yours faith- fully, T. So-Tr. oi; Timm's. The Wershipful,the Mayor of Widnes.—The Deputy Mayor moved that the offer of Mr. beloaapted and that he be thanked for his gener- ohs, gift.—Alderman Wareing seconded.—Carried. s,:', POST OFFICB ACCOIIODATION. . In reply to the, memorial from the Corporation' ' Mr. 3. S. Gilliat, M.P., who presented it, had received the fol- • lowing reply "General Post Office, London, 3rd Feb., 1894. Sir,—With reference to the memprial from the Corporation.of Widnes, which accompanied 'your letter of the' 21th Ult., I am directed by the Postmaster General to state that the Post Oce premises in that town are provided not by this Department but by the Postmaster, who receives a suitable allowance for (ace rent to en- able him to do so. Authority has iust been given for raising this allowancelo enable the Postmaster to pro- vide a new and improved office in the Victoria-road, which,:Mr- Morley is assured, will fully meet the re- quirements both of the Seriice and of the. public.—l am; sir; your obedient servant, G. W. Smitra.—.l. S. Gilliat, Esq.,M:P." 4 ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HIGHWAY MATTERS.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAY MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Highway Committee, after a consultation with the builders, resolved that the byelaws relating to new streets and 4uildings be sulamitted to the Local Govern- ment BearcOor-provisional approval. The SurVeyor had presented a special report with regard to the ventilation of the sewers itt the Mill Brow and Halton View districts of the 'borough. Further consideration was deferred until next meeting. Mr. 4. S. Sinclair, borough sur- veyor., having appliedlor an increase of salary, it waA resolved that the same be not entertained.—Mr. Christie moved the adopticn of the minutes, which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>HEALTHMITTME. - • MIS Health Committee reported that they had received an application for an increase of salary from Miss Mill- burn, matron at the Infectious Diseases Hospital. It was resolved that tbe same be not entertained. The Medical Officer reported that during the Oast month there were 122 births and.s3 deaths, being at the respective rates oi 48.:.8 and .21-2 per 1,000. The , deaths included 18 children under one year and 4 children between 1 and 5 yeara. Seventeen cases of infectious disease had bet.rt reported.—The.rnieutes were pasaed, on the motion of .Councillor</p>
        <p>• OS AND WATEE.• • The/Gas and Water Committee reported- that the mean rainfall for the month had been 1..805 *OS. The illu- minating power of the gas during the mouth , had aver- aged 1828 candles. An application had been received , from Mr. Fred Thorpe,assistant ma,nager,Gas and Water -works for adincrease of salary. ResOlved thathis salary be increased to £l3O per annum.—Alderman Thrimis thoved the acceptance of the minutes.—Alderman seconded.-7-Counciilor Shuttleworth pointed out that there had been eight . or nine applications for in- creases orsafary, aocl he rruved that a small Committee be appointed to consider the whole question of 'salaries Riad for,maxiinuin salaries.—AldermanWilliamsiin asked if .heicculd withdraw from seconding the motion, and the Toviridlerk ruled that he', could not, but he 'could ' vote. egainstlt„—The Town Clerk 'ruled the amendment Out arirder, and Couneillor ShUttleworth then'mpted that the minute be 'referred , back.—Councillor Gough seconded and said the stim proposed was L'lo more than the former assistant manager received.:—Councillor ,Bul- lough saidyThorpe was quite entitled to the the advance. He suggested the idda of fixing maximum salaries.— Aidehric,n Timmis in reply,- stated that Thorpe had more work and Larder responsibility than the previous assist- ant. The works Were larger. and during the past twelve months or tiid years, :Thorpe had carried oniithe gas works while Mr. Carr was superintending special work.. He had proved himself a capable official and worthy c.f an increase: The minutes were passed by 11. votes to 8, i)EOPOSHD EiTENSION OF THE TOWN HALL : SUDGFISTEH</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>zxpEyorrußn or £1,200.</head>
        <p>zxpEyorrußn or £1,200.</p>
        <p>At a' special meeting of the General Purposes and ' Parliaine.ntary Committee held on January 19th,. Mr. Francis .11. mime, architect, Liverpool, attended before the ccinimittee and produced the following draft plans, ; viz. ~plan (A) showing completed Town Hall with Coun- cil -Chamber as at present, with additional space given by proposed recess to match the recess now existing on ! the north side, together with rider showing alternative scheme with new Council Chamber And accommodation for Mayor and Tessa Clerks and other offices theteunder. --Plan (Bs showing proposed mode of providing for the present purposes of the Corporation, according to parti- culars furnished to him by the borough surveyor in accordance with the instructions ef,this committee. Mr. Holrne klso read a detailed re/36ft prepared by him explanathrysef the plans wad the mode of utilising the !" existing buildings as shown thereon, and estimating the cost Olthe.sehemes as follows :—Plan (A) withouttaddi- ,tions shown on rider £ll,OOO, additions shown on rider 5..':!.1,260, clod( tower £B5O, total £12,110. Plan (13) with- out rider RAMO, rider £1,2130, heating apparatus £l5O, total &amp;AO, Mr. Holme having withdrawn, the cont.' mittee discussed the Several plans and report, and the farther consideration of the matter was adjourned. At ' asopecial meethass on the 25th January the committee again .met, and the Town Clerk having read the resolu- tion of the Town Council, dated 14th February, 1893, instructinethis committee to inquire and report to the ; Council as to what additions or alterations are required as to the Town Hall ,Buildings for' the purposes of the Corporation, and as to the desirability of provid- ing, in connection therewith, suitable assembly rooms and other accommodation for the public it was resoLved—that the whole of the Dims be submitted to the Council. In conclusion, the ccmmittee. for the reasons set out, recommend the adoption by the Council of an.entire scheme similar in character to the one shown .upon,plan A, with riders thereto.—Alderman Wareing moved .the acceptance of the report. —Councillor Christie seconded.--Couneillor Wright said he had been asked by the Prcreerty Owners' Association to present a memorial en their behalf against any further extension of the Town 11 ac the present time.—The Deputy Mayor" said in the ordinary course this ought to have been sent to the Mayor, and been presented under the head of Mayors' 'ehmmunications. It Was creating a precedent, but he would take the responsibility and ask the Town cork to read the petition. The petition drew attention to tho fact that the trade of the town was not in a satis- factory condition; that an increase of 4d. in the pound cm the rates over last year, fell very heavily on property owners who had sustained loss through the coal strike, that the techinical schools at a cost of £lO,OOO was a large undertaking at the present time without adding the extension of the Town all.—Mr. Wright said the petition had about 247 or 248 signatures ; and he emp!4- asiaed the sentiments expressed.—Councillor Christie endoraed and was discussinr,,Y the general question when the Chairman said the proposal was merely to adopt the report. Plans would have to come before the Coun- cil whyrevar it was proposed that anything should be done.—Councillor Bullough prsposed this amendment (drafted by the Town Clerk) "That the minutes be con- firmed, with this addition, that while accepting and thanking the Committee for their report with respect to the Town Hall extension, the Council do not at the present time see fit to take any steps thereon or to ,e,ive any instructions in reference thereto.—Councillor Davies seconded.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>•‘</p>
        <p>fr</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH BATES : GRATIFYING REDUCTIOW. The Finance Committee suhtnitted their estimate for the year ending 25th March, 1895, and in moving its adoP- tion, Councillor Owens said this amount proposed to be raised was equivalent to a rate of 13. 4:1. in the) E,which was 301. less than last year. About 4d. in the £ was all that was required for the purp3ses of the municipility. the School Board and .police requiring a shilling in. the £.</p>
        <p>TIM SALARIES QUESTION. The Finance Committee reported that they ba3 received applications for increases of salatl from MessrP. Samuel Boyle, borough account- ant ; James Woodend, chief clerk ; Charles Whitfield', collector; Isaac Sankey, clerk; and David Martine clerk. The Committee have resolved that consideration be deferred pending the consideration and settlement' by the Committee of a scale of salaries fon. the whole or the officials in their,department. Councillor Owen Sole the Committee carefully dealt with the matter.anc) &amp;lank conCluded that at present they could notentet# tam n the increisee. They thought the applications lW some instances were meritorious and redolved to earls take into conaideratioe a scale of salaries, so that a- maxinaurnealary for each official could be,arrived at- Other committees might with advantage emulate the Finance Committee in their action.—Councillor llodg- kinson seconded the adoption of the minutes.=Carried•</p>
        <p>• SUNDAY OPENING OF TRH TREE LIBRARY. . • Couneillor.Wright moved the adoption of the Librarf and Technical Instruction Committee's Minutes, in Wbief was recorded the result of the proposal to open the 'rena: ingroom arid library 'on *ladays.—Councillor O'Donbell seconded the motion. He had done his best to have tbt institution open on Sundays, and he could promise bo_At" the Council and the public that he should try again he had no fear of the result.--Councillor Quinn asked he could again raiie the question by way of an amend" inent, and the TOWn Clerk replied that it 'would be better considered on notibe of motion.—The mitfutes• weie- 513". proved.</p>
        <p>ROUSES LET IN LODOINOS. Councillor Neil Moved the acceptance of byelawS re" lating to houses let in lodgings, and said there v 700,51 Ideation to aet unjustly or harshly.-oouncillor seconded.—Councillor Quinn objected that the byelasl_w were too stringent and weuld be the means of drawieV, wdgqrs from the dwellings into the common houses and that numbers of widows Would thus ler their means of livelihood, and would become. a burdela on the rates. He moved that they be referred back foir further consideration.—Alderman Williamson monde"' —Councillor Davies supported the amendment as a Pr!: pertTowner.—lt was explained that the byelaws • Or"' applied to houses let at ss. a week or under.--The De- puty MO,yoi said when a family 'rented a house and SO - let half to anether'fainily it was monstrous ttiarAte' landlord should be made responsible.-Councillor kinson said that there .was-no hardship in asking a to keep his hOuse clean.—Alderman Wareing said.tnere Werominy houses that the byelaws wOuld affect valich were AtEhy in the extreme and hot beds ' of disease."' Councillor Gough supported the •.byelaws. Ho biomes in Waterloo-road where there had been 24 indiates- ' and it was quite ushal f0r.12 people to live in the ?eget houses.—Councillor Bullough.said he had visited sore: of houses in his ward and he, could certainly say:,thaa the ceilings, the walls, the floor and the woman were a : of one colour.—A Counzillor : And what colcolourlva° : that 2—Councillor Bullough : Black. (Laughter.)—lu tryelaws were adopted only through 3 voting against. '</p>
