        <p>ST. HELENS HEATH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE( WORK OF VIE FEMALE SANITARY INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the St. Tielens health Committee was held on Wednesday, Councillor Joe. Massey, deputy-chairman, presided, and there were also present ' the Mayor (Alderman A. Sinclair), Alderman Gamble, C. 8., Alderman J. C. Gamble, Councillors Green Jones and Burchall. A question was raised by Mr. B , urchall whether a Mora economical method Of removing nightsoil and ashes could not be adopted. Mr. Massey said the chairman had always taken up a strong attitude in the matter,and was dia me- trically opposed to Mr. Bureball's view that they should ' be' eaceted separately.—Alderman J. C. Gamble sug- gested that Mr. Burchall should arrange with the Sur- . veyor to try the experiment, and if they found it an im- provement they could bring the matter forward, At present, the committee were convinced the method now adopted wag the best. The committee rescinded a for- mer resolution that the wooden hospital at Peasiey Cross be removed to Parr Platt, and ordered its removal to the Old Wynt at Flaydock, where cases of small- pox arising both at St. Helens and Flaydock would be treated. A special report Stated :;--The female sanitary inspec- tor commenced work• on the 2nd January, 1894, and during the whole of the month her duties had been con- fined to Greenbank. She had there visited 477 houses, and of that number 103 were found in a satisfactory condition. The remaining 374 were insanitary,or dirty, and required visiting and revisiting frequently. Verbal instructions were given for cleaning houses, yards, &amp;c., in the first:instance,and, when'those were not complied with, notices were served. It was found necessary to take that course in ninety-nine case:. The condition of Many of the houses was filthy in the extreme, and it 'was only by her frequent visits that they could expect the district to be kept in a healthy csndition. Up to the end of January twenty-four houses ha ci been lime- washed by the tenants, and many more had promised to limewash them soon. Limewash brushel had beeu lent Ito those who could not afford to buy them. A very ilarge nutnber of backyards were now in a better condi- tion than formerly, and a large number of the houses had been washed more regularly. In about twenty cases instructions had been given where young infants were being carelessly fed or clothed. ,</p>
        <p>r SAD SEND OF AN EIRLESTOWN MAN.,</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning the body of an elderly man was found cut to pieces on the Lannashire and York3hire. Railway at Clifton, near Manchester. Shortly after 1 a.m. the' man was seen by a points- man wandering about the line. Wsrned that he might get killed, the man made. reply that ke was all right, and asked for shelter, but was refused. Later on his mangled remains were ,found. The man, Who had appeared strange in his manner, was discovered by an engirifedriver cut in two 'across the cliest,.and minus both arms and.a. foot. .On Monday the body-was iden- .. tined by his wife as that of Joseph Birchall: Deceased had, it is said. lived apart from his family and begun -eanoforte tunieg at Hulrne. He was a clever lingUist. is wife and family, who have allowed him half a ,inea weekly, liv.6 at Haydock-st., Earlestown. It is a story how deceased came on the railway—An inquest held on Tuesday,niorning at Clifton by Mr. F. Price, tounty coroner, relative to the death of Joseph Rine aged 52,'Who of late had been a.pianoforte tuner ;etticd reSidence. On 'Saturday mornitig at two the deceased was ordered Off the line of the 'iire and Yorkshire Railway about half-a-mile :on Fold Station by a pointsman named John Che man went in the direction or Pinfold siding to the Manchester-road. About nine minutes received a Ulegraphie message from the d Cabin that the ,driver of the 10.45 p.m. is train had run over something, and a few • Yates learnt that a Man had been cut to line. When search was made the deceased's 7 was found . at the Unity Brook siding, vet portion' bf the 'body being on the up line "' and the' lower portion in the le :jury retusned a verdict of Acci-</p>
        <p>gt, my V 7 EJEI the t chall, of no , o'clock LancaSl from Di: Yates. ' to get on later Yate, Robin Hoo express gook minutes late: pieces on the, mutilated bod. the head and u, outside of the" " four-foot."—Ti</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY AND LANCASHIRE; TRADES UNIONISTS. ITHREATENED REPRISALS. A correspondent writes :—I hear that the Conservative trades unionists in Lancashire are very angry over Lord Salisbuivreference " cruel organiation," and that some of,ilieir leaders haVe written to the Tory leader on the saihje.et. Many of the Tory members from Laopa- shire are much annoyed by lord Salisbury's language,: and they teat ltst it -will lose them several seats at the general election. Mr. Balfour, it is understood, is doing his best to soothe the Lancashire malcontents, but some of them will be satisfied with nothing less than an apology from Lord Salisbury.</p>
