      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>GERMINATION OP SEED.</head>
        <p>THE GERMINATION OP SEED.</p>
        <p>Sir.—As to dead seed germinating, I have no hesita- tion in telling " Harry Esmond" it is not true, and that be mayeasily verify my assertion by experimenting ou seed. "Prove all things," is an excellent adage, and we are living in an age when it is stringently applied to all questions (wherever found and however venerable may e the author) where there is a doubt as to their truth- fulness_ When I tell " Harry &amp;mond " that I am by occupation a farmer, I hope he will give me credit for know;ng semething about seeds. May I hope that my -evidence will help tt. remove from his mind the doubt which the quotation in question has incurred ? If so, my object in thus writing will have been realized.— Yours, &amp;c., Lymm, February 6. 1894.</p>
        <p>EX-FARMER.</p>
        <p>-- TUE TOWN CLOCK: A REMARKABLE I RECORD. Sir,—Will you kindly permit me to call attention to the remarkable accuracy of the town clock during the past two months? The following is a record of its errors in striking the hour,as compared with the time obtained from the Birkenhead gun On Dec. 7th the clock was sec. fast. ff ? 1 14th 11 0 if ft „ „ 19th ft i sec. slow. II ft 237 h ~ 2 ft VP „ Jan. 4th f t 1 IS ft b 16P ft f • - 21 sec.fast. ~ ~ 25th 1, A 11 f 9 ff •9 30th 1 sec. slo-w. „ Feb. 7th ~ 5 tf ff Flaying; kept a record for the whole of the past year, lam also able to state that the clock has seldom been more than i minute wrong.—Yours, &amp;c., F. W. R.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>R.A.TIONALISII VERSUS CIfIRCHISSI.</head>
        <p>R.A.TIONALISII VERSUS CIfIRCHISSI.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Howell is the funniest "beggar of the ques- tion "I ever had the felicity of reading. He is one of that very numerous class who imagine that a restate- ment of a question in other words, is a solution thereof. He is like the man who described an Archdeacon as" a person who performs archdeaconal duties." In like manner to prove the historical accuracy of the " fall " he says there is sin and sorrow in the world. If the rest of 1 his cumulative proofs are no better than this, his " fall " has indeed fallen. The letters of Mr. Howell would be I excellent as examples in a text book on logic of "Things to be avoided in, reasoning," much as a page of bad granesnar is given to pupils for correction. What position as a reasoner can a man hold who ac- cepts the " fall" as a fact and rejects " special crea- tion," both of these statemer.ts having equal authority as coming from the Book upon which, and upon which alone the pretentious of his creed are founded? To a mind not trained to the assimilation of doctrinal absur- dities it is difficult to understand why two statements having the same Biblical authority should not be either rejected together or accepted together. Together they stand ; together they must fall. But alas! the fall of the " fall" means the ruin of " Salvation," and this Mr. Howell's adopted church has" defended "—Yours, Sm. JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>RATIONALISTS AND BIBLICAL CRITICISM. Sir,—The instance of tho very loose method of rea- soning usually employed by Rationalists, is the reply of Mr. Peers to Mr. Esmond in your last issue. The Parable of the seed (John xii. 24) is the incident chosen to overthrow the inspiration of the New Testament, and i priori the Divinity of Christ. Well, let us look into it. Both correspondents say "Exeept a corn of wheat . . die." What do they mean by " die " ? The Christian means a dissolution of soul and body ; the resolution of the body into the dust, and the return of tho soul to the God who gave it. Mr. Peers being a materialist means the absolute extinction of conscioue life ; so our friends are ab initio, arguing from opposite premises. But how is the New Testament affected? Is it un- scientific? Certainly not. The grain of wheat falls into the ground and dies, in the Christian meaning of the term (the scientific meaning else, I may add) it is re- solved into its original constituents, and the ever-living portion as Mr. Peers admits—rises again and is multi- plied ! This meaning is confirmed by all Catholic com- mentaries on this verse. St. Augustine says, "By this grain of corn our Saviour means Himself, who was to die . • . and be multiplied by the faith of the Gen- tiles"(tract 51 in Joann); also Bishop Worthington in the 18th century, before the so-caoed " higher criticism " started, "The ccnnp,,rison is this, that as the seed must be changed and corrupted in the ground before it fructify, so the world would not be converted but by Christ's death." The only reason for Mr. Peers' aninaadvergione on these subjects seems to be an affliction of Cacoethes scribendi which it would be good, both for the health of himself and your readers, to get rid of !—I am sir, yours faithfully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>JOHN S. HOWELL. 9, Montgomery-road; Chigvrick, London, W. Februaiy 11.!1), 1394.</head>
        <p>JOHN S. HOWELL. 9, Montgomery-road; Chigvrick, London, W. Februaiy 11.!1), 1394.</p>
        <p>THE 'PERPLEXING PROBLEM."</p>
        <p>Sir,—On venturing to disagree with the view taken by your correspondent Mr. Ben Chayil, as to the fate of the seed when cast into the ground, I shall be stigma- tized by him as one " not gifted with common sense and ordinary discernment." The little botany I learnt, which,however is sufficient for the purpose, tells me that the seed contains in an embryonic condition, all the essential parts of which a plant is ultimately composed. and only requires suitable conditiens to cause it to manitet its wonderful power of development. Surely Mr. E‘liter this is not death, either on Biblical or any other authority, and Mr. B. C. is straining the point very much when he attempts to make it so by telling us that the nutritious matter of a grain of wheat (which is quite distinct from the life part j of the seed) undergoes change. I imagine there are few people in these days who go to the Bible for scientific accuracy. Wkat matters to me whether Jesus was or was not acquainted with the botanical structure and exact behaviour of a seed ; does it detract one jot or tittle from his beautiful life, example or teaching, or make him less my human brother ? Certainly not ! Your correspondent Mr. John Peers, in regarding Unitarianism as "a half way house towards thorough reasonableness in religious matters" has to my mind grasped only half the fact of what the present day Uni- tarianism stands for. Judging from the latter portion of his letter, I venture to think there are more points in which he would agree than disagree with the avenwe Unitarian, and perhaps even arriye'at the conclusion as I have done, that instead of coming under the definition as being " a bed to catch a falling christian," it is a rea- sonable faith with a noble conception 9f God and a true idea of the character and teaching of Jesus far more calculated, were it better understood, to raise men from the blank despair of atheism than all the orthodox creed of Christendom.—Yours, Sze.,</p>
        <p>G. DE NORO.</p>
        <p>THE RESURRECTION OF SEEDS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Harry Esmond's perplexities seem to have been infectious. Your correspondent ilen Chavil has caught it,and is perplexed that anyone should be so ignorant as to think as a seed does not die in germination ! Would he kindly refer the matter to " our scientific hotanists," who " know that the corn of wheat when buried in the soil does die "—would he kindly submit it to them and let their opinion be published in ay scientific Journal? They, however, must not write over a nom de plume as your correspondent has done thus showing the only bit of sound judgment in his letter. As I am not quite green in this matter—my verdancy dating back quite a generation. It is exactly thirty years since I had the temerity to publish a Paper on "Seeds "in a London botanical journal—so I come not. to the question quite raw, and I defy any botanist, -scientific or other, to show that any part of a seed neces- sarily dies in germination. The seed of a plant and the egg of a bird are--physiologie,ally—strictly analogous, so much so that the one may he stated in terms of the other. Well, I think even "Ben Chayil," would not feel disposed to evoke the laughter of the initiated by maintaining that sitting eggs must die ere they will hatch ! If this correspondent would look facts squarely in the face instead of bemusini . himself by a silly tra-; dition—if he would ask-tifnself the question " Is there ' anything in a seed—say a grain of wheat—that is or ever has been alive, except the germ ; and can that which never have life die ? " Any maltster would be able to put this seminal resnr- • Tection dogma to death by detailing exactly what takes place on his own " coming loon" Here all the processes of initial germination are gone through. The grain is first" wetted," steeped in water, then it is spread on a flagged floor some six or nine inches thick,when the won- derful chemistry of germination converts the starch in- to sugar so that the germ or embryo can feed upon it. In this there is nothing that can be called death. It is essentially and entirely a vital and a vitalizing process— to which the word "die" can no more be rightly applied, than in the allied case of the hatching eggs. As to" Ben Chavirs " reference to Balfour's First Book on Botany the book is a mere Primer, and its en- dorsement of this popular blunder a mere sop for the Sunday school. The book is dated 1874. Would the author dare to publish that now in any scientific journal? If we look back a little we may find pseudo-scientific</p>
        <p>people endorsing all the physical errors of the Bible. For my part I cannot regard a vital and vitalizing process of growth as other than a living process and not a mori- bund one.—Yours, Scca,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
