      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>JOHN PEERS. DOES THE SEED ? A PERPLEXING PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>JOHN PEERS. DOES THE SEED ? A PERPLEXING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Need I say that I am still perplexed, that my head is still troubled with an unsolved problem? Pain- ful though it may be to de so, I am obliged to confess to my ignorance and want of comprehension. I have not received the needed illumination from Mr. Chayil's letter —sad though this statement may appear to him. His first statement that every scientific botanist well knows that the " corn of wheat" when buried in the soil" does die" is flatly refuted by your other corres- pondent, Mr. Peers. It is further refuted by a botanist friend of mine who sent me the following note after read- ing my first letter :—" Dear Bsmond, Structural Botany as a science must have been at a very low ebb if the quotation which adorns the entrance to St. James' burial ground is intended to be taken literally, for the not-too-advanced student of botany will tell you at once that the seed most certainly does not die, but on the contrary it contains the future parts of the plant in an embryonic state, and only requires such suitable environ- ment as a congenial soil supplies to encourage it to begin its wonderful process of development. Have you ever seen a seedsman testing the germinating properties of a sample of seeds? Perhaps out of the sample 10 per cent. will be dead, but if so, no germination takes place." So you see, sir, it is not well known to every scientific botanist that the" corn of wheat" when buried in the soil "does die." I have no intention to discuss the question of the various parts of the seed dying. I will leave that with Mr. Peers and Mr. Chayil. I will only say this, that it is the merest quibbling to say that the external wrappings or investiture of the seed dies. Was this external part ever possessed of life? If not, how could it be said to die? That which constitutes the seed a "living seed" is the embryo, and neither Mr. Chayil nor Dr. Balfour will contend that it ever dies and comes to life again. Mr. Chayil's second statement is " that the use our Lord makes of this fact is one of the most beautiful and apt illustrations of His divine prescience and wisdom." Now, with all respect, and with no intention of irreverence, I say it is nothing of the kind. Here, a living seed is put into the ground, and it germinates, quite naturally, develops into a plant, and reproduces its own kind. Needless to say, man does not reproduce his own kind in any such man- ner. And when a man is buried ills usually, ef course, as a dead body, and in process of time his body resolves itself into its original elements in the soil. How many millions of people have been buried in the earth of whom not a vestige can be found ? Will Mr. Chayil contend that they will come to life again? Of course we know that many people believe they will—but the belief is quite incapable of verification. Not so with the seed— the evidence that it comes up out of the ground again is palpable to every one. But if the seed is dead before it is put into the ground, just as the body of a man is be- fore it is buried, then it remains in the ground and rots —not dies—just as the body does, and nothing more is seen of it. I think Mr. Chayil- will hardly expect us to believe that when a man dies and is buried, his soul is buried too, and is only emancipated by the decomposi- tion of the body. In no other sense can there be any conceivable analogy between the two cases. And in which way is the idea reconcileable with the belief that at death the soul, or the spirit, is at once released, and goes back to its Maker ? I hope I shall not be misun- derstood. lam not arguing against the immortality of the soul. With me, this is an open question. Mr. Chayil's remarks about a malignant spirit of hos- tility towards religion have no application to nso. lam a member of a Christian Church, attend its services re- gularly, and derive both pleasure and profit from doing so. Further, I am in no way responsible for those theories as to the limitations of Christ's knowledge. To the best of my recollection I never heard or read any- thing about them till I saw them in the " Methodist Re- corder." Does Mr. Chayil believe that this journal is animated with a malignant spirit of hostility towards re.ligien ? Still, the advocacy of these views by the journal in question—or perhaps I ought to say by Pro- fessor Davictson—is a matter of intellectual confusion and perplexity to me. How does he reconcile them with the doctrine of the Divinity of Christ ? lam certainly wanting in comprehension here.—Yours, HARRY ESMOND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>NOT S ON GAMES A. 71)</head>
        <p>NOT S ON GAMES A. 71)</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTIINLL.</p>
        <p>Warrington have booked another vic.tory. They met Broughton in the County Championship on Saturday, and beat them by five points. Despite this success, ' however, the team is said to have played a much worse game than for some time. Tho play was loose, and oc- casionally there were to be had glimpses of the old sel- fishness which has been so much condemned. Consider- . ing the relative positions of the two teams, and the wide difference in their abilities one cannot but regard Warrington's victory, as having a needlessly narrow margin. Throughout the game the wir&amp;pullers showed a careless attitude—" a-don't-care-if- we-do-win .''—sort of Rir. I cannot, any more than many others, account for this degenerate er.hibition. Let us hope of it, as we have done of the deluge : That it won't occur again.</p>
        <p>Warring.ton'a forward play was most conspicuous, and it is probable that had it not been tor tivAr excellent form the match would have been (G.A. As for the backs— well, they seemed to have forgotten all about their re- cent brilliant exhibitions, and they played without any combination. Of course it is something to their credit that they should be able to " lick " a team with one' hand tied behind them, as it were. Heesom was some- what defective, and Broady, who played in the place of Barber, did not show anything like the resource of the latter promising player. John Bate was cot present to assist." Bro. Smack."</p>
        <p>The results of the competition up to Saturday were thus : For. Ag,t. Pld. Won. Tmst. Drn. (IT. (I.l'. Pts Oldham l3 ...10 ... 1 ... 2 ... 17 V... 3 5 -.22 Swinton l2 ... 7 ... 0 ... 5 ... 10 15... 1 4 ...19 Warrington 3 ... 57.2 4 ...15 Salford Tyldesley Broughtonnangers l2 ... 4 ... 5 ... 3 ... 7 3 ... 6 11 -.11 Po01-145de Hornets Barrow Broughton l3 0 .12 0 2 5 ...24 27 ... 0</p>
        <p>As the matches in the Lancashire Rugby Competition progress. the int4rest becomes more and moreintense. There will unfloubt‘dly be a large crowd on the Oldham Club ground this (Saturday) afternoon. The match is certainly the most important of the compejtiom matches to be played to-day. Oldiwn hold the fore- most place in the c cornp4titon With throe points ahead, ad Warrineton are a tie with Bwinton for second place, and there is consequently a vast amount of interest centered in the match. When Oldham playeu at Warrington they won by a penalty goal, 5 tries, 18 points, to Warrington 1 goal 5 points. But since then, the Warrington men have shown great improvement and the Oldham men will have to put in all they know to repeat the " trick " they performed at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The followitnr are the names of the seams for to dav Warrinaten : Full hack, F. Fleesom ; three-quart(-rs, P. Carey, C. Potts, .1, W. Evans, and 11. Barber ; half backs, John Bate and James Bate; forwards, Fair Turner, J. Jolley, W. Novine, J. Taylor, G. Daken, .1. Buckley, W. Berry, and J. T. Thornily. Oldham : Full back, W. L. Pennington ; three-quartArs, W. McCut• . cheon, S. Lees, D. Gwynn, and J. Hurst ; hil backs, H. Varley and A. Lass ; forwar.ls. J. Chadwick, J. Bard- man, G. Furnesa, J. Murrell, E. Hughes, T. Bonsor, T. Sellers, and J. Barnes.</p>
        <p>The Lanca.;hire team, for the match next week with the Midland counties has been chosen. Its complsltion is as follows :—T. Foulkes, (St. Helens), full back ; W. M'Cutcheon (Oldham), S. Lees (Oldham). T. Auderton (Leigh), and J. 'Valentine (Swinton), captain, three- quarter hacks ; H. Varley (Oldham), and S. Walsh (Sal- ford). half-hacks ; J. Jolley (Warrington), G. Woodward (Tyldesley), J. Simpson (Rochdale Hornets), S. Bingham (Liverpool), J. Jones (Salford), R. Pierce (Liverpool), H. Cas4) (Swinton), and Rideha.ugh (Rochdale St. Clements), forwards. Reserves: W. Win terhottom (Swinton), full back ; J. Hurst (Oldham), R. Lewis (Ulverston), and F. Miles (Salford), threequarter-backs; W. Halliwell (Wigan), half-back ; T. Butterworth (Roch- dale Hornets), W. Unsworth (Wigan), and J. C. Gould (Liverpool Old Boys), forvvards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>BOATINe.</head>
        <p>BOATINe.</p>
        <p>The meetinc! of the Warrington Soiling. Clul9, to which I referred last week, was held on Monday night, at the Ship Inn. I belif:vo a " warm " hour was spent before the real business was got at. The trouble did not arise, I hear, friorn any moveinunt on the part of those mem- bers termed the "pirates" It; was in connection with the appointment of a new secretarw.</p>
        <p>The officers appointed for the forthcoming season were :—Mr. H. Warhurt,n, enmmodore : Mr. F. Jeffer- son, vice-commodore; Mr. G.Righy, secretary ; and Mr. E. Gleave, treasurer. A eGromittee was also appointed. There have been many more entries for the race re- ceived than last year, and i i.s expected that the privs will be much more valualle thau formerly.</p>
        <p>It is isn't often one sees :a vf.ssel making its way along a main street ! But the other evening the venerable " Woodcock " was seen on the top of a " bogey" travelling along Church-itrcet. It was being taken to the yard behind the" Marquis of Grandby," where it will undergo some rem:mat-ion. This is quite a now move. When the boats receive their annual" painting- up," it is generally giver to them on the bank.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE EXAMINER_ ‘s,ATITEDAY FEBRUARY 17, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>The billiard players at the Liberal Club have arranged a series of competitions for Saturday night suppers. The match played last week was between the teams ar- ranged by Mr. G. J. Jennings and Mr. A. Jones, and the latter team won by some 20 points,and " supped " at the expense of their adversaries. The repast was presided over Mr. T. Allen who also took part in the subse- quent concert, at which songs were sung by Messrs. W. W. Whittle, J. G. Chappell, and G. Finch. The catering was carried out very satisfactorily by Mr. Mitchell, the manager of the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>GICIfNASIIIII.</head>
        <p>GICIfNASIIIII.</p>
        <p>The Warrington eight ought to have journeyer] to Aberdeen yesterday, to meet the eight of the town to-day (Saturday) in the Shield. Competition. They have not made the journey however. This is because the two eights cannot come to terms about the expenses. The rules of the Society state that the team visited should defray the railway expenses of the visiting team. This, Aberdeen have refused to do, and have warned Warrington that they will have to take "their luck."</p>
        <p>By taking " their luck," they meant Warrington would have to be content with what was given to them, no matter how small it might be. The Warringtonians very properly refused to be a party to such a violation of the rules, and the matter is now a subject for discussion before the council of die Society. Of course the expense incurred in travelling would be considerable, and Aber- deen might be out of pocket if they complied with the rules. It appears to me that a fund of some kind ought to be founded to help in emergencies of this description.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>-VISIT TO WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>A -VISIT TO WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>As I passed into the vestibule a gentleman took nay hand. He shook it cordially and said: "Good afternoon—l hope you are well." I said I was—very well, in fact. _</p>
        <p>"It is a nice day." "It is, indeed." He paused for a moment, still retaining my hand in his and smiling. Then he said, in a confidential whisper: "May I ask what subject you will take?" This is the humorous gentleman of the body, I said to myself—and maybe he expects me to keep up the "chaff." Then in a responsive, confidential whisper: "Any sort of a subject is good enough for me to take, if I can only lay my hands upon it—of course I have a partiality for plate, and jewellery,and--" " Sir ! May I ask if you are not the speaker ?" Then I saw we both had made a mistake. "Chaff- ing" had been the farthest thing from his thoughts.</p>
        <p>My object in going down to Wycliffe Church on this Sunday afteenoon, was to investigate the "Pleasant Sunday Afternoons for Men" scheme. I presently came into contact with a gentleman who could give me all necessary information rela- tive to this excellent movement. This was Mr. T. Wallington the secretary. We chat- ted together on the matter for a time. It seems that there are three branches of the movement in Warrington,Wycliffe being immensely larger in membership than either of the others. The first branch to spring into existence was the one held in the rooms of the Y.M.C.A. The Wycliffe , branch started on the fifth of March last year—a few weeks after the one at Friars Green—but its opening had been delayed some weeks,owing to the smallpox epidemic which then prevailed. The third branch is at Bank Quay Mission, and was formed a ' few weeks ago. It held an opening meeting then, but it cannot be said that it is, as yet, fairly established. The movement, I believe, first origin- ated in Derby, some few years ago. It" took on instantly and since then has been growing and flourishing, till now, like some young giant, it stands out in unique prominence among the fore- mest of our Christian teaching bodies. Those who address its meetings are not allowed to waste time in lecturing upon formula—but on the whole, are occupied in delivering good, broad Christian advice. Then again, the proceedings of the meetings are not made tedious by the performance of useless ritual ; they are such that an ordinary man—who is not hampered with theological fads—can understand and enjoy. The motto is " Brief Bright, and Brotherly."</p>
        <p>"If you will come into the vestibule" said Mr. Wallington "you can see the system we have of checking the attendances. Each member is given an attendance card, and pays a penny per week. This money is spent in book prizes. Twenty-two attendances in the half-year will entitle a member to a first class prize, ano twelve to a second class prize. Of course we admit no members under the age of 18 years." "There are no female members?" " Oh, no! It is for men only. Every quarter,how- ever, we have what is termed an open day, when the member." are allowed to brine their wives and lady friends. There is no doubt a society will be formed for women, but we could nor attempt its organiza- tion—we are too much engaged just now." By this time we had reached the vestibule and here I observed several gentleman sitting at a table, and by no means making that part of their Sabbath, a period of rest. Through each of the entrance doers came a stream of members, who passed up to the tables and handed to sac of the register markers, his attendance card, and contribution. Then on the card, opposite the date, the marker stamped "P.l'." meaning" present and paid " ; or in the case of a member bringing an absentee's coutri- buticn there would be stamped " AT." meaning "absent and paid." With a thought, perhaps as inueh of sympathy for the markers as anything else,. I asked: " And how many members are there ? " "There are now 1,170 enrolled members. We commenced our first Sunday with about 700, and we only opened after much labour. For fortnight previous, the whole town had been can- vassed, the work in each district being carried out by a captain and committee. It was very hard work to wet the people to recogniee the movement. But. it has since gone on with surprieing rapidity. Among our members there are men from every creed in the town, besides quite a number who had never in their lives belonged to any religious institution. We got a large number of these on the first Sunday. But what an anxioue time it was ? We opened the doors at half-past two—there wasn't a soul about. We looked at each other in a very doleful manner, and spoke about wasted labour' in a very dejected way. All at once, however, men began to stream in. They came from all quarters of the town, and • were very quickly 700 strong. "Oh, no, there is none of the revival bu.inese about the movement. We do not pry into each other's private lives. There is no compulsion about the at- tendance se that we can trust each other. There is a committee which looks absentees up, of course. A very singular thingehy the way,is that we know al- ways whereabouts in the church to find a member ; the men invariably make for the pew they first oc- cupied." " It was a somewhat novel thing for those who came nst their first visit to a religious institution ?'' " Yes, some of them used to look very shy miff sheepisl: when they first came. We would some- times see a man come sauntering, along, furtively glancing about to see if anybody was watching him. If he thought there was no one looking he would step insnle and ge to a pew. If observed he would wait about with a half-ashamed air until an opportunity arose. However, they soon got over that, and we have had the satisfaction of knowing that a number of men have been steadied up by coming here. We have a benevolent fund now,and if we hear of any member being straits ' be is assisted unostentatiously."</p>
        <p>It ,vas now getting time for tho service to com- mence, so we moved inside. Here I got a pro- gramme of the proceedings. Every item is timed off sie as to bring the proceedings to a close in an hour and a quarter. Thero were fourteen items altogether. The first was a hymn of wel- come sung by the choir, and then came a hymn sung by the meeting. Following came sacred solos by local vocali9ts,or maybe a recitation, , or reading. Then came two addresses, for one of ! which five minutes was allowed, and for the othe! • 15 minutes. No poaching upon time is permitted to ! the speakers. They have to pull up at the minute— or they are pulled down by the coat, tails. This is the recognised way of putting a stop to an encroach- ing discourse, so I was informed by the secretary. Once—a few weeks ago—ono of the speakers got ahead of his time. Ile was a very energetic ! speaker. and his somewhat, numerous gesticulations I caused his coat to work up his back in " tizcks,"and 11 the collar to creep up the nape of his neck, and over ! his ears. Having exceeded his time he was promptly pulled by the coat tails, whereupon he turned round and innocently said : "Thank you,' re-adjusted his coat and went on. Whenever the tails were pulled again he simply put his hand be- hind and helped pull the coat; down and smooth it out. So they had to allow him to get to the end of his tether.</p>
        <p>KEINE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>It was not necessary to introduce this useful little brake on this occasion. The first speaker, who was described as a workingman, spoke exactly on to the live minutes. His address was what one might term as ideal. It was crisp, well deliv- ered, and above all practical. During the course of its delivery I heard for the third time, something that ought to make one's heart glad. It was the sound of honest, hearty applause,in this—a Church I The next address was of fifteen minutes duration. For my part I was not over struck with its appropriate- ness. Tee speaker set out to dwell upon the three greatest evils of the day. The first, he said, was pernicious literature. Then he went on to say that novels were responsible for one half of the wicked- ness of this country and that 99 out of every 100 ought to be consigned to the flames. When I looked round,and saw among those thousand grown rnen,the faces of many taking prominent parts in the trade of the town—men who are" hard headed "and well read—then I confess I did not think that assertion would be received favourably. For my part I differ from it. Again, the speaker compared the an who reads novels and the one who reads the bible to two ships. 'the novel reader was the ship without ballaat, and the bible reader the ship with ballast. They both sailed out of harbour, into mid-Atlantic, when a gale came on. Then what became of them? The vessel without ballast ran upon the rocks— the other sailed safely into harbour. I put myself in the position of an average, intelligent person, like the majority of those present, and how did this simile strike me? As being as impossible as the first assertion was inappropriate. A vessel never puts out to sea without ballast— few, if any, cross the Atlantic with only ballast— and in the case of a hurricane the unballasteci ship's danger would be that of capsizing, and not that of running upon the rocks. Besides, neither an un- ballasted ship nor a ballasted one would run upon the rocks in mid-Atlantic—because there aren't any rocks convenient. Of course, I only mention this because lam afraid it caused grim smiles to per- vade the faces of many present, and also to point out how necessary it is to be careful in speaking to a thousand of perhaps the most common-sensed men in the town. Above all, it must not be taken as a thrust at the institution. Having attended such a meeting, one cannot help but see that it is a movement born of the progress of the times; it is a religious tree, which has had a colossel growth, and the nourishing principles upon which it thrives will force its further progress until, some day per- haps, it will overtop most of it fellows and its influence dominate them. Above all, it is a move- merit which bears the impress of democracy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BRUNNER, M.P., AT NORTHWICH.</head>
        <p>BRUNNER, M.P., AT NORTHWICH.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING SPEECH.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening Mr. J. T. Btunner, M.P. for the Northwic'a Division, was cordially welcomed by the members of the Gladstone Liberal Club, North- wich, on his first visit since his return from the South of France, where he has been sojourning for the benefit of his health. The hon. member was received with rounds of cheering. After congratu- latory speeches from Mr. H.. Bratt, C.C. (chairman of the club) and other members. Mr. Brunner,:in responding, said he could assure them in 'spite of the , dolelul accounts of his health which had been given by his friends that he was pretty fit. (Hear, hear.) It was a fact that for nine or ten weeks he was a very poor creature, and only four weeks ago he was obliged ta use a stick in walkine, and found that a walk of a mile was absolutely exhausting. He had to acknowledge that he bad had a lesson, and would ask them that night to give him their appeThatiOn in his effort to thoroughly recover his health in order to go on with his work on their be- half. (Applause.) It had been a great pleasure to him to hear that their club was prospering, and it was also a great pleasure to see so many young men present. He trusted that the amusement they obtained there—and from the look of that and the rooms downstairs he found there was more and more provision for amusement— would convince them that Liberals as a whole were a clubable lot and a good sort of people to live amongst. (Applause.) He had to thank them ,from his heart for the very beautiful present they sent to his daughter, whom he gave away last Tuesday. It was a matter of extreme delight to him when his friend Mr. Yearsley ' came to tell him of the present the club had made up their minds to give her. He had not thought of it; but if he had thought, amongst the many things he had to think about, of his friends of the Gladstone Club he might not have been so much surprised, because it was prey another evidence, following upon many a one, of their kindness towards him and his. (Applause.) He remembered when he came home about eleven weeks ago,his wife sent for the doctor, who told him that it was not influzaza that was troubling him, but that he had been overweirked. That was a rough and colloquial way of speaking of such a matter. Hard work never did any- body any harm—(bear, hear)—and the more a man worked, so long as lie stoked the boiler regularly, the better for him. But he had had an enormous amount of worry as well as work, and it was "care that killed the cat which had nine lives." The 16 weeks of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>COAL STRIKE</head>
        <p>THE COAL STRIKE</p>
        <p>took a great deal out of him, for his responsibility in tbe matter was very great. When he multiplied the number of men who were in the employ of Brunner, Mond,and Co.by four he got to a number of individuals with mouths to he filled every day so great as to weigh very heavily upon him, and when it was necessary for him to decide day after day, as he had to do, whether he should pay three times the ordinary price for coal or not —if he did ILA Day three times the ordinary price their works would stop and food would not come to those thousands of people—he could assure them that that was worry,care and anxiety,and it was net at all a won- der that at the end of the 16 weeks he was considerably below par. He had got up again and meant to go on with greater cheerfulness after meeting them. (Ap- plause.) The coal strike told upon a good many. Two other members of Parliament were in his mind—Mr. Pickard and Mr. Woods. He had heard over and over again from Tories, that those two gentlemen fomented the strike for the benefit af their own pockets. The leaders of the workingmen of this country during many weeks suffered in their pockets by their own voluntary accounts; many of them gave up the pay that was -due to them ; they gave up their time,their energy and their health in the service of their fellows, and •he said all honour to them for the work they did. (Applause.) He was not to be understood as entirely endorsing the policy of the leaders of the Miners' Federation, that was too</p>
        <p>_ long a.isd too dry a subject for him to pursue that night, There were one or two matters upon which he kad to congratulate them as inhabitants and ratepayers of that district and as well-wishers of all in the constituency. The year 1894, they might take it from him, would see the final settlement of the old quarrel about compensa- tion for subsidence. (Applause.) He should like to send urssage upon that subject to his friend Mr. Maddocks. Wear, hear.) fie bad to say to Mr. Maddocks that if he happened to meet a director of the Salt Union wander- ing about the neighbourhood and found him in a sporting mood, willing to offer two to one against the settlement of the compensation question this year,he would advise Mr.MatidDcks to take the off:r as many times over as he could "et it. (Applause and a Voice :" We shall have him a Liberal a.tter that.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WEAVER TRUST.</head>
        <p>THE WEAVER TRUST.</p>
        <p>He had also to congratulate them upon the fact that Parliament hal ordered the Weaver Trustees to corns up with a Bill next year at the latest for their own ainenG- ment. He had told them over and over again tnathe considered they wanted amending very seriously. (Ap- plause.) To him, personally, as well as to the cause of right and justice, it was a distinct triumph when Parlia- ment ozdered-thein to bring in that Bill ; hut when they . .</p>
        <p>came forward with it he would advise them to watch them to see Vett the new constitution of the Weaver frost was based upon right lincs,and that some of the old mischief was breufeht to an end. The county of Cheshire and the County Council were looking forward to a pretty stiff fight for the maintenance of the old contrlmtion from the Weaver. The English people had a sneaking kindness for an old iai- quity, and the older it was the better they liked it, and the County Council were going to take advantage of this sentiment of theirs. He (the speaker) was going to light them, and he was satisfied that they were going to fight fair ly, and he was sure they would fight in a friendly fashion, too.Fle was going to fight,: not only for the money which would be left in the lian&amp;; of the Weaver Trustees, but principally because he considered that a tax upon trade for which no service Whatever was or ever had been rendered was an utter injustice, and must be brought to an end at the earliest possible day. (Ap- plause.) In 1366,they might remember that the county was visited by a terrible murrain among cattle. According to the old phrase—a phrase very eld and with a good deal of truth in it—it was a visitation from God for the sins of Cheshire. He did not mean to say that God sent the cattle plague because (iissAlt was so rife in Cheshire—(laughter)—or any other such sin ; but the feet that the farmers of Cheshire did not realize that cattle not only wanted good food, ot which they were always given plenty, but good water to drink and good air to breathe by night as well as day—they housed their cattle in dirty fashion in low, close shippons—was responsible for the terrible loss amongst the cattle. Farmers, like everybody else, mend their ways, and in this Union they had been providing good water for a large number of people as well as cattle. But in 1886 the farmers Gould not pay their rents, and that was a sort of thing landlords did not like, and the landlords of Cheshire appealed to Parlia- ment for national belt). Parliament replied that Cheshire val squeezing enough money out of the salt trade to meet the liability, and, there- fore, Cheshire must pay. a county rate was made, instead of, as the landowners of Cheshire, askEd, a national rate. The Cheshire landowners did not pr.pese, and nobody suggested it on behalf of the salt manufacturers, and that salt manufacturers, should ex- empt froin the county rate. Therefore, it had come</p>
        <p>about for the last 28 years they had all been contribut- ing the money twice ever. Perhaps he ought not to al- ludo to himself; but, at any rate,the salt trade had been paying the money that met the cattle plague rate and had been paying the county rate into the bargain. This was the instance he alluded to when he avid that the</p>
        <p>English people had a sneaking kindness for an old ini-1 quity. The cattle plague rate would come to an end in 1896 ; in 1895 the Weaver Trustees were to come up wit a Bill for their own amendment, and he was; satisfied, with the better appreciation of such matters that pre- vailed in the House of Commons—he would not say the House of Lords, for they were past praying for—(ap- plause)—that the fact that the cattle plague rate came to an end would be a very strong argument in their favour when they claimed that the Weaver Trust should</p>
        <p>no longer pay anything to the county because they were going to be relieved of some £14,000 a year of the cattle plague rate. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>LIBBRAL ORGANIZA.TION, AC. He had another matter upon which to coagratulate them, and far which he had to give praise to the officials of their Liberal organization. They were going to have a better supply of polling stations, which would be of great advantage to them. He was promised a contest at the next election, his friend Mr. Yerburgh, tho mem- ber for Chester, having told him in the frankest possible way that they would find someone to fight hint because they could not afford to keep him quiet. (A Voice : "It won't be Chesters Thompson.") Mr. Chesters Thompson would certainly not send any hired cabs into this constituency next time. (Laughter and applause.) It was only the previous night in his drawing room at home that he made the remark that he had never known I a man of Mr. Balfour's rank make so many speeches in one week within so-little space as Mr. Balfour had done in his constituency in Manchester. He wondered why on earth it was that a man who had led the House of</p>
        <p>Commons for years should speak in so many school- rooms in one town in one week. Now the cat was out of the bag. The fact that Mr. Chesters Thompson with his money bags would not be ready at the next election and that Mr. Chesters Thompson's myrmidons would not be ready to help Mr. Balfour, had induced the for- mer leader of the House of Commons to rely, not upon such unwholesome influences as some people thought were brought to bear at the last election, but upon fair political argument in lieu thereof. To him it was amusing that morning to find from the papers what a clear explanation there was of such extremely unusual proceedings on Mr. Balfour's part. (Laughter.) But to return to the:line he was pursuing before this digression, the new polling stations would be a great convenience to the people of the neighbourhood, and he had to thank the officials of the ;Liberal Association for the very gocd work they had done. They had so laid the case before the magistrates, who had the matter in hand that a large number of new polling stations had been established throughout the county, some being very conveniently situated. They might de- pend upon it that he did not pull a long face when Mr. Yerburgh promised him an opponent at the next election. They knew quite well that he loved a fight jest as well as any man in the division. (Applause.) It did not matter what his politics, his age, or his fighting weight, and as long as he found that he was cordially welcomed by his friends wherever he went in the constituency they might be sure he was not going to show the white feather, no matter who came up to them. (A Voice: "What is going to be done with the House of Lords ?") He had been warned that he ought not to talk politics, and so far he had not done se. Front the very kindly expressions he had heard from those who had spoken for them that night he did not think there was any necessity for him to defend anything or excuse anything ' he had done, and he was not going to draw back from anything he had ever said before them. (Applause.) He often heard to his amusement, and sometimes to his astonishment, from fellow members of the House of Commons that they had at one time or other said things that stood in their way when they were asked to go in the division lobby with the Government. He (Mr. Brunner) was usually accounted a hot-headed impulsive sort of a chap; but he did not remember ever having said anything in addressing them upon which he wanted to go back into a political sense. The fact of the matter was that the House of Commons and the Government had not nearly come up to his requirements and wishes; but when they had settled the set of questions which were involved in the Newcastle progromme he would be perfectly ready with a complete new programme for them and for himself. His friend said,</p>
        <p>"WHAT ABOUT THS HOUSE OF LORDS ?"</p>
        <p>He did not know at the moment what the Newcastle programme said about the House of Lords ; but their leader, Mr. John Morley, at one time put before them the alternative" end them or mend them." End them he (the speaker) would Fay. (Applause.) When he thought over that question his mind travelled back to the e:ear 1884, when they were discussing the action of the House of Lords with regard to the Reform Bill of 1884-85, and he would warn the House of Lords that the course they had taken and the spirit they had eh wen during 1893 was the very best method they could possi- bly have devised for arousing the people in opposition to them and their order as the people were aroused in 1831. , His friend Mr. Bratt would remember well how the . meetings of 1834 greeted any member of the House of ILords. He was satisfied that if the country was asked Ito decide between the Liberal Government and their proposals on the one hand, and the existence. of an leffete and absurd institution such as the House of Lords was to-day—(hear, hear)—the verdict of the country would be such as would open the eyes of those gentlemen lin another place, because the place that knew them now would know them no more. (Loud applause.) He ' could say a great deal; but as he had been asked not to talk politics he would reserve what he had to say more about the House of Lords till another day. He would, however like to say a few words about what they were to do with the new powers that were 'going to be be- strewed upon them when the Local Government Bill passed. Before he touched upon it he would ask from them an expression of opinion—not, lie was sorry to say as to the course he was to take, because he was going to pair with a Tory and keep him from doing mischief whilst he did himself good—as to what they thought of the House of Lords and what advice they had to give the members of the House of Commons. This opinion they might give at the annual meeting. PARISI* AND DISTRICT COUNCILS. When the Bill to which he had referred had passed he should like every man in the club to study it, not onl7 as far as regarded such a district as the Northwich Urban district, but to study it so that they would be able to advise the voters in the rural districts as to the sort of men they should elect upon the first Parish and District Councils. They might depend upon it that the sort of men who were first appointed would give tone to the doings of those councils for probably half a genera- tion to come. He wanted them to try and persuade every voter they met to place on those councils men imbued with a sense of duty towards the public, and not simply towards property. The great fight during the discussion of the Bill in the House of Commons had been between duty to the public and the absurd and exaggerated respect for property. They Liberals were convinced that men were more than money; that the public were more than property, and he asked them as a duty to themselves aud their neighbours and as a kindness and tha,nk-offering,to study the Bill and induce their neighbours to put the right ma.n at the first upon the Parish and District Councils. This came home to them in Northwich, and they should take care to have upon their District Council, under the new rule one m mn one vote, men who had in their hearta the grand idea that the people of Northwich were first to be thought ot, • and when their welfare was secured, then they might begin to consider property. There was a good deal of property in Northwich whieh wanted amending, and they weill,! mint be doing their duty as citizens if they did eot eleet men of the calibre he had named. (Aepleuse.) He. could assure them that it had been a great pleasure to hear of the prosperity of the club, and he trusted that whenever the time came that the club was to justify it existence, there would he many hands ready for the work and many willing hearts. (Applause.) The efficiency of a inembsr ef Parliament depended not altogether upon himself, his brains or his heelth, as upon the spirit with with he was supperted and upon the loyalty of the eeeple in his district to Liberal principles. (Applause.) He thanked them very heartily, and pro- mised t hem that en the very first oecasion it was possi- ble he would come to .Nerthwich again, and in public meeting meet them face to face, and there render an account of his stewardship. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTif OF WARRINGTON. •</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTif OF WARRINGTON. •</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 6th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registe,red :—Births :, Warrington, 29 ; Latchford. 3; toi a 1,32. Deaths: Warrington, 25 ; Latchford, 3 ; total2B. The excess of births over deaths was 4. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 26 per 1,000, against 18.7 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 18.8 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zyrnofic diseases was equal to 2.7 per 1,000, against 2.7 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 4.7 the corresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were :—O to 1,7; 1 to 5,5; 5 to 15, 4 ; 15 to 25, 1 ; 25 to 60, 10 ; 60 up, 1; total, 28. The deaths included-3 from scarlet fever; 1 from influenza; 6 from phthisis : 4 from lung disease; 2 from brain disease; 2 from kidney disease; 1 from liver disease; 5 from stomach disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever, 8; membraneous croup, 2. Total number of patients in hospital week ending 10th February, 25. METEOROLOGY.— The mean temperature was 44.4 Fah., and there- fore 3.7 deg. higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 56 F., was recorded on the Bth inst., and the minimum temperature 34 F., on the 7th inst. The rainfall measured 1.21 inch. The general direction was S.W., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 2,200 miles.—JoHti H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TEST NEWS. SUPPOSED ANARCHIST PLOT IN LONDON,</head>
        <p>LA TEST NEWS. SUPPOSED ANARCHIST PLOT IN LONDON,</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE BOMB ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>The London police have discovered an Anarchist con- spiracy which it is believed will prove to be the most desperate and dangerous of any revolutionary plot that has ever had its head-quarters in London. There are many obscure points yet to be cleared up, and much mystery remains to be solved before the complete designs if the miscreants and the actual ramifications of the conspiracy can be stated with absolute certainty, but these facts, among others, are beyond dispute, that the inquiries of the detectives, although cautiously , , made, frightened the plotters, that the gang hurriedly ; scattered, and that its chief met with a horrible death on Thursday evening when endeavouring, in a panic of fear, to carry away to some safe place the deadly explo- sives which were to have been used againstsociety either in this country or in France. Another correspondent rays :—Greenwich and the vicinity were in a state of the wildest excitement on Thursday night in consequence of a tragedy which occurred at five o'clock in the afternoon, in Greenwich Park, the circumstances surrounding which are of the most significant and sensational character, and give colour to the belief that an Anarchiat attack upon the Royal Observatory was not only in contemplation, but was about to be attempted. At the time mentioned, Park-keeper Sullivan was attracted by the noise of a tremendous explosion in the park. Immediately after- wards he and his mate discovered the almost inanimate body of a man, who, upon subsequent examination, was found to be almost disembowelled through the explosion, apparelltly of a bottle or other glass vessel containing some liquid of an explosive character. The man was taken to the Seaman's Hospital, where he died shortly after admission. The Central News representative, upon inquiry at the Seamen's Hospital, found that the dead man was des- cribed in the hospital book as Martial Bourdin, of Great Titchfield-st., W.C. He was admitted at a quarter past five, and was reemived by Dr. Jervis, who found that his injuries were of a most shocking description. The left hand had been completely blown away just above the wrist, and several large pieces sof flesh from the arm were also missing. The nature of the injuries suggest I that the deceased had the bottle in his trousers pockets, and that that in consequence of having stumbled and being about to fall he put his hand upon the bottle to save it, but did so toe late,and the bottle coming in con- tact with the ground, exploded, the full force of the ex- plosion coming in the region of the stomach. The vital wounds were those in the stomach, in which was a ghastly wound, a considerable portion of the bowels protruding. There were als) large pieces of something lodged in the body and in the legs,but the nature of this has net yet been ascertained,as no post-naortem examin- ation has been made. It is believed, however, that the various substances will preve to be fragments of the glass bottle. The pieces of glass which were found amongst the man's clothes and in the vicinity of the ex- plosion were of a very thin description, and the explo- sive material itself, or what was presumably the explo- sive material,and which was plentifully distributed over the clothes, was black and of a sticky nature like prin- ter's ink. Everything possible was done by the medical authorities, but the nature of .the injuries, it was at once _ .</p>
        <p>apparent, precluded the possibility of the patient rally- ing for even a brig period, and at twenty minutes to six he died. PARTICULARS OF BOURDIN'S CAREER. Tie Press Association states that the explosion has caused considerable sensation in London, and the police are making diligent inquiries into the affair, with the object, if possible, of securing the arrest of individuals who are known to be associates of Bourdin. That the latter was a member of the Anarchist body is not to be doubted. He has been known as an associate of the Anarchists for years, and as a matter of fact as one of their most zealous workers. The representative of the Press Association learned yesterday morning, in the course of an interview with one who was intimately acquainted with Bourdin, that the latter, whose correct name is Martial Bourdin, first came • from Par is, where his parents live, to London six years ago. He was then a raw lad of sixteen, and any idea of political intrigue did not appear to enter his mind. Like the majority of French youths he was lively and excitable' but nothing beyond. On reaching London he worked for his brother in Great Tichfield-st., and gradually began to visit clubs in the vicinity, until While- in with the Anarchist ring he became imbued with their opinions, and at last did not withhold from his companions that he was one of their nu mber,and believed in their methods of operation. Three years ago Bourdin went to America,but whether he journeyed thither on an Anarchist mission is not at pre- sent quite plain. While in America he found employ- ment as a ladies' tailor with a Jew named Wingard or Weingart, and was never short of money. The sugges- tion is that he received funds from headquarters.but of this there is at present no actual proof, as Bourdin after entering the inner ring of the movement was extremely reticent as to his friends and actions. On leaving America he re•Lurned to Paris, and thenc proceeded to London where he resumed his employment as ladies' tailor at the establishment of his brother, who, like the parents, had no connection whatever with the Anarchist movement. The brother doss not live at his bus miss premises, but at a private house, and Bourdin himself lodged at the house in Cleveland-st., the number of which if not alrea.dy known to the police, will soon be in their possession. Once in London again Bourdin returned to his old haunts and habits, and if possible seemed more fervent than ever in his belief that he had espoused a good cause. Implicit confidence was placed in him by his associates,and it is not denied that they themselves regret the loss of one of their most zealous organisers. On Thursday afternoon it appears Bourdin did not go to work, and in the course ot the afternoon he was seen in Hanover- square in company with another man making his way in the direction of Charing-cross Station. They seemed in doubt as to what route they should take, and subsequently walked towards Whitehall, where they parted. The second man turned and made his way to- wards the West-end, and Bourdin walking over West- minster Bridge, took the tram for Greenwich,where he a short time afterwards suddenly and unexpectedly met his end. Whether he we there with the idea of taking human life, or whether he intended experimenting with a new kind of bomb in an open part of the park, is not quite clear. That the bomb went off before it was in- tended to do eels certain, and the explanation of the premature explosion is that. the stopping of a small bottle of sulphuric acid inside the weanon became ex- hausted before it was anticipated, and so ignited the ex- plosive contents of the machine. Bourdin, who was a single man, was of remarkably short stature. and from this peculiarity alone attracted more than usual notice in quarters where he was accustomed to frequent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SEVERE GALE IN RUNCORN AND WIDNES DISTRICTS.</head>
        <p>SEVERE GALE IN RUNCORN AND WIDNES DISTRICTS.</p>
        <p>Strong winds prevailed for some days prior to Sunday. and on the evening of that day, they culminated in a gale of very great violence. Pedestrians moved along the thoroughfares with difficulty, and while many had narrow escapes from falling chimney pots and slates, few were injured. Anna Lea, of Wellington-street. Runcorn, was standing at the door of a friend's house at Weston, awaiting is opening, when a slate fell from the roof, inflicting a serious wound. She was taken home in a cab, and Dr. McKenzie put ten stitches in the wound and dressed it. A young woman name unknown, was also struck on the head by a falling slate in Shaw-street, Runcorn. On Sunday afternoon the traffic at the docks was carried on with difficulty, and at night no attempt was made to deal with it and no vessels arrived from Liverpool. On Monday at Widnes, during the night the large north window of St. Paul's was blown in and a quantity of bricks overhauled inside causing considerable damage. The windows of numbers of shops and dwellings were also broken, and a large hoarding in Victoria-street was carried several hundred yrrds by the force of the wind. In both Runcorn and Widnes on Monday morning the streets were strewn with broken slates and chimney pots. In the country districts, trees were uprooted and stacks of hay and straw scattered to the wind. At Sutton near Runcorn, the washhouse of Mr. Joseph Acton' i farm was unroofed,and a corrugated iron build- ing belonging to Mr. John Bate was overthrown. One dwelling-house in the village was rocking to such an extent that the family dare not remain in it and they spent the night in a empty house The most serious damage was done at the farm of Mr. Wm. Moss, of Newton, near Frodsham. A large shippon was rendered a complete wreck about 3 o'clock in the morning. The building was practically levelled with the ground. There were about 20 cattle in the stalls, and they were released by Mr. Moss and his neighbours. Seine of the unfortunate animals had their tails broken, and others . were shorn of their horns, and one was wedged between two " boskins" and its ribs severely crushed. The gale was felt with its full force at the new " town " of Salt- port, on the Ship Canal. Three pontoons lying in the river are missing, and a barge used as a lightship 'nas been smashed to pieces. About a couple of hundreds of logs have gone adrift. The chimney of one of ths offiaes was blown down, and it descended into the kitchen. Fortunately Mr. Monaghan and family, wha are about to remove to Manchester,were away from home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-02-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WOMEN'S LIBERAL FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>THE WOMEN'S LIBERAL FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Committee of the Warrington Women's Liberal Federation was held at the Club, Bewsey-street, yesterday afternoon. Miss Bessie Dutton signified her intention of resigning her office as secretary, and it was decided to invite Miss Cavanagh to take the position. The meeting also agreed to invite Miss Southall and Miss Stewart Brown to speak at the meeting of the Federation to he held on the Bth of March. The meeting was presided over by Mrs. Z. Armitage, and there were also present : Miss Bessie Dutton, Mrs. H. Roberts, Mrs. W. Pierpoint, Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Cavanagh and Mre. liolford.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE RECENT GALE. A considerable amount of damage was done by the ravages of the gale which prevailed on Sunday night. It is said to have been the most hoisteroug tempest that has visited the neighbourhood for more than five years. Its full force was not felt, until about ten o'clock. It came then with a shock that startled people, Those in doors could at times feel the very houses rock and "shake, whilst for pedestrians the experience was more exciting than pleasant. For an hour and a half there was quite downfall of slates, and the wonder is, seeing how profusely the streets were strewn with them the next day, that more accidents than have been heard of, did not occur. Many persons experienged narrow escapes, but the most extraordinary one chronicled occurred in Winwick street. According to a Liverpool paper a lad of some 15 years, who was accom- panied by a younger sister, had the top of his hat takes clean off it is supposed by a piece of slate, blown of some building by the wind. The lad was; passing aluns Winwick-street, and feeling his head cold, took off hat, and Diund the top of the crown clean gone, while a bit of slate was stuck in the brim.—A Loardine, at the side of the Cheshire Lines in IC,ndriek-street, and an- other in Dixon-street were completely blown down, as well as other hoardings in the town. Considerable darn- age was done to the Parish Church. A portion of the orna- mental stone work on the tower has been broken down and the churchyard was scattered over with the pieces. The canopy over one of the Apostles on the west side vraS somewhat defective, but Mr. Woods, the churchwarden, has taken the precaution to have ropes fastened rounl. to secure it. During Sunday night a slate crashed ') through the glass roof of Mr. Charles Sudlow's office, Winwick-street, but fortunately, no one was injured. A part of the roof of a house in White-street, occupied by vt, Mr. T. Pemberton was broken through by a _falling Og, chimney, but fortunately, Mr. Pemberton and his family- Ad Escaped injury. The shutters of the shop of Mr. I3ow- den, tea merchant, Sankey-street, came down about it o'clock, and the breaking of the windows as well as . much damage to the shop was only prevented by the efforts of two or three of the employ64.—The gale has also had the effect of demolishing the signal box on the Ship Canal, near Stockton Heath drawbridge. At Stockton Heath, a large tree on the Chester-road, was blown down, and lay across the road, all traffic being stopped for some considerable time. Several men were engaged for more than an hour in removing it. Noose was injured. At Rixton considerable damage was done to the outbuildings of Mr. James Ton,qe, farmer. MO. roof was almost denuded of slates, and the gable end was literally blown in. One of the horses was injured by the fail of debris, and the animals were only liberated at great personal risk.</p>
