        <p>BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON, MINE FEVER INFECTED ARRAS. BY THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE. ATOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN, that by an Order passed 11 by the Board on the 6th February, 1894, the following area. among others, was declared to be infected with swine Fever for the purposes of the Swine Fever (Infected Areas) Order of 1894, viz.:—An Area comprising the County or Lancaster (except the Hundred of Lonsdale North of the Sands). and also comprising the Boroughs of Accrington, Ashton-under-Lyne, Bacup, Blackburn, Blackpool. Bolton, Bootle, Burnley, Bury, Chorley, Clitheroe, Darwen, Eccles. Haslingden, Heywood. Lancaster, Liverpool, Manchester, Middleton, Mosaics,. Nelson, Oldham, Freston, Rawtenstall, Rochdale, St. Helens, Salford, Southport, Warrington, Widnes, and Wigan.. Under Article 2 of the Order last-named. no Swine can be , moved out of an Infected Area, and no Public Sale of Fat Swine can be held in an Infected Area except under the c,iin- ditions described in that Order. The Order takes effect trom the 18th February, 1894. Dated this 14th day of February, 3.94. 7. LYON WHITTLB, Town Clerk. Town Hall, 'Warrington.</p>
        <p>COUN IT OF CHESTER. CONTAGIOUS DISEASES (ANIMALS) ACTS, 1878 AND 1886. SWINE FEVER INFECTED AREA. NOTICE is Hereby Given, that the Board of Agricul- ture by virtue and in exercise of the powers vested in them under the above Acts, have, by Order dated the 6th day of February instant, ORDERED—That from and after the Eighteenth day of February instant, an Area comprising (inter-alia) the County of Chester, and the Boroughs of Birken- head, Chester Congleton, Crewe, Hyde, Maccles- field, Stalybri4Ye, and Stockport, shall be an Area infected with Swine Fever for the purposes of the Swine Fever (Infected Areas) Order of 1894, and dated the 26th day of January, 1894. And NOTICE is Hereby Also Given, that in conse- quence of the above Area having been declared to be "An Area Infected with Swine Fever," the provisions of the said Swine Fever (Infected Areas) Order, 1894, come into operation and take effect on and from the said 18th day of February, 1894. The attention of Auctioneers, Farmers, Butchers, Dealers, and others interested in the movement of Swine, is drawn to the Notices published threughout the County setting out the provisions of the above- mentioned Swine 'Fever ( Infected Areas) Order, and copies of which Notice, and any other information con- nected with such Order and the movement of Swine generally, can be obtained from the Local Inspectors of Police appointed under the Contagious Diseases (Ani- mals) Acts. REGINALD Porrs, Clerk to the Cheshire County Council. Chester, 13th February, 1894.</p>
